VBI({"OBJ-00600":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the wedding feast","d":"A king's invited guests would not come to his son's wedding but killed his servants, and he destroyed them and filled the hall from the highways; a guest without a wedding garment was cast into outer darkness, for many are called, but few are chosen (Matthew 22:1–14)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Matthew 22:6; NKJV notes, note on Matthew 22:13; Matthew 22:1–14","v":"A king's invited guests would not come to his son's wedding but killed his servants, and he destroyed them and filled the hall from the highways; a guest without a wedding garment was cast into outer darkness, for many are called, but few are chosen"},{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 22:1–14","v":"The kingdom of heaven is like a certain king who arranged a marriage for his son"},{"p":"made by","r":"Matthew 22:1–14","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Matthew 22:1–14","e":"EVT-02861"}],"nm":{"EVT-02861":"The chief priests and elders question Jesus's authority","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00601":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the fig tree","d":"When the fig tree's branch is tender and puts forth leaves, summer is near; so when they see all these things, they are to know that it (or He, by the NKJV note) is near, at the doors (Matthew 24:32–33)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Matthew 24:33; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:29; Matthew 24:32–33; Mark 13:28–29","v":"When the fig tree's branch is tender and puts forth leaves, summer is near; so when they see all these things, they are to know that it (or He, by the NKJV note) is near, at the doors"},{"p":"contents","r":"Luke 21:29–31","v":"As Luke tells it: when the fig tree and all the trees are already budding, they know that summer is near; so when they see these things happening, they are to know that the kingdom of God is near"},{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 24:32–33; Mark 13:28","v":"learn this parable from the fig tree"},{"p":"made by","r":"Matthew 24:32–33; Mark 13:28; Luke 21:29","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Matthew 24:32–33; Mark 13:28–29; Luke 21:29–31","e":"EVT-02869"}],"nm":{"EVT-02869":"Jesus teaches His disciples on the Mount of Olives about the end of the age","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00602":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the wise and foolish virgins","d":"Ten virgins went out with their lamps to meet the bridegroom; the five wise took oil and went in with him to the wedding, but the five foolish had none and were shut out, so they are to watch, not knowing the day or the hour (Matthew 25:1–13)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Matthew 25:1–13","v":"Ten virgins went out with their lamps to meet the bridegroom; the five wise took oil and went in with him to the wedding, but the five foolish had none and were shut out, so they are to watch, not knowing the day or the hour"},{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 25:1–13","v":"the kingdom of heaven shall be likened to ten virgins"},{"p":"made by","r":"Matthew 25:1–13","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Matthew 25:1–13","e":"EVT-02869"}],"nm":{"EVT-02869":"Jesus teaches His disciples on the Mount of Olives about the end of the age","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00603":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the talents","d":"A man going on a journey gave his servants five, two and one talents; those who traded doubled theirs and entered the joy of their lord, but the servant who hid his talent in the ground had it taken from him and was cast into the outer darkness (Matthew 25:14–30)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Matthew 25:14–30","v":"A man going on a journey gave his servants five, two and one talents; those who traded doubled theirs and entered the joy of their lord, but the servant who hid his talent in the ground had it taken from him and was cast into the outer darkness"},{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 25:14–30","v":"the kingdom of heaven is like a man traveling to a far country"},{"p":"made by","r":"Matthew 25:14–30","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Matthew 25:14–30","e":"EVT-02869"}],"nm":{"EVT-02869":"Jesus teaches His disciples on the Mount of Olives about the end of the age","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00604":{"k":"object","n":"The alabaster flask of fragrant oil","d":"The alabaster flask of very costly fragrant oil that a woman poured on Jesus's head at Bethany (Matthew 26:7–9)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Matthew 26:7–9; Mark 14:3–5","v":"Very costly fragrant oil, which the disciples said might have been sold for much and given to the poor"},{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 26:7","v":"an alabaster flask of very costly fragrant oil"},{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:3; Mark 14:3","v":"an alabaster flask of very costly oil of spikenard"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 14:3–5","v":"She broke the flask and poured the oil on His head; it might have been sold for more than three hundred denarii","at":"EVT-02873"},{"p":"owner","r":"Matthew 26:7; Mark 14:3","e":"PER-03060"}],"nm":{"EVT-02873":"A woman anoints Jesus at Bethany","PER-03060":"The woman who anointed Jesus at Bethany"}},"OBJ-00605":{"k":"object","n":"The thirty pieces of silver paid to Judas","d":"The thirty pieces of silver the chief priests counted out to Judas Iscariot to deliver Jesus to them, which he later threw down in the temple (Matthew 26:15; 27:3–10)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 26:15; 27:3, 9","v":"thirty pieces of silver"},{"p":"purpose","r":"Matthew 26:15","v":"The price for which Judas would deliver Jesus to the chief priests"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00606":{"k":"object","n":"The scarlet robe put on Jesus","d":"The scarlet robe the soldiers put on Jesus to mock Him, and took off again (Matthew 27:28, 31)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 27:28","v":"a scarlet robe"},{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 27:28; Mark 15:17, 20","v":"purple","s":"alternative","w":"Mark says they clothed Him with purple (Mark 15:17, 20), where Matthew says they put a scarlet robe on Him (Matthew 27:28); Matthew's telling, entered first, stands as the preferred."}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00607":{"k":"object","n":"The crown of thorns","d":"The crown the soldiers twisted of thorns and put on Jesus's head to mock Him (Matthew 27:29)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on Matthew 27:29; Matthew 27:29; Mark 15:17","v":"a crown of thorns"},{"p":"material","r":"Matthew 27:29; Mark 15:17","v":"Thorns"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00608":{"k":"object","n":"The accusation over the cross","d":"The accusation written against Jesus and put up over His head on the cross (Matthew 27:37)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Matthew 27:37","v":"That this is Jesus, the King of the Jews"},{"p":"contents","r":"Matthew 27:37; Mark 15:26","v":"The King of the Jews","s":"alternative","w":"Mark gives the inscription as THE KING OF THE JEWS (Mark 15:26), where Matthew gives it as THIS IS JESUS THE KING OF THE JEWS (Matthew 27:37); Matthew's telling, entered first, stands as the preferred."},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:38; Matthew 27:37; Luke 23:38","v":"This is the King of the Jews","s":"alternative","w":"Luke gives the inscription as This is the King of the Jews (Luke 23:38), where Matthew gives This is Jesus, the King of the Jews (Matthew 27:37); the telling entered first stands as the preferred."},{"p":"contents","r":"Matthew 27:37; John 19:19","v":"Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews","s":"alternative","w":"John gives the title as Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews (John 19:19), where Matthew gives This is Jesus the King of the Jews (Matthew 27:37); the telling entered first stands as the preferred."},{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 27:37","v":"the accusation written against Him"},{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:26; Mark 15:26","v":"the inscription of His accusation"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 23:38","v":"an inscription"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 19:19–20","v":"a title"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:38; Luke 23:38; John 19:20","v":"It was written over Him in letters of Greek, Latin and Hebrew (NU omits written and in letters of Greek, Latin, and Hebrew, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 19:19–20","v":"As John tells it: Pilate wrote it and put it on the cross, and many of the Jews read it, for the place where Jesus was crucified was near the city"},{"p":"made by","r":"John 19:19–22","e":"PER-03065"}],"nm":{"PER-03065":"Pilate"}},"OBJ-00609":{"k":"object","n":"The stone at the door of the tomb","d":"The large stone Joseph rolled against the door of the tomb, which was sealed and guarded (Matthew 27:60, 66; 28:2)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 27:60","v":"a large stone"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Matthew 28:2; Matthew 28:2","v":"The angel of the Lord rolled it back from the door (NU omits from the door, by the NKJV note) and sat on it","at":"EVT-02898"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 16:3–4","v":"The women asked who would roll it away from the door, and looking up they saw it rolled away, for it was very large","at":"EVT-02898"},{"p":"feature","r":"Luke 24:2","v":"As Luke tells it, the women found it rolled away from the tomb","at":"EVT-02898"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 20:1","v":"As John tells it, Mary Magdalene saw that it had been taken away from the tomb","at":"EVT-02898"},{"p":"placed at","r":"Matthew 27:60; Mark 15:46","e":"PLC-01482"}],"nm":{"EVT-02898":"The women find the tomb empty and the angel tells them Jesus is risen","PLC-01482":"The tomb of Joseph of Arimathea"}},"OBJ-00610":{"k":"object","n":"The Gospel according to Mark","d":"The beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God: the preaching of John the Baptist, the works and teaching of Jesus in Galilee and on the way to Jerusalem, His death and His rising again (Mark 1–16)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:2; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:4; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:10; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:12; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:14; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:15; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:25; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:27; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:35; Isaiah 40:3; Malachi 3:1; Mark 1:1–45","v":"The gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God, begins as written in the Prophets (NU Isaiah the prophet, by the NKJV note), with the messenger sent before Him and a voice in the wilderness (Malachi 3:1; Isaiah 40:3): John came baptizing in the wilderness, preaching a baptism of repentance for sins to be forgiven (or because of forgiveness, by the NKJV note); all Judea and Jerusalem were baptized by him, and he told of One mightier, who would baptize with the Holy Spirit. Jesus came from Nazareth of Galilee and was baptized by John in the Jordan; coming up from (NU out of, by the NKJV note) the water He saw the heavens parting (torn open, by the NKJV note) and the Spirit descending on Him like a dove, and a voice from heaven called Him My beloved Son. The Spirit drove (sent, by the NKJV note) Him into the wilderness forty days, tempted by Satan, with the wild beasts, and angels ministered to Him. After John was put in prison Jesus came to Galilee preaching the gospel of the kingdom (NU omits of the kingdom, by the NKJV note) of God: the time was fulfilled and the kingdom at hand (has drawn near, by the NKJV note). He called Simon and Andrew, and James and John the sons of Zebedee, to follow Him. In the synagogue at Capernaum on the Sabbath He taught with authority and silenced (lit. muzzled, by the NKJV note) an unclean spirit and cast it out, and all were amazed at His new doctrine (teaching, by the NKJV note) and His authority over unclean spirits (the NU reads it otherwise, by the NKJV note); He raised up Simon's wife's mother from her fever, and at evening healed many sick and cast out many demons; in the morning He prayed in a solitary (deserted, by the NKJV note) place, then preached in the synagogues throughout all Galilee. He cleansed a leper, who spread the matter so that Jesus stayed outside in deserted places"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 2:2; NKJV notes, note on Mark 2:10; NKJV notes, note on Mark 2:16; NKJV notes, note on Mark 2:17; NKJV notes, note on Mark 2:19; Mark 2:1–28","v":"After some days Jesus was in the house at Capernaum (NU omits Immediately, by the NKJV note), preaching the word to a crowd that filled the doorway, and four men let a paralytic down on his bed through the roof; seeing their faith He forgave his sins, and when scribes reasoned that He blasphemed He showed that the Son of Man has power (authority, by the NKJV note) on earth to forgive sins, and the man rose, took up his bed and went out before them all. By the sea He called Levi the son of Alphaeus from the tax office and dined in his house with many tax collectors and sinners, answering the scribes and Pharisees (NU the scribes of the Pharisees, by the NKJV note) that He came to call sinners (NU omits to repentance, by the NKJV note). Asked why His disciples did not fast, He spoke of the bridegroom's friends (lit. sons of the bridechamber, by the NKJV note), of unshrunk cloth and new wine; when His disciples plucked grain on the Sabbath He answered the Pharisees with what David did in the days of Abiathar the high priest, and said the Sabbath was made for man, and the Son of Man is Lord also of the Sabbath"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 3:2; NKJV notes, note on Mark 3:3; NKJV notes, note on Mark 3:5; NKJV notes, note on Mark 3:14; NKJV notes, note on Mark 3:15; NKJV notes, note on Mark 3:16; NKJV notes, note on Mark 3:32; Mark 3:1–35","v":"In the synagogue, watched by those who would accuse (bring charges against, by the NKJV note) Him, Jesus bade the man with the withered hand step forward (lit. arise into the midst, by the NKJV note), asked whether it was lawful on the Sabbath to save life or to kill, and, grieved at their hardness of heart, restored the hand (NU omits as whole as the other, by the NKJV note); the Pharisees plotted with the Herodians to destroy Him. He withdrew to the sea, and a great multitude came from Galilee, Judea, Jerusalem, Idumea, beyond the Jordan, and Tyre and Sidon; He healed many, and warned the unclean spirits who called Him the Son of God not to make Him known. On the mountain He appointed twelve (NU adds whom He also named apostles, by the NKJV note; NU and He appointed the twelve, by the NKJV note) to be with Him, to preach and to have power (authority, by the NKJV note) to heal sicknesses (NU omits to heal sicknesses and, by the NKJV note) and to cast out demons: Simon, whom He named Peter, James and John, whom He named Boanerges, Sons of Thunder, Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, James the son of Alphaeus, Thaddaeus, Simon the Cananite and Judas Iscariot. His own people said He was out of His mind, and scribes from Jerusalem said He cast out demons by Beelzebub; He answered that Satan cannot cast out Satan, that a strong man must first be bound, and that blasphemy against the Holy Spirit is never forgiven. When His mother and brothers (NU, M add and Your sisters, by the NKJV note) sent for Him, He said that whoever does the will of God is His brother and sister and mother"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:4; NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:7; NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:11; NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:20; NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:26; NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:39; NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:40; Isaiah 6:9–10; Mark 4:1–41","v":"By the sea, teaching from a boat, Jesus told the multitude the parable of the sower, whose seed fell by the wayside, where the birds (NU, M omit of the air, by the NKJV note) devoured it, on stony ground, among thorns, where it yielded no crop (lit. fruit, by the NKJV note), and on good ground. Alone with the twelve and those around Him He said the mystery (secret or hidden truths, by the NKJV note) of the kingdom of God was given to them, while to those outside all came in parables, as Isaiah wrote (Isaiah 6:9–10), and explained the sower: the word sown, taken away by Satan, received without root, choked by cares and riches, or accepted (received, by the NKJV note) and bearing fruit. A lamp is set on a lampstand, and with the measure one uses it is measured back. The kingdom of God is like seed a man scatters (sows, by the NKJV note), which grows he knows not how until the harvest, and like a mustard seed, the smallest of seeds, which grows greater than all herbs. That evening, as they crossed the sea, a great windstorm arose while He slept on a pillow in the stern; He rebuked the wind and said to the sea, Peace, be still (lit. Be quiet, by the NKJV note), and asked how they had no faith (NU Have you still no faith?, by the NKJV note); they feared exceedingly, asking who He was, that the wind and the sea obeyed Him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 5:1; NKJV notes, note on Mark 5:3; NKJV notes, note on Mark 5:7; NKJV notes, note on Mark 5:13; NKJV notes, note on Mark 5:20; NKJV notes, note on Mark 5:29; NKJV notes, note on Mark 5:38; Mark 5:1–43","v":"In the country of the Gadarenes (NU Gerasenes, by the NKJV note) a man with an unclean spirit, living among the tombs, whom no chains could bind (NU adds anymore, by the NKJV note), ran and worshiped Jesus and cried out, adjuring (by the NKJV note) the Son of the Most High God not to torment him; his name was Legion, for they were many, and the unclean spirits, given leave (NU He gave, by the NKJV note), entered about two thousand swine, which rushed into the sea and drowned. The people found the man clothed and in his right mind and begged Jesus to leave; the man, sent home to tell what the Lord had done for him, proclaimed it in Decapolis (lit. Ten Cities, by the NKJV note). Back across the sea Jairus, a ruler of the synagogue, begged Jesus to heal his little daughter, who lay at the point of death; on the way a woman with a flow of blood twelve years, who had suffered much from many physicians, touched His garment and was healed of her affliction (suffering, by the NKJV note), and Jesus, knowing that power had gone out of Him, told her that her faith had made her well. Told that the girl was dead, He bade Jairus only believe; with Peter, James and John and the child's parents He went in past the tumult (an uproar, by the NKJV note) of mourners, took her by the hand and said, Talitha, cumi, Little girl, arise, and the girl of twelve rose and walked; He charged them that no one should know it"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:11; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:15; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:19; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:20; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:33; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:36; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:44; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:45; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:50; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:54; Mark 6:1–56","v":"In His own country, on the Sabbath, Jesus taught in the synagogue, and those who heard Him asked whether this was not the carpenter, the Son of Mary, the brother of James, Joses, Judas and Simon, with His sisters among them, and were offended; a prophet has honor except in his own country, and He could do no mighty work there but heal a few sick, and marveled at their unbelief. Going about the villages teaching, He sent the twelve out two by two with power over unclean spirits, taking only a staff, wearing sandals, and shaking off the dust where they were not received (NU whatever place, by the NKJV note; NU omits the rest of v. 11, by the NKJV note); they preached repentance, cast out demons and anointed the sick with oil. King Herod said John, whom he had beheaded, was raised, while others said Jesus was Elijah or the Prophet (NU, M a prophet, by the NKJV note): Herod had imprisoned John for the sake of Herodias, his brother Philip's wife, whom he had married; Herodias held it against (held a grudge, by the NKJV note) John, but Herod feared him as a just and holy man and heard him gladly (NU was very perplexed, yet, by the NKJV note), until at his birthday feast her daughter danced and, prompted by her mother, asked for John's head on a platter, and the king, sorry but bound by his oaths, had him beheaded in prison; John's disciples laid his corpse in a tomb. The apostles returned and told Jesus all they had done; in a deserted place, where the multitudes (NU, M they, by the NKJV note) ran before them, He had compassion on them as sheep without a shepherd, taught them, and fed about (NU, M omit about, by the NKJV note) five thousand men with five loaves and two fish, sitting in ranks on the green grass (the disciples' bread, NU something to eat, by the NKJV note), and twelve baskets of fragments were taken up. He made (invited, strongly urged, by the NKJV note) the disciples go before Him to Bethsaida, prayed on the mountain, and in the fourth watch came to them walking on the sea, bidding them be of good cheer (take courage, by the NKJV note); the wind ceased, and they were amazed, for their heart was hardened. At Gennesaret the people (lit. they, by the NKJV note) brought the sick to Him from the whole region, and as many as touched the hem of His garment were made well"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:2; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:3; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:8; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:9; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:16; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:19; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:24; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:26; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:35; Exodus 20:12; 21:17; Isaiah 29:13; Mark 7:1–37","v":"The Pharisees and scribes from Jerusalem, who with all the Jews wash their hands in a special way (lit. with the fist, by the NKJV note) by the tradition of the elders, found fault (NU omits when and they found fault, by the NKJV note) that His disciples ate with unwashed hands; Jesus answered with Isaiah's word of lip worship (Isaiah 29:13) and showed how they set aside (by the NKJV note) the commandment to honor father and mother (Exodus 20:12; 21:17) by calling a parent's due Corban, a gift to God (NU omits the rest of v. 8, by the NKJV note). He told the multitude that nothing entering a man defiles him, but what comes out of him (NU omits v. 16, by the NKJV note), and in the house explained that food enters the stomach, not the heart, thus purifying all foods (NU sets this off as Mark's comment that Jesus declared all foods clean, by the NKJV note), while evil thoughts and every wickedness come from within. In the region of Tyre and Sidon (NU omits and Sidon, by the NKJV note) a Greek (Gentile, by the NKJV note) woman, a Syro-Phoenician (a Syrian of Phoenicia, by the NKJV note) by birth, begged (by the NKJV note) Him to cast a demon out of her daughter, and answered that even the little dogs eat the children's crumbs; for that saying the demon left her daughter. Coming through Decapolis to the Sea of Galilee, He took aside a deaf man with an impediment in his speech, touched his ears and tongue and said, Ephphatha, Be opened, and his tongue was loosed (lit. bond, by the NKJV note); charged to tell no one, they proclaimed it the more"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 8:14; NKJV notes, note on Mark 8:15; NKJV notes, note on Mark 8:17; NKJV notes, note on Mark 8:26; NKJV notes, note on Mark 8:33; Mark 8:1–38","v":"When a great multitude had been with Him three days with nothing to eat, Jesus had compassion on them and fed about four thousand with seven loaves and a few small fish, and seven large baskets were left; by boat He came to the region of Dalmanutha. The Pharisees sought a sign from heaven, testing Him, and He sighed and said no sign would be given to this generation. In the boat, when the disciples (NU, M they, by the NKJV note) had only one loaf, He warned them of the leaven (yeast, by the NKJV note) of the Pharisees and of Herod, and asked whether their heart was still (NU omits still, by the NKJV note) hardened, reminding them of the baskets left from the five thousand and the four thousand. At Bethsaida He led a blind man out of the town and laid His hands on his eyes twice, until he saw everyone clearly, and sent him home, not into the town (NU Do not even go into the town, by the NKJV note). On the way to the towns of Caesarea Philippi Peter answered that He was the Christ, and Jesus charged them to tell no one; He began to teach openly that the Son of Man must suffer, be rejected by the elders, chief priests and scribes, be killed and rise after three days, and rebuked Peter, who was not mindful (setting his mind, by the NKJV note) of the things of God. Whoever would come after Him must deny himself, take up his cross and follow Him, for whoever loses his life for His sake and the gospel's will save it, and the Son of Man will be ashamed of those ashamed of Him in this adulterous and sinful generation"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:1; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:19; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:23; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:29; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:33; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:40; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:42; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:43; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:44; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:45; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:49; Isaiah 66:24; Mark 9:1–50","v":"Some standing there would see the kingdom of God present (having come, by the NKJV note) with power. After six days, on a high mountain, Jesus was transfigured before Peter, James and John, His clothes whiter than any launderer could make them; Elijah and Moses talked with Him, Peter in his fear spoke of three tabernacles, and a voice from the cloud said, This is My beloved Son. Hear Him. Coming down He charged them to tell no one until the Son of Man had risen, and said Elijah had come. A father whose son had a mute spirit, which the disciples could not cast out, was told that all things are possible to him who believes (NU If You can!, by the NKJV note) and cried, Lord, I believe; help my unbelief; Jesus, who called them an unbelieving (by the NKJV note) generation, cast out the deaf and dumb spirit, saying this kind comes out only by prayer and fasting (NU omits and fasting, by the NKJV note). Passing through Galilee He foretold His death and rising the third day, and they did not understand. At Capernaum, when they had disputed (discussed, by the NKJV note) who was greatest, He said the first must be last and servant of all and took a child in His arms; He forbade them to hinder one who cast out demons in His name, for whoever is not against us (M you, by the NKJV note) is on our side, and promised reward for a cup of water. Whoever causes a little one to stumble (to fall into sin, by the NKJV note) were better drowned; better to enter life maimed (crippled, by the NKJV note) than go to hell (Gr. Gehenna, by the NKJV note), where their worm does not die (Isaiah 66:24; NU omits vv. 44, 46 and the rest of v. 45, by the NKJV note); everyone will be seasoned with fire (NU omits the rest of v. 49, by the NKJV note), and they were to have salt in themselves and peace with one another"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 10:5; NKJV notes, note on Mark 10:24; NKJV notes, note on Mark 10:29; NKJV notes, note on Mark 10:34; NKJV notes, note on Mark 10:51; NKJV notes, note on Mark 10:52; Genesis 1:27; 2:24; Mark 10:1–52","v":"In Judea beyond the Jordan the Pharisees asked about divorce; Jesus answered that Moses wrote that precept (command, by the NKJV note) for the hardness of their heart, but God made them male and female and joined the two as one flesh (Genesis 1:27; 2:24), and that whoever divorces and marries another commits adultery. He was greatly displeased when the disciples rebuked those who brought little children, and took them in His arms and blessed them. A man who ran and knelt to ask how to inherit eternal life, and had kept the commandments from his youth, He loved and bade sell all and follow Him, and he went away sorrowful, for he had great possessions; it is hard for those who trust in riches (NU omits for those who trust in riches, by the NKJV note) to enter the kingdom of God, but with God all things are possible, and those who have left all (NU omits or wife, by the NKJV note; lit. fields, by the NKJV note) will receive a hundredfold now, with persecutions, and eternal life. Going up to Jerusalem, while they followed amazed and afraid, He told the twelve He would be mocked, scourged (flogged with a Roman scourge, by the NKJV note), spat upon and killed, and rise the third day. James and John asked to sit at His right and left in His glory; He said they would drink His cup, and that the Son of Man came to serve and give His life a ransom for many. Leaving Jericho He gave sight to blind Bartimaeus, the son of Timaeus, who cried, Son of David, have mercy on me, and called Him Rabboni (lit. My Great One, by the NKJV note); his faith had made him well (lit. saved, by the NKJV note), and he followed Jesus on the road"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 11:1; NKJV notes, note on Mark 11:4; NKJV notes, note on Mark 11:10; NKJV notes, note on Mark 11:26; Psalms 118:26; Isaiah 56:7; Jeremiah 7:11; Mark 11:1–33","v":"Drawing near Jerusalem at Bethphage (M Bethsphage, by the NKJV note) and Bethany, at the Mount of Olives, Jesus sent two disciples for a colt on which no one had sat, found tied by the door (NU, M a door, by the NKJV note), and rode into Jerusalem as many spread clothes and branches and cried Hosanna, blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord (Psalm 118:26), and blessed the kingdom of our father David (NU omits in the name of the Lord, by the NKJV note). He looked around in the temple and went out to Bethany with the twelve. The next day, hungry, He found only leaves on a fig tree, for it was not the season for figs, and said no one would eat fruit from it again; in the temple He drove out the buyers and sellers, let no one carry wares through it, and taught that His house is a house of prayer for all nations, but they had made it a den of thieves (Isaiah 56:7; Jeremiah 7:11), and the scribes and chief priests sought to destroy Him. The next morning the fig tree was dried up from the roots, and He taught them to have faith in God, to believe when they pray, and to forgive, that their Father may forgive them (NU omits v. 26, by the NKJV note). In the temple the chief priests, scribes and elders asked by what authority He acted; He asked whether John's baptism was from heaven or from men, and when they would not answer, He would not tell them"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:1; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:4; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:14; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:17; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:24; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:28; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:30; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:40; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:42; Exodus 3:6; Leviticus 19:18; Deuteronomy 6:4–5; Psalms 110:1; 118:22–23; Mark 12:1–44","v":"In parables He told of a vineyard leased to vinedressers (tenant farmers, by the NKJV note) who beat, stoned (NU omits, by the NKJV note) and killed the servants sent for its fruit and at last killed the owner's beloved son, so that the owner would destroy them and give the vineyard to others, and of the stone the builders rejected (Psalm 118:22–23); the rulers knew He spoke against them. To the Pharisees and Herodians, who said He courted no man's favor (by the NKJV note; lit. look at the face of men, by the NKJV note), He answered over a denarius, Render (pay, by the NKJV note) to Caesar what is Caesar's and to God what is God's. To the Sadducees He said they were mistaken (or deceived, by the NKJV note), for the risen neither marry nor are given in marriage, and God is the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, of the living (Exodus 3:6). To a scribe who asked the first (foremost, by the NKJV note) commandment He answered, Hear, O Israel, the LORD our God, the LORD is one, and to love God with all the heart, soul, mind and strength (NU omits the rest of v. 30, by the NKJV note), and one's neighbor as oneself (Deuteronomy 6:4–5; Leviticus 19:18), and told him he was not far from the kingdom of God. He asked how the Christ could be David's Son when David called Him Lord (Psalm 110:1), warned against the scribes who devour widows' houses and pray long for a pretense (for appearance' sake, by the NKJV note), and said a poor widow who threw two mites (Gr. lepta, very small copper coins, making a quadrans, a Roman coin, by the NKJV notes) into the treasury had given more than all, for she gave all she had"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:8; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:9; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:11; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:13; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:14; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:22; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:27; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:29; Mark 13:1–37","v":"As Jesus went out of the temple a disciple pointed to its stones and buildings, and He said not one stone would be left upon another. On the Mount of Olives Peter, James, John and Andrew asked when these things would be: He warned them that many would come in His name, with wars, earthquakes and famines (NU omits and troubles, by the NKJV note), the beginnings of sorrows (lit. birth pangs, by the NKJV note); that they would be delivered up and brought (NU, M stand, by the NKJV note) before rulers and kings, the gospel first preached to all the nations, and the Holy Spirit speaking for them (NU omits or premeditate, by the NKJV note), and that he who endures (bears patiently, by the NKJV note) to the end will be saved. At the abomination of desolation (NU omits spoken of by Daniel the prophet, by the NKJV note) those in Judea are to flee, for the tribulation will be unlike any since creation, and the days will be shortened for the elect (chosen ones, by the NKJV note); after it the sun and moon will be darkened, the stars fall, and they will see the Son of Man coming in the clouds with great power and glory, gathering His elect (chosen ones, by the NKJV note) from the four winds. As the fig tree's leaves tell that summer is near, so they will know it (or He, by the NKJV note) is near; this generation will not pass till all takes place, and His words will not pass away. Of that day and hour no one knows, not the angels nor the Son, but only the Father; like the doorkeeper of a man gone to a far country, they are to watch"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:1; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:3; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:5; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:12; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:19; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:22; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:24; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:26; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:27; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:29; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:56; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:65; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:70; Zechariah 13:7; Mark 14:1–72","v":"Two days before the Passover the chief priests and scribes sought to take Jesus by trickery (deception, by the NKJV note). At Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper, a woman broke an alabaster flask of very costly spikenard (perfume of pure nard, by the NKJV note) and poured it on His head; to those who scolded (by the NKJV note) her, saying it might have been sold for more than three hundred denarii, He said she had anointed His body beforehand for burial and would be remembered wherever the gospel is preached. Judas Iscariot went to the chief priests, who promised him money. On the first day of Unleavened Bread, when the Passover lamb was killed (sacrificed, by the NKJV note), two disciples followed a man carrying a pitcher of water to a large upper room, furnished and prepared; at evening, eating with the twelve, Jesus said one who dipped with Him in the dish would betray Him (NU omits the rest of v. 19, by the NKJV note), and He gave them the bread, His body (NU omits eat, by the NKJV note), and the cup, His blood of the new (NU omits new, by the NKJV note) covenant, shed for many. When they had sung a hymn (or hymns, by the NKJV note) He foretold that all would stumble (NU omits because of Me this night, by the NKJV note), as the Shepherd struck (Zechariah 13:7), and that Peter, who said he would not fall away (by the NKJV note), would deny Him three times before the rooster crowed twice. In Gethsemane He prayed, Abba, Father, take this cup away from Me, yet not what I will, and three times found Peter, James and John sleeping. Judas came with a multitude from the chief priests, scribes and elders, said Rabbi and kissed Him; one cut off the ear of the high priest's servant, all forsook Him and fled, and a young man left his linen cloth and fled naked. Before the high priest the false witnesses did not agree (were not consistent, by the NKJV note); asked if He was the Christ, the Son of the Blessed, He said, I am, and that they would see the Son of Man at the right hand of the Power, and all condemned Him; they spat on Him and struck Him (NU received Him with slaps, by the NKJV note). Below in the courtyard Peter denied Him three times, the last with curses, when told his speech showed him a Galilean (accent, by the NKJV note; NU omits the rest of v. 70, by the NKJV note); the rooster crowed a second time, and he wept"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:4; NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:8; NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:16; NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:26; NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:28; NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:32; NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:39; Psalms 22:1; Isaiah 53:12; Mark 15:1–47","v":"In the morning the chief priests, with the elders, scribes and whole council, bound Jesus and delivered Him to Pilate; asked if He was the King of the Jews He said, It is as you say, and to their many accusations (NU of which they accuse You, by the NKJV note) answered nothing. At the feast the crowd, crying aloud (NU going up, by the NKJV note) and stirred up by the chief priests, asked for Barabbas, who was chained with rebels who had murdered in the rebellion, and cried Crucify Him; Pilate, knowing it was envy, gratified them, scourged Jesus and delivered Him up. The soldiers in the Praetorium (the governor's headquarters, by the NKJV note) clothed Him in purple, crowned Him with thorns and hailed Him King of the Jews. Simon a Cyrenian, the father of Alexander and Rufus, was compelled to bear His cross to Golgotha, the Place of a Skull; refusing wine mingled with myrrh, He was crucified at the third hour between two robbers, under the inscription of His accusation (crime, by the NKJV note), THE KING OF THE JEWS, and the Scripture was fulfilled that He was numbered with the transgressors (Isaiah 53:12; NU omits v. 28, by the NKJV note); passers-by and the chief priests mocked Him (M believe Him, by the NKJV note). At the ninth hour He cried, Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani, My God, My God, why have You forsaken Me (Psalm 22:1), and breathed His last; the veil of the temple was torn, and the centurion, seeing how He breathed His last (NU He thus breathed His last, by the NKJV note), said He was truly the Son of God. Women looked on from afar, Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James the Less and of Joses, and Salome, who had followed Him in Galilee. On the Preparation Day Joseph of Arimathea, a prominent council member, asked Pilate for the body, wrapped it in fine linen and laid it in a tomb hewn out of the rock"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 16:7; NKJV notes, note on Mark 16:8; NKJV notes, note on Mark 16:9; NKJV notes, note on Mark 16:17; NKJV notes, note on Mark 16:18; Mark 16:1–20","v":"When the Sabbath was past Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Salome brought spices, and early on the first day of the week found the very large stone rolled away; a young man in a long white robe, sitting within the tomb, told them that Jesus of Nazareth, who was crucified, was risen, and that they were to tell His disciples, and Peter, that He was going before (ahead of, by the NKJV note) them into Galilee; they fled trembling (NU, M omit quickly, by the NKJV note) and said nothing to anyone. Risen early on the first day of the week, He appeared first to Mary Magdalene, out of whom He had cast seven demons, then in another form to two of them in the country, and the rest did not believe; then to the eleven at the table, rebuking their unbelief, and sent them into all the world to preach the gospel to every creature: whoever believes and is baptized will be saved, and signs will follow those who believe (have believed, by the NKJV note; NU and in their hands they will, by the NKJV note). The Lord was received up into heaven and sat at the right hand of God, and they preached everywhere, the Lord working with them. Verses 9–20 are bracketed in the NU text as not in the original text, lacking in Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Vaticanus, though nearly all other manuscripts of Mark contain them (by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"designation","r":"Mark 1:1","v":"The beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 1:1–11","v":"It opens with John the Baptist preparing the way, as written in the prophets, and begins the story of Jesus at His baptism"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 1:10, 12, 18, 20–21, 28, 31, 42; 2:8, 12; 3:6; 4:5, 15–17, 29; 5:2, 29–30, 42; 6:25, 27, 45, 50, 54; 7:35; 8:10; 9:15, 20, 24; 10:52; 11:2–3; 14:43, 45; 15:1","v":"It moves quickly from one work of Jesus to the next, again and again with the word immediately"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 1:25, 34, 43–44; 3:12; 5:43; 7:36; 8:26, 30; 9:9","v":"Again and again Jesus charges the unclean spirits, and those He heals, to keep silent about Him"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 3:17; 5:41; 7:11, 34; 14:36; 15:34","v":"It gives some words of Jesus in His own tongue and translates them"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 16:14–20","v":"It ends with the risen Jesus sending the eleven into all the world to preach the gospel to every creature, His receiving up into heaven, and their preaching everywhere, the Lord working with them"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 16:9; Mark 16:9–20","v":"Its last verses, 16:9–20, are bracketed in the NU text as not in the original text; they are lacking in Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Vaticanus, though nearly all other manuscripts of Mark contain them","b":"NKJV note"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00611":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the growing seed","d":"The kingdom of God is as a man who scatters seed on the ground; it sprouts and grows he does not know how, and when the grain ripens he puts in the sickle (Mark 4:26–29)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:26; Mark 4:26–29","v":"The kingdom of God is as if a man should scatter (sow, by the NKJV note) seed on the ground and sleep and rise night and day, while the seed sprouts and grows, he does not know how; the earth bears of itself, first the blade, then the head, then the full grain, and when the grain ripens he puts in the sickle, for the harvest has come"},{"p":"designation","r":"Mark 4:26","v":"The kingdom of God is as if a man should scatter seed"},{"p":"made by","r":"Mark 4:26","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Mark 4:26–29","e":"EVT-02825"}],"nm":{"EVT-02825":"Jesus teaches the multitude in parables by the sea","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00612":{"k":"object","n":"The herd of swine of the Gadarenes","d":"The large herd of swine feeding near the mountains in the country of the Gadarenes, about two thousand, into which the unclean spirits went, and which ran down the steep place into the sea and drowned (Mark 5:11–13; Matthew 8:30–32)","f":[{"p":"count","r":"Mark 5:13","v":"2000"},{"p":"designation","r":"Mark 5:11","v":"a large herd of swine"},{"p":"feature","r":"Matthew 8:32; Mark 5:13; Luke 8:32–33","v":"At Jesus's leave the unclean spirits entered them, and the herd ran violently down the steep place into the sea and drowned","at":"EVT-02802"}],"nm":{"EVT-02802":"Jesus sends the demons out of two men into a herd of swine"}},"OBJ-00613":{"k":"object","n":"The colt on which Jesus rode into Jerusalem","d":"The colt the disciples found tied in the village opposite them, on which no one had sat, and on which Jesus rode into Jerusalem (Mark 11:2–7; Matthew 21:2–7)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 21:2; Mark 11:2; Luke 19:30, 33, 35; John 12:15","v":"a colt"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 12:14","v":"a young donkey"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 11:2; Luke 19:30","v":"No one had ever sat on it"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 11:4; Mark 11:4–6","v":"It was found tied by the door (NU, M a door, by the NKJV note) outside on the street, and those who stood there let the disciples take it when they said the Lord had need of it","at":"EVT-02857"},{"p":"owner","r":"Luke 19:33","e":"GRP-01105"}],"nm":{"EVT-02857":"Jesus rides into Jerusalem on a donkey","GRP-01105":"The owners of the colt"}},"OBJ-00614":{"k":"object","n":"The widow's two mites","d":"The two mites, very small copper coins making a quadrans, which the poor widow threw into the treasury (Mark 12:42)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:42; Mark 12:42","v":"two mites"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:42; Mark 12:42","v":"They were very small copper coins (Gr. lepta, by the NKJV note), and two make a quadrans, a Roman coin (by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"material","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:42; Mark 12:42","v":"Copper","b":"NKJV note"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00615":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the man gone to a far country","d":"A man going to a far country leaves his house, gives his servants authority and each his work, and commands the doorkeeper to watch; so they are to watch, for they do not know when the master is coming (Mark 13:34–37)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Mark 13:34–36","v":"It is like a man going to a far country, who left his house, gave his servants authority and each his work, and commanded the doorkeeper to watch: they are to watch, for they do not know whether the master of the house is coming in the evening, at midnight, at the crowing of the rooster or in the morning, lest coming suddenly he find them sleeping"},{"p":"designation","r":"Mark 13:34","v":"a man going to a far country"},{"p":"made by","r":"Mark 13:34","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Mark 13:34–37","e":"EVT-02869"}],"nm":{"EVT-02869":"Jesus teaches His disciples on the Mount of Olives about the end of the age","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00616":{"k":"object","n":"The Gospel according to Luke","d":"An orderly account, written to most excellent Theophilus, of the things delivered by those who were eyewitnesses from the beginning: the births of John the Baptist and of Jesus, the works and teaching of Jesus, His death, His rising and His being carried up into heaven (Luke 1–24)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:3; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:9; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:19; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:28; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:29; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:34; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:45; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:46; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:78; Malachi 4:6; Luke 1:1–80","v":"Writing an orderly account to most excellent Theophilus of the things fulfilled (or most surely believed, by the NKJV note) among them, the writer, having perfect understanding (lit. accurately followed, by the NKJV note) from the first, tells that in the days of Herod, the king of Judea, the priest Zacharias of the division of Abijah and his wife Elizabeth, of the daughters of Aaron, were righteous but childless and old. As Zacharias burned incense in the temple, chosen by lot (by the NKJV note), Gabriel told him Elizabeth would bear a son, John, who would go before the Lord in the spirit and power of Elijah (Malachi 4:6); for not believing these glad tidings (this good news, by the NKJV note) he was mute. Elizabeth conceived, and in the sixth month Gabriel told the virgin Mary at Nazareth, betrothed to Joseph of the house of David, that by the Holy Spirit she would bear a Son, Jesus, the Son of the Highest, who would reign over the house of Jacob forever (NU omits blessed are you among women, by the NKJV note; NU omits when she saw him, by the NKJV note; am a virgin, by the NKJV note). Mary visited Elizabeth in the hill country, the babe leaped in Elizabeth's womb, and Elizabeth blessed her who believed (or believed that there, by the NKJV note); Mary sang that her soul magnifies (declares the greatness of, by the NKJV note) the Lord. John was born; on the eighth day, when they would have named him Zacharias, his father wrote, His name is John, and his mouth was opened; filled with the Holy Spirit, Zacharias blessed the Lord God of Israel for a horn of salvation in David's house and the Dayspring (lit. Dawn; the Messiah, by the NKJV note) that has visited (NU shall visit, by the NKJV note) them, and said his son would be called the prophet of the Highest; the child grew in the deserts till his manifestation to Israel"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:5; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:9; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:14; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:17; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:21; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:33; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:37; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:38; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:40; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:43; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:51; Exodus 13:2; Leviticus 12:8; Luke 2:1–52","v":"In the days of a decree of Caesar Augustus that all the world be registered, while Quirinius governed Syria, Joseph went up from Nazareth to Bethlehem, the city of David, being of David's house, with Mary his betrothed (NU omits wife, by the NKJV note), who was with child; there she brought forth her firstborn Son and laid Him in a manger (feed trough, by the NKJV note), for there was no room in the inn. An angel of the Lord (NU omits behold, by the NKJV note) told shepherds keeping watch by night that a Savior, Christ the Lord, was born in the city of David, and a multitude of the heavenly host praised God, on earth peace, goodwill toward men (NU toward men of goodwill, by the NKJV note); the shepherds found the Babe and made the saying (NU omits widely, by the NKJV note) known, and Mary kept these things in her heart. On the eighth day (NU for His circumcision, by the NKJV note) He was named Jesus. When the days of purification were completed they presented Him to the Lord in the temple with the offering of the law (Exodus 13:2; Leviticus 12:8); Simeon, waiting for the Consolation of Israel, took Him in his arms and blessed God that he had seen His salvation, a light to the Gentiles, and told Mary of the fall and rising of many and the sword through her soul (NU And His father and mother, by the NKJV note); Anna, a prophetess of great age (NU until she was eighty-four, by the NKJV note), gave thanks (NU God, by the NKJV note) and spoke of Him. They returned to Nazareth, and the Child grew strong (NU omits in spirit, by the NKJV note) and wise. At twelve, at the Passover, He stayed behind in Jerusalem, and after three days His parents (NU, by the NKJV note) found Him in the temple among the teachers, about His Father's business; He went down with them to Nazareth, subject (obedient, by the NKJV note) to them, and increased in wisdom and stature, and in favor with God and men"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 3:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 3:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 3:14; NKJV notes, note on Luke 3:19; Isaiah 40:3–5; Luke 3:1–38","v":"In the fifteenth year of the reign of Tiberius Caesar, Pilate governing Judea, Herod tetrarch of Galilee, his brother Philip of Iturea and Trachonitis, Lysanias of Abilene, and Annas and Caiaphas high priests (NU, M in their high priesthood, by the NKJV note), the word of God came to John the son of Zacharias in the wilderness, and he preached a baptism of repentance through all the region of the Jordan, as Isaiah wrote of the voice in the wilderness (Isaiah 40:3–5). He called the multitudes a brood (offspring, by the NKJV note) of vipers, bade those with two tunics share, tax collectors take no more than appointed and soldiers not intimidate (lit. shake down for money, by the NKJV note) anyone, and told of One mightier who would baptize with the Holy Spirit and fire. Herod the tetrarch, rebuked for Herodias, his brother Philip's wife (NU brother's wife, by the NKJV note), shut John up in prison. When all the people were baptized, Jesus was baptized too, and as He prayed the heaven opened and the Holy Spirit descended on Him in bodily form like a dove, and a voice from heaven called Him My beloved Son. Jesus began His ministry at about thirty, being, as was supposed, the son of Joseph, the son of Heli, and so back through Zerubbabel and Nathan the son of David, through Abraham, Shem and Noah, to Adam, the son of God"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:4; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:5; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:8; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:12; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:18; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:23; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:26; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:28; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:35; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:38; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:41; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:44; Deuteronomy 6:13, 16; 8:3; Isaiah 61:1–2; Luke 4:1–44","v":"Filled with the Holy Spirit, Jesus returned from the Jordan and was led by the Spirit into (NU in, by the NKJV note) the wilderness, tempted (tested, by the NKJV note) forty days by the devil, and answered him from Scripture (NU omits but by every word of God, by the NKJV note; NU reads it otherwise, by the NKJV note; NU omits Get behind Me, Satan, by the NKJV note; test, by the NKJV note; Deuteronomy 8:3; 6:13; 6:16), when shown the kingdoms of the world and set on the pinnacle of the temple; the devil departed until an opportune time (testing, by the NKJV note). In the power of the Spirit He taught in the synagogues of Galilee. At Nazareth, where He was brought up, He read in the synagogue from the book of Isaiah, The Spirit of the Lord is upon Me to preach the gospel to the poor (NU omits to heal the brokenhearted, by the NKJV note; downtrodden, by the NKJV note; Isaiah 61:1–2), and said it was fulfilled that day; asked whether He was not Joseph's son, He said no prophet is accepted in his own country (NU Capharnaum, by the NKJV note), and spoke of Elijah sent only to a widow of Zarephath (Gr. Sarepta, by the NKJV note) and of Naaman the Syrian, so that in wrath (rage, by the NKJV note) they thrust Him out to throw Him down from the brow of the hill, but He passed through them. At Capernaum He cast out the spirit of an unclean demon (lit. Be muzzled, by the NKJV note), rebuked the high fever (afflicted with, by the NKJV note) of Simon's wife's mother, and at sunset laid His hands on the sick and healed them, not letting the demons who called Him the Son of God (NU omits the Christ, by the NKJV note) speak (or say that they knew, by the NKJV note). At day He went to a deserted place, and when the crowd would keep Him He said He must preach the kingdom of God to the other cities, and He preached in the synagogues of Galilee (NU Judea, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:11; NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:12; NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:17; NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:30; NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:33; NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:36; NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:38; NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:39; Luke 5:1–39","v":"Teaching the multitude from Simon's boat by the Lake of Gennesaret, Jesus bade him let down the nets after a fruitless night, and the catch filled both boats; Simon fell at His knees, saying he was a sinful man, and Jesus said that from then on he would catch men, and with James and John he left behind (by the NKJV note) all and followed Him. In a certain city a man full of leprosy begged (by the NKJV note) to be made clean, and Jesus touched him; the report spread, and He often withdrew into the wilderness to pray. Before Pharisees and teachers of the law from Galilee, Judea and Jerusalem, the power of the Lord present to heal (NU with Him to heal, by the NKJV note), men let a paralyzed man down through the tiling, and Jesus forgave his sins and bade him rise and go home. Levi the tax collector left all and gave Him a great feast, and to the Pharisees and their scribes who grumbled (NU But the Pharisees and their scribes, by the NKJV note; grumbled, by the NKJV note) He said He came to call sinners to repentance; asked why His disciples did not fast (NU makes it a statement, by the NKJV note), He spoke of the bridegroom, of a piece from a new garment (NU tears a piece from a new garment, by the NKJV note), of new wine in new wineskins (NU omits and both are preserved, by the NKJV note), and of those who find the old wine better (NU omits immediately, by the NKJV note; NU good, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:9; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:10; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:21; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:26; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:35; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:45; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:48; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:49; 1 Samuel 21:6; Luke 6:1–49","v":"On a Sabbath (NU on a Sabbath, by the NKJV note) His disciples rubbed grain in their hands, and He answered the Pharisees with what David did (1 Samuel 21:6), for the Son of Man is Lord also of the Sabbath; on another Sabbath He healed a man whose right hand was withered (NU, M him, by the NKJV note; NU omits as whole as the other, by the NKJV note), asking whether it was lawful to save life or to destroy (M to kill, by the NKJV note), and the scribes and Pharisees were filled with rage. After a night of prayer on the mountain He chose twelve, whom He named apostles, among them Simon called the Zealot and Judas the son of James. On a level place He healed a great multitude from Judea, Jerusalem and the coast of Tyre and Sidon, and taught His disciples: blessed are the poor, the hungry who will be filled (satisfied, by the NKJV note) and those who weep, and woe to the rich, the full and those of whom all (M omits all, by the NKJV note; NU, M omit to you, by the NKJV note) speak well; love your enemies, give, lend hoping (expecting, by the NKJV note) for nothing, and be merciful as the Father; judge not, for with the measure one uses it is measured back; the blind cannot lead the blind; a tree is known by its fruit, and out of the heart's treasure (NU omits treasure of his heart, by the NKJV note) the mouth speaks; and he who hears and does His sayings is like a house founded (NU well built, by the NKJV note) on the rock, while the house without a foundation fell (NU collapsed, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:10; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:16; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:19; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:21; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:23; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:28; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:29; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:30; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:31; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:34; Malachi 3:1; Luke 7:1–50","v":"At Capernaum a centurion, who loved the nation and built the Jews a synagogue, sent elders and friends to ask Jesus to heal his dying servant, not thinking himself worthy to come, and Jesus marveled at his faith; the servant was found well (NU omits who had been sick, by the NKJV note). The next day at the gate of Nain Jesus had compassion on a widow, touched the coffin of her only son and raised him, and fear seized (by the NKJV note) all, who said a great prophet had risen. John sent two disciples to ask whether He was the Coming One (NU the Lord, by the NKJV note; should we expect, by the NKJV note); He cured many of illnesses (by the NKJV note) and bade them tell John what they saw, blessed is he who is not offended (caused to stumble, by the NKJV note), and He told the multitudes that John was the messenger of whom Malachi wrote (Malachi 3:1), none greater (NU none greater than John, by the NKJV note); the tax collectors justified (declared the righteousness of, by the NKJV note) God, but the Pharisees and lawyers (the experts in the law, by the NKJV note) rejected His will, like children in the marketplace (NU, M omit And the Lord said, by the NKJV note) calling John demon-possessed and the Son of Man a glutton and winebibber (an excessive drinker, by the NKJV note). In the house of Simon the Pharisee a sinful woman washed His feet with her tears and anointed them; He told Simon of two debtors forgiven, the one who owed more loving more, and said her many sins were forgiven, for she loved much, and her faith had saved her"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:3; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:8; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:10; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:18; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:23; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:26; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:27; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:36; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:45; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:48; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:49; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:51; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:54; Luke 8:1–56","v":"He went through every city and village proclaiming the good news (by the NKJV note) with the twelve and certain women healed of evil spirits and sicknesses (by the NKJV note), Mary called Magdalene, Joanna the wife of Chuza, Herod's steward, Susanna and many others, who provided for Him (NU, M them, by the NKJV note) from their possessions (by the NKJV note). He told the parable of the sower, whose seed on good ground yielded a hundredfold (lit. fruit, by the NKJV note), and explained that the seed is the word of God, the mysteries (secret or hidden truths, by the NKJV note) given to them; those on the rock fall away in testing (by the NKJV note), those on good ground bear fruit with patience (endurance, by the NKJV note); one lights a lamp to set it on a lampstand, and from him who has not, even what he seems (thinks that he has, by the NKJV note) to have will be taken. His mother and brothers are those who hear and do the word. In the storm on the lake, the boat in danger (by the NKJV note), He rebuked the wind and asked where their faith was. In the country of the Gadarenes (NU Gerasenes, by the NKJV note) He cast the demons named Legion out of a man who long had worn no clothes (NU reads it otherwise, by the NKJV note) and lived in the tombs, and they went into the swine; the man, healed (delivered, by the NKJV note), proclaimed what Jesus had done. A woman with a flow of blood twelve years touched the border of His garment and was healed, and Peter (NU omits and those with him, by the NKJV note; NU omits the rest of v. 45, by the NKJV note) wondered that He asked who touched Him (NU omits be of good cheer, by the NKJV note); Jairus's only daughter, about twelve, died, but Jesus (NU adds anymore, by the NKJV note; NU adds with Him, by the NKJV note; NU, M Peter, John, and James, by the NKJV note; NU omits put them all outside, by the NKJV note) took her by the hand, and her spirit returned"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:17; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:23; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:31; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:33; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:35; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:41; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:50; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:54; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:55; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:56; Luke 9:1–62","v":"Jesus gave the twelve power over all demons and sent them to preach the kingdom of God and heal, taking nothing for the journey. Herod the tetrarch, perplexed that some said John had risen, said he had beheaded John and sought to see Jesus. The apostles returned, and in a deserted place belonging to Bethsaida He fed about five thousand men with five loaves and two fish, all filled (satisfied, by the NKJV note), and twelve baskets left. As He prayed alone Peter confessed Him the Christ of God, and He foretold that the Son of Man would suffer, be rejected and killed and be raised the third day, and bade them take up their cross daily (M omits daily, by the NKJV note). About eight days later, as He prayed on the mountain, His face was altered and His robe glistened, and Moses and Elijah spoke of His decease (death, lit. departure, by the NKJV note) at Jerusalem; Peter spoke of three tabernacles (tents, by the NKJV note), and a voice from the cloud said, This is My beloved Son (NU My Son, the Chosen One, by the NKJV note), Hear Him. The next day He healed a boy, an only child, whose spirit the disciples could not cast out, calling them a faithless (unbelieving, by the NKJV note) generation that He must bear (put up, by the NKJV note) with, and foretold His betrayal; to their dispute who was greatest He set a child by Him, and forbade them to hinder one who cast out demons in His name (NU you, your, by the NKJV note). When the time came for Him to be received up He set His face to go to Jerusalem; a Samaritan village would not receive Him, and He rebuked James and John, who would call down fire (NU omits just as Elijah did, by the NKJV note; NU omits the rest of v. 55, by the NKJV note; NU omits v. 56, by the NKJV note); on the road He told three that the Son of Man has nowhere to lay His head, that the dead bury their own dead, and that no one who looks back from the plow is fit for the kingdom of God"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:11; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:12; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:20; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:22; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:25; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:30; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:35; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:39; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:41; Luke 10:1–42","v":"After these things the Lord appointed seventy (NU seventy-two, by the NKJV note) others and sent them two by two before Him, as lambs among wolves, carrying nothing, to heal and say the kingdom of God had come near, and to wipe off the dust (NU our feet, by the NKJV note) where they were not received, for it would be more tolerable (NU, M omit But, by the NKJV note) for Sodom; woe to Chorazin and Bethsaida, and Capernaum will be brought down to Hades (NU reads it as a question, by the NKJV note). The seventy returned with joy that the demons were subject to them, and He said He saw Satan fall like lightning, but they were to rejoice (NU, M omit rather, by the NKJV note) that their names were written in heaven. He rejoiced in the Spirit and thanked the Father, Lord of heaven and earth (M And turning to the disciples He said, by the NKJV note), and blessed the eyes that saw what they saw. To a lawyer (expert in the law, by the NKJV note) who asked how to inherit eternal life He told of a man who fell among thieves (robbers, by the NKJV note) on the road from Jerusalem to Jericho, passed by a priest and a Levite, and cared for by a Samaritan (NU omits when he departed, by the NKJV note), and bade him go and do likewise. In a village Martha welcomed Him, and her sister Mary sat at His (NU the Lord's, by the NKJV note) feet; Jesus (NU the Lord, by the NKJV note) told Martha, troubled with much serving, that Mary had chosen the good part"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:4; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:11; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:22; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:29; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:34; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:39; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:41; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:43; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:44; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:53; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:54; Genesis 4:8; 2 Chronicles 24:20–22; Luke 11:1–54","v":"Asked to teach them to pray, as John taught his disciples, He gave them a prayer to the Father (NU omits Our and in heaven and the rest of v. 2 and But deliver us from the evil one, by the NKJV notes), told of a friend at midnight who gives for persistence, and bade them ask, seek and knock, for the Father gives the Holy Spirit to those who ask (NU reads v. 11 otherwise, by the NKJV note). When He cast out a mute demon some said He did it by Beelzebub (NU, M Beelzebul, by the NKJV note) and others sought a sign: a kingdom divided falls, He casts out demons by the finger of God, a stronger one overcomes the strong man and divides his spoils (plunder, by the NKJV note), and an unclean spirit returns with seven worse; blessed are those who hear the word of God and keep it; no sign will be given but the sign of Jonah (NU omits the prophet, by the NKJV note), for the queen of the South and the men of Nineveh will condemn this generation, and the lamp of the body is the eye, good (clear, or healthy, by the NKJV note) or bad (evil, or unhealthy, by the NKJV note). At a Pharisee's table He said the Pharisees cleanse the outside of the cup while within is greed (lit. eager grasping or robbery, by the NKJV note), and should give alms of what they have (or what is inside, by the NKJV note); He pronounced woes on the Pharisees, who love the best seats (or places of honor, by the NKJV note), and hidden graves (NU omits scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, by the NKJV note), and on the lawyers, who load burdens, build the prophets' tombs and take away the key of knowledge, so that the blood of the prophets from Abel to Zechariah would be required of this generation; the scribes and Pharisees began to assail Him (NU And when He left there, by the NKJV note; NU omits and seeking and that they might accuse Him, by the NKJV notes)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:6; NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:26; NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:27; NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:31; NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:39; NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:42; Luke 12:1–59","v":"In the meantime, before an innumerable multitude, He warned His disciples against the leaven (yeast, by the NKJV note) of the Pharisees, which is hypocrisy, and bade them fear Him who can cast into hell, for five sparrows sold for two copper coins (Gr. assarion, a coin worth about a sixteenth of a denarius, by the NKJV note) are not forgotten before God, and the Holy Spirit would teach them what to say. To one who asked Him to bid his brother divide the inheritance He said to beware of covetousness (NU all covetousness, by the NKJV note) and told of a rich fool whose soul was required of him that night. He bade His disciples not be anxious (worry, by the NKJV note), for God feeds the ravens and clothes the lilies more finely than Solomon was arrayed (clothed, by the NKJV note), but to seek the kingdom of God (NU His kingdom, and these things, by the NKJV note), and to be ready like servants waiting for their master, for the Son of Man comes at an hour not expected, as a thief comes to a house whose master would have watched (NU not have allowed it to be broken into, by the NKJV note); to Peter's question He told of the faithful steward who gives the household its food in due season (at the right time, by the NKJV note). He came to send fire on the earth and division, and He bade the multitudes discern this time and settle with their adversary on the way"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:9; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:11; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:19; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:21; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:31; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:32; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:35; Luke 13:1–35","v":"Told of the Galileans whose blood Pilate had mingled (mixed, by the NKJV note) with their sacrifices, He said that neither they nor the eighteen on whom the tower in Siloam fell were worse sinners than others, but unless they repented all would likewise perish, and told of a fig tree that bore no fruit for three years and was spared one year more, though it used up (wasted, by the NKJV note) the ground (NU reads v. 9 otherwise, by the NKJV note). In a synagogue on the Sabbath He loosed a woman bent over eighteen years by a spirit of infirmity, who could not raise (straighten, by the NKJV note) herself up, and answered the indignant ruler of the synagogue, Hypocrite (NU, M Hypocrites, by the NKJV note); He likened the kingdom of God to a mustard seed that grew into a large (NU omits large, by the NKJV note) tree and to leaven (yeast, by the NKJV note) hidden in three measures (Gr. sata, about 2 pecks in all, by the NKJV note) of meal. Journeying toward Jerusalem He bade them strive to enter through the narrow gate. On that very day (NU In that very hour, by the NKJV note) some Pharisees warned Him that Herod wanted to kill Him; He bade them tell that fox that the third day He would be perfected (Resurrected, by the NKJV note), and lamented over Jerusalem, whose house is left to her desolate (NU, M omit assuredly, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:3; NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:5; NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:11; NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:21; NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:35; Luke 14:1–35","v":"On a Sabbath at the house of one of the rulers of the Pharisees, watched closely, He asked the lawyers and Pharisees whether it was lawful to heal on the Sabbath (NU adds or not, by the NKJV note), healed a man with dropsy, and asked which of them would not pull out a donkey (NU, M son, by the NKJV note) or an ox fallen into a pit. To the guests who chose the best places He told a parable, for whoever exalts himself will be humbled (put down, by the NKJV note); He bade His host invite the poor, the maimed (crippled, by the NKJV note), the lame and the blind, to be repaid at the resurrection of the just; and to one who blessed him who eats bread (M dinner, by the NKJV note) in the kingdom of God He told of a great supper whose guests made excuses, so that the master brought in the poor and the maimed (crippled, by the NKJV note) and compelled others from the highways. To the great multitudes He said that whoever does not hate his own family and life, bear his cross and forsake all cannot be His disciple, and that salt that has lost its flavor is fit neither for the land nor for the dunghill (rubbish heap, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:8; NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:16; NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:22; Luke 15:1–32","v":"When the tax collectors and sinners drew near to hear Him and the Pharisees and scribes complained that He received (welcomed, by the NKJV note) sinners and ate with them, He told of a man who left the ninety-nine to seek one lost sheep, for there is more joy in heaven over one sinner who repents than over ninety-nine just (upright, by the NKJV note) persons; of a woman who swept the house for one lost silver coin (Gr. drachma, a valuable coin often worn in a ten-piece garland by married women, by the NKJV note); and of a younger son who wasted his share in prodigal (wasteful, by the NKJV note) living, longed for the pods (carob pods, by the NKJV note) the swine ate, and came home to a father who ran to meet him and bade his servants (NU Quickly bring, by the NKJV note) bring the best robe, while the angry older son was told that his brother had been dead and lived again"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:6; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:9; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:14; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:17; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:19; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:21; Luke 16:1–31","v":"He told His disciples of a steward accused of wasting (squandering, by the NKJV note) his master's goods, who lowered the debtors' bills, a hundred measures of oil (Gr. batos, the same as the Hebrew bath, 8 or 9 gallons each, by the NKJV note) to fifty and a hundred measures of wheat (Gr. koros, the same as the Hebrew kor, 10 or 12 bushels each, by the NKJV note) to eighty, and was commended for his shrewdness; they were to make friends by unrighteous mammon (lit., in Aramaic, wealth, by the NKJV note), that when they fail (NU it fails, by the NKJV note) they may be received into an everlasting home, and they cannot serve God and mammon. The Pharisees, lovers of money, derided (lit. turned up their nose at, by the NKJV note) Him; He said God knows their hearts, the law and the prophets were until John, and not one tittle (the smallest stroke in a Hebrew letter, by the NKJV note) of the law will fail, and He told of a rich man who fared sumptuously (lived in luxury, by the NKJV note) and a beggar named Lazarus who longed for the crumbs (NU what fell, by the NKJV note) from his table: Lazarus was carried by the angels to Abraham's bosom, the rich man was tormented in Hades, and Abraham said the rich man's brothers had Moses and the prophets"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:3; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:4; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:9; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:21; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:23; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:31; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:34; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:36; Luke 17:1–37","v":"He told the disciples that offenses (stumbling blocks, by the NKJV note) must come, but woe to him who offends (causes to stumble, by the NKJV note) one of these little ones, and that a brother who sins (NU omits against you, by the NKJV note) and repents, even seven times in a day (M omits to you, by the NKJV note), is to be forgiven; to the apostles, who asked Him to increase their faith, He said that faith as a mustard seed could uproot a mulberry tree, and that servants who have done all they were commanded (NU omits the rest of v. 9, M omits him, by the NKJV note) are unprofitable servants who have done their duty. Passing through the midst of Samaria and Galilee on the way to Jerusalem, He cleansed ten lepers, and only one, a Samaritan, returned to give thanks. He told the Pharisees that the kingdom of God does not come with observation, for it is within (in your midst, by the NKJV note) them (NU reverses here and there, by the NKJV note); He told the disciples that the Son of Man would come like the lightning (NU reverses here and there, by the NKJV note) but must first suffer, and that His day would be as the days of Noah and of Lot: the one on the housetop is not to come down for his goods (possessions, by the NKJV note), and of two men (or people, by the NKJV note) in one bed, two women grinding and two men in the field (NU, M omit v. 36, by the NKJV note), one will be taken and the other left"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:3; NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:5; NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:14; NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:28; NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:31; Luke 18:1–43","v":"He told a parable, that men always ought to pray and not lose heart, of an unjust judge who did not regard (respect, by the NKJV note) man but avenged (vindicated, by the NKJV note) a widow who kept asking him to get her justice from (avenge her on, by the NKJV note) her adversary; and to some who trusted in their own righteousness, of a Pharisee and a tax collector who went up to the temple to pray, for whoever exalts himself will be humbled (put down, by the NKJV note). He bade the disciples let the infants come to Him; to a ruler who asked how to inherit eternal life He said to sell all and follow Him, for it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God; and to Peter, who said they had left all (NU our own, by the NKJV note), He promised many times more and eternal life. He told the twelve that all the prophets wrote of the Son of Man would be accomplished (fulfilled, by the NKJV note), His death and His rising the third day, but they understood none of it; and as He came near Jericho He gave sight to a blind man who cried out to Him as the Son of David"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:5; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:6; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:21; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:29; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:45; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:46; Luke 19:1–48","v":"Passing through Jericho, He looked up (NU omits and saw him, by the NKJV note) at Zacchaeus, a rich chief tax collector who had climbed a sycamore tree to see Him, and bade him make haste (hurry, by the NKJV note) and come down; he made haste (hurried, by the NKJV note) and received Him joyfully, while all complained (grumbled, by the NKJV note), and gave half his goods to the poor, for the Son of Man came to seek and to save the lost. Near Jerusalem He told of a nobleman who gave his servants minas (Gr. mna, the same as the Hebrew minah, each worth about three months' salary, by the NKJV note) to trade with, and judged the one who called him an austere (a severe, by the NKJV note) man and hid his mina. Drawing near to Bethphage (M Bethsphage, by the NKJV note) and Bethany at Olivet, He sent two disciples for a colt and rode it, as the multitude of the disciples praised God; seeing the city He wept over it, foretelling its fall. In the temple He drove out those who bought and sold (NU were selling, saying, by the NKJV note), saying His house is (NU shall be, by the NKJV note) a house of prayer, and He taught there daily"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:5; NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:9; NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:10; NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:19; NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:23; NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:25; NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:30; NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:31; Luke 20:1–47","v":"Asked by the chief priests, scribes and elders by what authority He acted, He asked whether John's baptism was from heaven, and they, reasoning why then (NU, M omit then, by the NKJV note) they had not believed him, said they did not know. He told the people of vinedressers (tenant farmers, by the NKJV note) who at vintage-time (lit. the season, by the NKJV note) beat the owner's servants and killed his beloved son, and the chief priests and scribes sought to seize Him but feared (M were afraid, by the NKJV note) the people. To spies who asked whether it was lawful to pay taxes to Caesar He said (NU omits Why do you test Me, by the NKJV note) to render (pay, by the NKJV note) to Caesar what is Caesar's and to God what is God's; to the Sadducees, who asked whose wife a woman married to seven brothers (NU omits the rest of v. 30, NU, M also left no children, by the NKJV notes) would be, that the sons of the resurrection neither marry nor die, and that God is the God of the living. He asked how the Christ is David's Son when David calls Him Lord, and warned His disciples against the scribes"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:4; NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:5; NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:8; NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:14; NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:34; NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:36; Luke 21:1–38","v":"He saw the rich putting their gifts into the treasury and a poor widow putting in two mites (Gr. lepta, very small copper coins, by the NKJV note), and said she had put in more than all, for they gave of their abundance to the offerings for God (NU omits for God, by the NKJV note), but she all her livelihood. When some spoke of the temple, adorned (decorated, by the NKJV note) with beautiful stones, He said not one stone would be left upon another; asked when, He bade them not be deceived nor (NU omits Therefore, by the NKJV note) go after (follow, by the NKJV note) those who come in His name, and foretold wars, earthquakes, famines and signs from heaven, and before them persecution, when they were not to meditate beforehand what to answer (say in defense, by the NKJV note), for He would give them wisdom none could resist (withstand, by the NKJV note); Jerusalem surrounded by armies and trampled by Gentiles until the times of the Gentiles are fulfilled; signs in the sun, moon and stars, and the Son of Man coming in a cloud with power and great glory. He spoke of the fig tree and all the trees, and bade them take heed lest their hearts be weighed down with carousing (dissipation, by the NKJV note), and watch and pray to be counted worthy (NU have strength, by the NKJV note) to escape these things. By day He taught in the temple, and at night He stayed on Olivet"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:14; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:18; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:31; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:37; Luke 22:1–38","v":"As the Feast of Unleavened Bread drew near, the chief priests and scribes sought how to kill Him, and Satan entered Judas Iscariot, who agreed with the chief priests and captains to betray Him for money. On the Day of Unleavened Bread, when the Passover must be killed (sacrificed, by the NKJV note), He sent Peter and John to prepare it in a large furnished upper room. Sitting down with the twelve (NU omits twelve, by the NKJV note) apostles, He said He had desired to eat this Passover with them before He suffered, and gave them the cup, saying He would not drink of the fruit of the vine (NU adds from now on, by the NKJV note) until the kingdom of God comes; He gave them the bread as His body and, after supper, the cup as the new covenant in His blood, and said the hand of His betrayer was with Him on the table. When they disputed which of them was greatest, He said He was among them as One who serves, and bestowed on them a kingdom; He told Simon (NU omits And the Lord said, by the NKJV note) that Satan had asked for him and that he would deny Him three times before the rooster crowed that day; and He bade them now take money bag and sword, for He must be numbered with the transgressors, as was written, accomplished (fulfilled, by the NKJV note) in Him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:43; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:57; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:60; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:61; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:64; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:68; Luke 22:39–71","v":"On the Mount of Olives He bade them pray not to enter into temptation, and, kneeling a stone's throw away, prayed that the cup be taken from Him if it were the Father's will; an angel strengthened Him, and in agony His sweat became like drops of blood (NU brackets vv. 43 and 44 as not in the original text, by the NKJV note). Judas came with a multitude to kiss Him; one of them cut off the right ear of the high priest's servant, and Jesus healed it. At the high priest's house Peter denied Him (NU it, by the NKJV note) three times, and as the rooster (NU, M a rooster, by the NKJV note) crowed the Lord looked at him, and he remembered His word (NU adds today, by the NKJV note) and wept bitterly. The men who held Jesus blindfolded and struck Him (NU And having blindfolded Him, they asked Him, by the NKJV note); at daybreak the council asked whether He was the Christ, and He said they would not answer (NU omits also, and the rest of v. 68, by the NKJV notes), but that the Son of Man would sit at the right hand of the power of God"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:6; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:11; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:17; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:23; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:25; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:34; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:38; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:39; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:42; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:44; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:45; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:51; Luke 23:1–56","v":"Before Pilate they accused Him of perverting the (NU our, by the NKJV note) nation and forbidding taxes to Caesar; Pilate found no fault in Him and, hearing of Galilee (NU omits of Galilee, by the NKJV note), sent Him to Herod, who with his men of war (troops, by the NKJV note) mocked Him and sent Him back. Pilate said neither he nor Herod (NU he sent Him back to us, by the NKJV note) had found Him deserving of death, and would have released Him at the feast (NU omits v. 17, by the NKJV note), but the voices of the people and of the chief priests (NU omits and of the chief priests, by the NKJV note) prevailed, and he released to them (NU, M omit to them, by the NKJV note) Barabbas and delivered Jesus to their will. Simon a Cyrenian bore the cross; Jesus told the women who mourned Him to weep for themselves; at Calvary He was crucified between two criminals, asked the Father to forgive them (NU brackets the first sentence as a later addition, by the NKJV note), and over Him was written (NU omits written and in letters of Greek, Latin, and Hebrew, by the NKJV note) This is the King of the Jews; one criminal blasphemed Him (NU Are You not the Christ, by the NKJV note), but the other asked (NU Jesus, remember me, by the NKJV note) to be remembered and was promised Paradise. From about the sixth hour (NU adds already, by the NKJV note) darkness covered the earth, the sun was darkened (NU obscured, by the NKJV note) and the veil of the temple was torn in two (the middle, by the NKJV note); He committed His spirit to the Father and breathed His last. Joseph of Arimathea, who had not consented to the council's deed and was waiting (NU who was waiting, by the NKJV note) for the kingdom of God, laid Him in a new tomb, and the women prepared spices and rested on the Sabbath"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:4; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:11; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:12; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:17; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:27; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:28; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:35; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:40; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:42; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:46; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:49; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:53; Luke 24:1–53","v":"Very early on the first day of the week the women (NU omits and certain other women with them, by the NKJV note) came with spices and found the tomb empty; as they were greatly (NU omits greatly, by the NKJV note) perplexed, two men in shining garments told them He was risen, as He had said in Galilee, but the apostles took their words for idle tales (nonsense, by the NKJV note); Peter ran to the tomb and saw the linen cloths lying (NU omits lying, by the NKJV note) by themselves. That day two going to Emmaus, seven miles (lit. 60 stadia, by the NKJV note) from Jerusalem, walked sad (NU they stood still, looking sad, by the NKJV note) with Jesus unrecognized; He expounded (explained, by the NKJV note) the Scriptures concerning Himself, made as if (acted as if, by the NKJV note) to go farther, and was known (recognized, by the NKJV note) to them in the breaking of bread; returning, they found the eleven saying He had appeared to Simon. Jesus stood among them, showed them His hands and His feet (some printed New Testaments omit v. 40, though it is found in nearly all Greek manuscripts, by the NKJV note), ate broiled fish and honeycomb (NU omits and some honeycomb, by the NKJV note), and said it was written that the Christ should suffer and rise (NU reads it otherwise, by the NKJV note) and repentance be preached to all nations, and bade them tarry in the city (NU omits of Jerusalem, by the NKJV note) for the Promise of His Father. At Bethany He blessed them and was carried up into heaven, and they were continually in the temple praising (NU omits praising and, by the NKJV note) and blessing God (NU omits Amen, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 1:3","v":"an orderly account"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:3; Luke 1:1–4","v":"It is written as an orderly account to most excellent Theophilus, by one who had perfect understanding (lit. accurately followed, by the NKJV note) of all things from the first, as those who were eyewitnesses and ministers of the word delivered the things fulfilled (or most surely believed, by the NKJV note) among them"},{"p":"feature","r":"Luke 1:5–80; 2:1–52","v":"It tells first of the births of John the Baptist and of Jesus, side by side, with the songs and prophecies spoken at them"},{"p":"feature","r":"Luke 3:23–38","v":"It gives Jesus's genealogy, as was supposed, from Joseph back through Nathan the son of David, Abraham and Adam to God"},{"p":"purpose","r":"Luke 1:4","v":"That Theophilus might know the certainty of the things in which he had been instructed"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00617":{"k":"object","n":"The song of Mary","d":"Mary's song at Elizabeth's greeting, that her soul magnifies the Lord, who has regarded her lowly state, scattered the proud, exalted the lowly and helped Israel, as He spoke to Abraham (Luke 1:46–55)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:46; Luke 1:46–55","v":"Her soul magnifies (declares the greatness of, by the NKJV note) the Lord and her spirit rejoices in God her Savior, who regarded her lowly state, so that all generations will call her blessed; He who is mighty has done great things, His mercy is on those who fear Him, He has scattered the proud, put down the mighty and exalted the lowly, filled the hungry and sent the rich away empty, and helped His servant Israel, as He spoke to Abraham and his seed forever"},{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:46; Luke 1:46","v":"My soul magnifies the Lord"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 1:46","e":"PER-03009"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 1:46–55","e":"EVT-02921"}],"nm":{"EVT-02921":"Mary visits Elizabeth","PER-03009":"Mary"}},"OBJ-00618":{"k":"object","n":"The prophecy of Zacharias","d":"Zacharias's prophecy, when he was filled with the Holy Spirit, blessing the Lord God of Israel for raising up a horn of salvation in the house of David, and telling of his son, the prophet of the Highest (Luke 1:67–79)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:78; Luke 1:68–79","v":"The Lord God of Israel has visited and redeemed His people and raised up a horn of salvation in the house of His servant David, as the prophets spoke, to save them from their enemies and remember His covenant and His oath to Abraham, that they might serve Him without fear; and the child will be called the prophet of the Highest, to go before the Lord and prepare His ways, through God's tender mercy, by which the Dayspring (lit. Dawn; the Messiah, by the NKJV note) from on high has visited (NU shall visit, by the NKJV note) them, to give light to those in darkness and guide their feet into the way of peace"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 1:68","v":"Blessed is the Lord God of Israel"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 1:67","e":"PER-03092"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 1:64, 67","e":"EVT-02923","b":"inferred","c":"probable","w":"When his mouth was opened at the naming he spoke, praising God (Luke 1:64), and, filled with the Holy Spirit, he prophesied (1:67)."}],"nm":{"EVT-02923":"John is circumcised and named","PER-03092":"Zacharias"}},"OBJ-00619":{"k":"object","n":"The manger in which Jesus was laid","d":"The manger in Bethlehem in which Mary laid her firstborn Son, wrapped in swaddling cloths, and in which the shepherds found Him (Luke 2:7; 2:12; 2:16)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:7; Luke 2:7, 12, 16","v":"a manger"},{"p":"feature","r":"Luke 2:7","v":"Mary wrapped Him in swaddling cloths and laid Him in it, because there was no room for them in the inn","at":"EVT-02770"}],"nm":{"EVT-02770":"Birth of Jesus"}},"OBJ-00620":{"k":"object","n":"The song of Simeon","d":"Simeon's blessing of God in the temple, that he could depart in peace, for his eyes had seen God's salvation, a light to the Gentiles and the glory of Israel (Luke 2:29–32)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Luke 2:29–32","v":"The Lord lets His servant depart in peace, according to His word, for his eyes have seen His salvation, prepared before all peoples, a light to bring revelation to the Gentiles and the glory of His people Israel"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 2:29","v":"Lord, now You are letting Your servant depart in peace"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 2:28–29","e":"PER-03096"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 2:28–32","e":"EVT-02928"}],"nm":{"EVT-02928":"Jesus is presented to the Lord in the temple","PER-03096":"Simeon"}},"OBJ-00621":{"k":"object","n":"Simon's boat","d":"The boat of Simon from which Jesus taught the multitudes by the Lake of Gennesaret, and which with its partners' boat was filled with fish (Luke 5:2–7)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 5:3","v":"one of the boats, which was Simon's"},{"p":"feature","r":"Luke 5:3–7","v":"From it Jesus taught the multitudes; at His word the nets let down from it took so great a number of fish that the net was breaking, and it and its partners' boat were filled and began to sink","at":"EVT-02934"},{"p":"owner","r":"Luke 5:3","e":"PER-03015"}],"nm":{"EVT-02934":"Jesus teaches from Simon's boat and the disciples take a great catch of fish","PER-03015":"Simon"}},"OBJ-00622":{"k":"object","n":"The flask of the woman in Simon's house","d":"The alabaster flask of fragrant oil with which the sinful woman anointed Jesus's feet in the Pharisee's house (Luke 7:37–38; 7:46)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 7:37","v":"an alabaster flask of fragrant oil"},{"p":"feature","r":"Luke 7:38, 46","v":"She anointed His feet with its fragrant oil","at":"EVT-02938"},{"p":"owner","r":"Luke 7:37","e":"PER-03144"}],"nm":{"EVT-02938":"A sinful woman anoints Jesus's feet in Simon the Pharisee's house","PER-03144":"The sinful woman who anointed Jesus's feet"}},"OBJ-00623":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the two debtors","d":"A creditor freely forgave two debtors, one of five hundred denarii and one of fifty; the one forgiven more will love him more (Luke 7:41–43)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Luke 7:41–43","v":"A creditor had two debtors, one owing five hundred denarii and the other fifty, and when they could not pay he forgave them both; Simon judged rightly that the one forgiven more would love him more"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 7:41","v":"a certain creditor who had two debtors"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 7:40–41","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 7:40–43","e":"EVT-02938"}],"nm":{"EVT-02938":"A sinful woman anoints Jesus's feet in Simon the Pharisee's house","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00624":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the good Samaritan","d":"A man going down from Jerusalem to Jericho fell among thieves; a priest and a Levite passed by, but a Samaritan had compassion, cared for him and paid for him at an inn (Luke 10:30–37)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:30; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:35; Luke 10:30–37","v":"A man going down from Jerusalem to Jericho fell among thieves (robbers, by the NKJV note), who stripped and wounded him and left him half dead; a priest and a Levite came by and passed on the other side, but a Samaritan had compassion, bound up his wounds with oil and wine, set him on his own animal, brought him to an inn, and on the next day (NU omits when he departed, by the NKJV note) gave the innkeeper two denarii to care for him, promising to repay more; the neighbor was he who showed mercy"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 10:30","v":"A certain man went down from Jerusalem to Jericho"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 10:30","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 10:29–37","e":"EVT-02946"}],"nm":{"EVT-02946":"A lawyer asks Jesus what to do to inherit eternal life","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00625":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the friend at midnight","d":"A man who goes to his friend at midnight to borrow three loaves for a traveler is given what he needs because of his persistence (Luke 11:5–8)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Luke 11:5–8","v":"A man goes to his friend at midnight to borrow three loaves for a friend come on a journey; though the friend in bed with his children will not rise because he is his friend, he rises and gives him all he needs because of his persistence"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 11:5","v":"Which of you shall have a friend, and go to him at midnight"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 11:5","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 11:5–8","e":"EVT-02948"}],"nm":{"EVT-02948":"Jesus teaches His disciples to pray","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00626":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the rich fool","d":"A rich man whose ground yielded plentifully planned greater barns and many years of ease, but God called him a fool, for that night his soul would be required of him (Luke 12:16–21)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Luke 12:16–21","v":"A rich man whose ground yielded plentifully, having no room to store his crops, resolved to pull down his barns and build greater, and to tell his soul to take its ease, eat, drink and be merry for many years; but God called him a fool, for that night his soul would be required of him, and whose then would his goods be; so is he who lays up treasure for himself and is not rich toward God"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 12:16","v":"The ground of a certain rich man yielded plentifully"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 12:16","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 12:15–21","e":"EVT-02952"}],"nm":{"EVT-02952":"One from the crowd asks Jesus to bid his brother divide the inheritance","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00627":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the barren fig tree","d":"A man who for three years found no fruit on the fig tree in his vineyard bade it be cut down, but the keeper asked that it be let alone one more year (Luke 13:6–9)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:9; Luke 13:6–9","v":"A man came seeking fruit on the fig tree planted in his vineyard and found none, and told the keeper of the vineyard that for three years he had found none, so it should be cut down, for why should it use up (waste, by the NKJV note) the ground; the keeper asked him to let it alone this year also while he dug around it and fertilized it, and if it bore fruit, well, but if not, after that he could cut it down (NU reads it otherwise, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 13:6","v":"A certain man had a fig tree planted in his vineyard"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 13:6","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 13:6–9","e":"EVT-02955"}],"nm":{"EVT-02955":"Jesus is told of the Galileans whose blood Pilate mingled with their sacrifices","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00628":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the lowest place","d":"One invited to a wedding feast should not sit in the best place, lest a more honorable guest come and he be moved down with shame, but sit in the lowest place and be bidden go up higher (Luke 14:7–11)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:11; Luke 14:8–11","v":"One invited to a wedding feast should not sit down in the best place, lest one more honorable be invited, and the host ask him to give place, and he begin with shame to take the lowest place; he should sit in the lowest place, so that the host may bid him go up higher and he have glory before those at the table; for whoever exalts himself will be humbled (put down, by the NKJV note), and he who humbles himself will be exalted"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 14:8","v":"When you are invited by anyone to a wedding feast"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 14:7","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 14:7–11","e":"EVT-02962"}],"nm":{"EVT-02962":"Jesus teaches the guests and His host at the ruler's table","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00629":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the great supper","d":"A man who gave a great supper sent his servant to call the invited, who all made excuses, so he brought in the poor, the maimed, the lame and the blind, and compelled others from the highways, and none of those invited would taste his supper (Luke 14:16–24)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:21; Luke 14:16–24","v":"A certain man gave a great supper and invited many, and at supper time sent his servant to say that all was ready; but they all began to make excuses: one had bought a piece of ground and must see it, another five yoke of oxen to test, another had married a wife; the angry master sent the servant into the streets and lanes of the city to bring in the poor, the maimed (crippled, by the NKJV note), the lame and the blind, and, there being still room, into the highways and hedges to compel them to come in, that his house might be filled, for none of those invited would taste his supper"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 14:16","v":"A certain man gave a great supper and invited many"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 14:16","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 14:15–24","e":"EVT-02962"}],"nm":{"EVT-02962":"Jesus teaches the guests and His host at the ruler's table","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00630":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the lost coin","d":"A woman with ten silver coins who loses one lights a lamp, sweeps the house and searches until she finds it, and calls her friends to rejoice; so there is joy before the angels of God over one sinner who repents (Luke 15:8–10)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:8; Luke 15:8–10","v":"A woman having ten silver coins (Gr. drachma, a valuable coin often worn in a ten-piece garland by married women, by the NKJV note) who loses one lights a lamp, sweeps the house and searches carefully until she finds it, and calls her friends and neighbors to rejoice with her; likewise there is joy in the presence of the angels of God over one sinner who repents"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 15:8","v":"what woman, having ten silver coins"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 15:8","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 15:8–10","e":"EVT-02964"}],"nm":{"EVT-02964":"Jesus tells the Pharisees and scribes of the lost sheep, the lost coin and the lost son","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00631":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the lost son","d":"A younger son who took his share and wasted it in a far country came home in want and was welcomed by his father with a feast, while the angry older son was told that his brother had been dead and lived again (Luke 15:11–32)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:16; NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:22; Luke 15:11–32","v":"A man had two sons, and the younger asked for the portion of goods that fell to him, and the father divided his livelihood between them; not many days after, the younger journeyed to a far country and wasted his possessions in prodigal (wasteful, by the NKJV note) living, and when he had spent all and a severe famine arose, he was sent to feed a citizen's swine and would gladly have eaten the pods (carob pods, by the NKJV note) they ate. Coming to himself, he went home to confess that he had sinned against heaven and his father and was no longer worthy to be called his son; while he was still a great way off his father had compassion, ran, fell on his neck and kissed him, and bade the servants (NU Quickly bring, by the NKJV note) bring the best robe, a ring and sandals and kill the fatted calf, for this son had been dead and lived again, had been lost and was found. The older son, coming from the field, was angry and would not go in, saying he had served many years and was never given a young goat, but his father said that all he had was his, and it was right to make merry, for his brother had been dead and lived again"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 15:11","v":"A certain man had two sons"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 15:11","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 15:11–32","e":"EVT-02964"}],"nm":{"EVT-02964":"Jesus tells the Pharisees and scribes of the lost sheep, the lost coin and the lost son","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00632":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the unjust steward","d":"A steward accused of wasting his master's goods lowered the debts owed to his master, so that the debtors would receive him, and the master commended him for his shrewdness (Luke 16:1–8)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:6; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:7; Luke 16:1–8","v":"A rich man's steward, accused of wasting (squandering, by the NKJV note) his goods, was told to give an account, for he could be steward no longer; unable to dig and ashamed to beg, he called his master's debtors and had one who owed a hundred measures of oil (Gr. batos, the same as the Hebrew bath, 8 or 9 gallons each, by the NKJV note) write fifty, and one who owed a hundred measures of wheat (Gr. koros, the same as the Hebrew kor, 10 or 12 bushels each, by the NKJV note) write eighty, that they might receive him into their houses; the master commended the unjust steward because he had dealt shrewdly, for the sons of this world deal more shrewdly with their own than the sons of light"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 16:1","v":"There was a certain rich man who had a steward"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 16:1","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 16:1–8","e":"EVT-02965"}],"nm":{"EVT-02965":"Jesus tells His disciples of the unjust steward","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00633":{"k":"object","n":"The rich man and Lazarus","d":"Jesus's account of a rich man and a beggar named Lazarus laid at his gate: at death Lazarus was carried to Abraham's bosom and the rich man was tormented in Hades, where Abraham told him that his brothers had Moses and the prophets (Luke 16:19–31)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:19; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:21; Luke 16:19–31","v":"A rich man clothed in purple and fine linen fared sumptuously (lived in luxury, by the NKJV note) every day, and at his gate lay a beggar named Lazarus, full of sores, longing to be fed with the crumbs (NU what fell, by the NKJV note) from his table, while the dogs licked his sores. The beggar died and was carried by the angels to Abraham's bosom; the rich man died and was buried, and in torments in Hades saw Abraham afar off and Lazarus in his bosom, and begged that Lazarus might cool his tongue. Abraham answered that he had received his good things in his lifetime and Lazarus evil things, and that a great gulf was fixed between them; asked to send Lazarus to warn the rich man's five brothers, he said they had Moses and the prophets, and if they would not hear them, neither would they be persuaded though one rose from the dead"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 16:20","v":"a certain beggar named Lazarus"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 16:19","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 16:19–31","e":"EVT-02966"}],"nm":{"EVT-02966":"The Pharisees deride Jesus, and He tells of the rich man and Lazarus","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00634":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the persistent widow","d":"An unjust judge who did not fear God nor regard man avenged a widow because her continual coming troubled him; so God will avenge His elect who cry out to Him (Luke 18:1–8)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:3; NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:5; Luke 18:2–5","v":"In a certain city was a judge who did not fear God nor regard (respect, by the NKJV note) man, and a widow of that city kept coming to him, asking him to get her justice from (avenge me on, by the NKJV note) her adversary; for a while he would not, but afterward he said within himself that because she troubled him he would avenge (vindicate, by the NKJV note) her, lest her continual coming weary him"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 18:2","v":"There was in a certain city a judge"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 18:1–2","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 18:1–8","e":"EVT-02972"}],"nm":{"EVT-02972":"Jesus tells the parable of the persistent widow","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00635":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the Pharisee and the tax collector","d":"A Pharisee who thanked God he was not like other men, and a tax collector who would not raise his eyes but asked God's mercy, prayed in the temple, and the tax collector went down justified (Luke 18:9–14)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:14; Luke 18:10–14","v":"Two men went up to the temple to pray, a Pharisee and a tax collector; the Pharisee stood and thanked God that he was not like other men, extortioners, unjust, adulterers, or this tax collector, for he fasted twice a week and gave tithes of all he possessed; the tax collector, standing afar off, would not raise his eyes to heaven but beat his breast and asked God to be merciful to him, a sinner; he went down to his house justified rather than the other, for whoever exalts himself will be humbled (put down, by the NKJV note), and he who humbles himself will be exalted"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 18:10","v":"Two men went up to the temple to pray"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 18:9","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 18:9–14","e":"EVT-02973"}],"nm":{"EVT-02973":"Jesus tells the parable of the Pharisee and the tax collector","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00636":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the minas","d":"A nobleman who went to receive a kingdom gave his servants a mina each to trade with, rewarded those who gained with cities, took the mina from the one who hid it, and slew the citizens who would not have him reign (Luke 19:11–27)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:21; Luke 19:12–27","v":"A nobleman went into a far country to receive a kingdom and return, and gave ten of his servants ten minas (Gr. mna, the same as the Hebrew minah, each worth about three months' salary, by the NKJV note), bidding them do business till he came; but his citizens hated him and sent a delegation to say they would not have him reign over them. Returning with the kingdom, he called the servants: the one whose mina had earned ten minas was given authority over ten cities, and the one whose mina had earned five, over five; but one who had kept his mina in a handkerchief, fearing him as an austere (a severe, by the NKJV note) man, was judged out of his own mouth, and his mina was given to him who had ten, for to everyone who has more will be given, and from him who has not even what he has will be taken; and the enemies who would not have him reign were slain before him"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 19:12","v":"A certain nobleman went into a far country"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 19:11–12","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 19:11–27","e":"EVT-02976"}],"nm":{"EVT-02976":"Jesus tells the parable of the minas","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00637":{"k":"object","n":"The Gospel according to John","d":"The book that begins, In the beginning was the Word: the Word made flesh, John the Baptist's witness to Him, the signs, teaching and works of Jesus, His death, His rising and His appearing to His disciples (John 1–21)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 1:5; NKJV notes, note on John 1:9; NKJV notes, note on John 1:11; NKJV notes, note on John 1:12; NKJV notes, note on John 1:15; NKJV notes, note on John 1:16; NKJV notes, note on John 1:18; NKJV notes, note on John 1:27; NKJV notes, note on John 1:28; NKJV notes, note on John 1:30; NKJV notes, note on John 1:41; NKJV notes, note on John 1:42; NKJV notes, note on John 1:51; Isaiah 40:3; John 1:1–51","v":"In the beginning the Word was with God and was God; all things were made through Him, and in Him was life, the light of men, shining in the darkness, which did not comprehend (or overcome, by the NKJV note) it. John, a man sent from God, came to bear witness of the true Light, giving light to every man coming into the world (or, coming into the world, giving light to every man, by the NKJV note); the world did not know Him, and His own (His own things or domain, and His own people, by the NKJV notes) did not receive Him, but to those who believe in His name He gave the right (authority, by the NKJV note) to become children of God. The Word became flesh and dwelt among us, the only begotten of the Father, full of grace and truth; John cried that He who came after him was preferred before him (ranks higher than I, by the NKJV note), for He was before him; of His fullness (NU For of His fullness, by the NKJV note) we have all received grace for grace, for whereas the law came through Moses, grace and truth came through Jesus Christ, and the only begotten Son (NU God, by the NKJV note), in the bosom of the Father, has declared God, whom no one has seen. To priests and Levites sent by the Jews from Jerusalem, John confessed that he was not the Christ, nor Elijah, nor the Prophet, but the voice crying in the wilderness (Isaiah 40:3), and that One stood among them, coming after him and preferred before him (ranks higher than I, by the NKJV note), whose sandal strap he was not worthy to loose; this was in Bethabara (NU, M Bethany, by the NKJV note) beyond the Jordan, where John was baptizing. The next day he called Jesus the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world, preferred before him (ranks higher than I, by the NKJV note), and bore witness that he saw the Spirit descend from heaven like a dove and remain on Him, and that He is the Son of God. The next day two of John's disciples followed Jesus and remained with Him that day; one of them, Andrew, told his brother Simon that they had found the Messiah (lit. Anointed One, by the NKJV note), and Jesus said that Simon the son of Jonah (NU John, by the NKJV note) would be called Cephas, A Stone (Gr. Petros, usually translated Peter, by the NKJV note). The following day Jesus found Philip of Bethsaida and bade him follow Him, and Philip brought Nathanael, who, when Jesus said He had seen him under the fig tree, called Him the Son of God and the King of Israel, and was told that hereafter (NU omits hereafter, by the NKJV note) he would see heaven open and the angels of God ascending and descending upon the Son of Man"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 2:11; NKJV notes, note on John 2:14; NKJV notes, note on John 2:17; NKJV notes, note on John 2:22; Psalms 69:9; John 2:1–25","v":"On the third day there was a wedding in Cana of Galilee; when the wine ran out the mother of Jesus told Him, and He answered that His hour had not yet come, but at His word the servants filled with water six waterpots of stone, set there for the purification of the Jews, and took to the master of the feast water made wine, the good wine kept until now: this beginning of signs Jesus did in Cana, and manifested (revealed, by the NKJV note) His glory, and His disciples believed in Him. He went down to Capernaum with His mother, brothers and disciples, staying not many days, and at the Passover of the Jews went up to Jerusalem; finding in the temple those who sold oxen, sheep and doves and the money changers doing business (lit. sitting, by the NKJV note), He made a whip of cords and drove them all out, and bade those who sold doves not make His Father's house a house of merchandise, and His disciples remembered that it was written, Zeal for Your house has eaten (NU, M will eat, by the NKJV note) Me up (Psalm 69:9). Asked for a sign, He bade them destroy this temple, saying He would raise it up in three days; He spoke of the temple of His body, and when He had risen His disciples remembered that He had said this to them (NU, M omit to them, by the NKJV note). At the feast many believed in His name, seeing His signs, but He did not commit Himself to them, for He knew what was in man"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 3:3; NKJV notes, note on John 3:13; NKJV notes, note on John 3:15; Numbers 21:8–9; John 3:1–36","v":"Nicodemus, a Pharisee and a ruler of the Jews, came to Jesus by night, owning Him a teacher come from God; Jesus told him that unless one is born again (or from above, by the NKJV note), of water and the Spirit, he cannot see or enter the kingdom of God; that no one has ascended to heaven but He who came down from heaven, the Son of Man who is in heaven (NU omits who is in heaven, by the NKJV note), who must be lifted up as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, that whoever believes in Him should not perish but (NU omits not perish but, by the NKJV note) have eternal life; that God, loving the world, gave His only begotten Son, sent not to condemn the world but that the world through Him might be saved; and that men loved darkness rather than light because their deeds were evil. After these things Jesus remained with His disciples in the land of Judea and baptized, while John, not yet thrown into prison, baptized in Aenon near Salim, where there was much water. When a dispute arose about purification and John's disciples told him that all were coming to Jesus, John said he was not the Christ but sent before Him; the friend of the bridegroom rejoices greatly at the bridegroom's voice, and so his joy was fulfilled: He must increase and John decrease; He who comes from above is above all, the Father has given all things into the Son's hand, and he who believes in the Son has everlasting life, while the wrath of God abides on him who does not"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 4:42; NKJV notes, note on John 4:46; John 4:1–54","v":"Knowing that the Pharisees had heard He made and baptized more disciples than John (though His disciples baptized, not He Himself), the Lord left Judea and departed again to Galilee, needing to go through Samaria. At Jacob's well by Sychar, a city of Samaria near the plot of ground Jacob gave to his son Joseph, He asked a woman of Samaria for a drink and told her of living water springing up into everlasting life; He told her of her five husbands, said the hour was coming when the true worshipers would worship the Father in spirit and truth, and told her that He was the Messiah. She left her waterpot and called the men of the city to come and see. In the meantime He told His disciples that His food was to do the will of Him who sent Him, and that the fields were already white for harvest. Many Samaritans of that city believed, because of her word and then of His own, and He stayed with them two days; they knew that He was indeed the Christ (NU omits the Christ, by the NKJV note), the Savior of the world. In Galilee, though a prophet has no honor in his own country, the Galileans who had been at the feast received Him. At Cana a nobleman (royal official, by the NKJV note) whose son lay at the point of death at Capernaum believed His word that his son lived, and found that the fever had left him at that same hour: the second sign Jesus did when He had come out of Judea into Galilee"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 5:2; NKJV notes, note on John 5:3; NKJV notes, note on John 5:16; NKJV notes, note on John 5:31; John 5:1–47","v":"After this there was a feast of the Jews, and Jesus went up to Jerusalem. By the Sheep Gate was a pool called in Hebrew Bethesda (NU Bethzatha, by the NKJV note), with five porches, in which a great multitude of sick people lay, blind, lame, paralyzed (withered, by the NKJV note), waiting for the moving of the water, for an angel went down at a certain time and stirred it, and whoever stepped in first was made well (NU omits the rest of v. 3 and all of v. 4, by the NKJV note). On the Sabbath Jesus bade a man who had an infirmity thirty-eight years rise, take up his bed and walk, and he was made well, and the Jews told him it was not lawful to carry his bed. Afterward Jesus found him in the temple and bade him sin no more, and he told the Jews it was Jesus who had made him well. For this the Jews persecuted Jesus and sought to kill Him (NU omits and sought to kill Him, by the NKJV note), because He had done these things on the Sabbath, and sought all the more to kill Him because He said God was His Father, making Himself equal with God. Jesus answered that the Son does what He sees the Father do; the Father has committed all judgment to the Son, that all should honor the Son as they honor the Father; he who hears His word and believes in Him who sent Him has everlasting life; the dead will hear the voice of the Son of God and live, and all in the graves will come forth, to the resurrection of life or of condemnation. If He bore witness of Himself His witness was not true (valid as testimony, by the NKJV note), but John, His works, the Father and the Scriptures bear witness of Him; Moses, in whom they trust, accuses them, for he wrote about Him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 6:2; NKJV notes, note on John 6:11; NKJV notes, note on John 6:19; NKJV notes, note on John 6:22; NKJV notes, note on John 6:37; NKJV notes, note on John 6:41; NKJV notes, note on John 6:43; NKJV notes, note on John 6:45; NKJV notes, note on John 6:47; NKJV notes, note on John 6:55; NKJV notes, note on John 6:60; NKJV notes, note on John 6:61; NKJV notes, note on John 6:66; NKJV notes, note on John 6:69; John 6:1–71","v":"After these things Jesus went over the Sea of Galilee, which is the Sea of Tiberias, and a great multitude followed Him for the signs He did on the diseased (sick, by the NKJV note); the Passover, a feast of the Jews, was near. On the mountain He tested Philip, asking where they might buy bread; Philip said two hundred denarii worth would not suffice, and Andrew told of a lad with five barley loaves and two small fish. About five thousand men sat down where there was much grass, and Jesus gave thanks and distributed the loaves to the disciples, and they to those sitting down (NU omits to the disciples, and the disciples, by the NKJV note), and likewise the fish, as much as they wanted; the fragments filled twelve baskets, and the men said He was truly the Prophet who is to come into the world. Perceiving that they would take Him by force to make Him king, He departed to the mountain alone. His disciples went over the sea toward Capernaum in the dark, with a great wind blowing, and had rowed about three or four miles (lit. 25 or 30 stadia, by the NKJV note) when they saw Him walking on the sea; when they received Him, the boat was at once at the land. The next day the people saw that only one boat had been there, that one (NU omits that, by the NKJV note) which His disciples had entered (NU omits which His disciples had entered, by the NKJV note), and that Jesus had not gone in it; other boats came from Tiberias, and they came in boats to Capernaum seeking Him. He told them to labor not for the food which perishes but for that which endures to everlasting life, which the Son of Man would give. He said He is the bread of life come down from heaven, and will by no means (certainly not, by the NKJV note) cast out the one who comes to Him but will raise him up at the last day. The Jews complained (grumbled, by the NKJV note) that He said He came down from heaven, since they knew His father and mother. He bade them not murmur (Stop grumbling, by the NKJV note), and said that all would be taught by God, that everyone who has heard and learned (M hears and has learned, by the NKJV note) from the Father comes to Him, that he who believes in Him (NU omits in Me, by the NKJV note) has everlasting life, and that the bread He would give is His flesh, given for the life of the world. Unless they eat the flesh of the Son of Man and drink His blood they have no life in them, for His flesh is food indeed (NU true food, by the NKJV note) and His blood drink indeed (NU true drink, by the NKJV note). He taught this in the synagogue at Capernaum. Many of His disciples called it a hard (difficult, by the NKJV note) saying and complained (grumbled, by the NKJV note). He asked whether it offended them (make you stumble, by the NKJV note) and said it is the Spirit who gives life, and from that time many went back (or away, lit. to the back, by the NKJV note) and walked with Him no more. When He asked the twelve whether they also would go away, Simon Peter answered that He had the words of eternal life and was the Christ, the Son of the living God (NU Holy One of God, by the NKJV note). Jesus said one of the twelve He had chosen was a devil, speaking of Judas Iscariot the son of Simon, who would betray Him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 7:1; NKJV notes, note on John 7:8; NKJV notes, note on John 7:16; NKJV notes, note on John 7:26; NKJV notes, note on John 7:29; NKJV notes, note on John 7:33; NKJV notes, note on John 7:39; NKJV notes, note on John 7:40; NKJV notes, note on John 7:50; NKJV notes, note on John 7:52; NKJV notes, note on John 7:53; John 7:1–53","v":"After these things Jesus walked in Galilee, not wanting to walk in Judea, because the Jews (the ruling authorities, by the NKJV note) sought to kill Him. With the Jews' Feast of Tabernacles at hand, His brothers, who did not believe in Him, bade Him go into Judea and show Himself to the world; He said His time had not yet come and that He was not yet (NU omits yet, by the NKJV note) going up to the feast, and He remained in Galilee. When they had gone up, He also went up, as it were in secret. While the people murmured, some saying He was good and others that He deceived the people, about the middle of the feast He taught in the temple, and to the Jews who marveled at His learning He answered (NU, M So Jesus, by the NKJV note) that His doctrine was His who sent Him; none of them kept the law Moses gave, and they circumcised on the Sabbath yet were angry that He made a man completely well on the Sabbath, so they should judge with righteous judgment and not by appearance. Some from Jerusalem asked whether the rulers knew He was truly (NU omits truly, by the NKJV note) the Christ; He cried out that He had not come of Himself, but (NU, M omit But, by the NKJV note) knew Him who sent Him; no one laid a hand on Him, for His hour had not yet come, and many believed in Him. The Pharisees and chief priests sent officers to take Him, and He told them (NU, M omit to them, by the NKJV note) He would be with them a little while longer and then go where they could not come. On the last day, that great day of the feast, He cried out that anyone who thirsts should come to Him and drink, speaking of the Spirit whom those believing (NU who believed, by the NKJV note) in Him would receive, for the Holy (NU omits Holy, by the NKJV note) Spirit was not yet given. Many (NU some, by the NKJV note) said He was the Prophet, others the Christ, and others asked whether the Christ would come out of Galilee and not from Bethlehem, and the people were divided. The officers came back saying no man ever spoke like this Man; the Pharisees called the crowd that does not know the law accursed, and when Nicodemus, who came to Jesus (lit. Him, by the NKJV note) by night (NU before, by the NKJV note), asked whether their law judges a man before it hears him, they answered that no prophet has arisen (NU is to rise, by the NKJV note) out of Galilee. And everyone went to his own house (NU brackets 7:53–8:11 as not in the original text, though they are present in over 900 manuscripts of John, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 7:53; NKJV notes, note on John 8:2; NKJV notes, note on John 8:4; NKJV notes, note on John 8:5; NKJV notes, note on John 8:6; NKJV notes, note on John 8:7; NKJV notes, note on John 8:9; NKJV notes, note on John 8:10; NKJV notes, note on John 8:11; NKJV notes, note on John 8:13; NKJV notes, note on John 8:28; NKJV notes, note on John 8:38; NKJV notes, note on John 8:54; NKJV notes, note on John 8:59; John 8:1–59","v":"Jesus went to the Mount of Olives, and early (M very early, by the NKJV note) in the morning He came again into the temple and taught all the people. The scribes and Pharisees set before Him a woman, saying she was caught (M we found this woman, by the NKJV note) in adultery in the very act and that Moses in the law (M in our law Moses, by the NKJV note) commanded that such should be stoned (NU, M to stone such, by the NKJV note), and asked what He said (M adds about her, by the NKJV note), testing Him; He stooped and wrote on the ground with His finger, as though He did not hear (NU, M omit as though He did not hear, by the NKJV note), raised Himself up (M He looked up, by the NKJV note) and bade the one among them without sin throw a stone at her first. Being convicted by their conscience (NU, M omit being convicted by their conscience, by the NKJV note), they went out one by one, beginning with the oldest; seeing no one but the woman (NU omits and saw no one but the woman; M He saw her and said, by the NKJV note), He asked where her accusers (NU, M omit of yours, by the NKJV note) were, did not condemn her, and bade her go and sin no more (NU, M add from now on, by the NKJV note); the passage 7:53–8:11 is bracketed in the NU text as not in the original text, though present in over 900 manuscripts of John (by the NKJV note on 7:53). Jesus said He was the light of the world; when the Pharisees said His witness of Himself was not true (valid as testimony, by the NKJV note), He said the Father who sent Him bore witness of Him. He spoke in the treasury while teaching in the temple, and no one seized Him, because His hour had not yet come. He said that where He was going they could not come, and that when they lifted up (Crucify, by the NKJV note) the Son of Man they would know that He is He; and many believed in Him. To those Jews who believed Him He said the truth would make them free; when they said they were Abraham's descendants He said they did what they had seen (NU heard from, by the NKJV note) with their father, and were of their father the devil, a murderer from the beginning and a liar. When the Jews said He had a demon, He said it was His Father who honored Him, of whom they said that He was their (NU, M our, by the NKJV note) God, that Abraham rejoiced to see His day, and that before Abraham was, I AM; they took up stones to throw at Him, but He hid Himself and went out of the temple, going through the midst of them (NU omits the rest of v. 59, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 9:4; NKJV notes, note on John 9:8; NKJV notes, note on John 9:9; NKJV notes, note on John 9:11; NKJV notes, note on John 9:16; NKJV notes, note on John 9:34; NKJV notes, note on John 9:35; John 9:1–41","v":"As Jesus passed by He saw a man blind from birth. His disciples asked whether this man or his parents had sinned, that he was born blind; He answered that neither had, but that the works of God should be revealed in him: He must (NU We, by the NKJV note) work the works of Him who sent Him while it is day, and as long as He is in the world He is the light of the world. He made clay with His saliva, anointed the man's eyes and sent him to wash in the pool of Siloam (which is translated, Sent), and he came back seeing. The neighbors and those who had seen that he was blind (NU a beggar, by the NKJV note) asked whether this was he who sat and begged; some said it was, others that he was like him (NU No, but he is like him, by the NKJV note), and he said, I am he, and told how a Man called Jesus had sent him to the pool of Siloam (NU omits the pool of, by the NKJV note) to wash, and he received sight. They brought him to the Pharisees; it was a Sabbath when Jesus made the clay. The Pharisees were divided, some saying this Man was not from God because He did not keep (observe, by the NKJV note) the Sabbath; asked again, the man told them, He is a prophet. The Jews did not believe he had been blind until they called his parents, who, fearing the Jews, said only that he was their son, born blind, and of age to be asked; for the Jews had agreed already that whoever confessed Him as Christ would be put out of the synagogue. Called again and bidden to give God the glory, the man said that though he was blind, now he saw, and that had this Man not been from God He could have done nothing; they reviled him and cast him out (excommunicated him, by the NKJV note). Jesus found him and asked whether he believed in the Son of God (NU Man, by the NKJV note); he said, Lord, I believe, and worshiped Him. Jesus said He came into this world for judgment, to give sight to those who do not see and to make blind those who see, and to the Pharisees who asked whether they were blind also He said that because they claim to see, their sin remains"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 10:8; NKJV notes, note on John 10:12; NKJV notes, note on John 10:20; NKJV notes, note on John 10:24; NKJV notes, note on John 10:26; NKJV notes, note on John 10:38; Psalms 82:6; John 10:1–42","v":"Jesus told of the sheepfold: whoever climbs in some other way than by the door is a thief and a robber, but the shepherd comes in by the door, calls his own by name and leads them out, and they follow, knowing his voice; they did not understand this illustration. He said He is the door of the sheep, and all who ever came before Him (M omits before Me, by the NKJV note) are thieves and robbers; whoever enters by Him will be saved, and He came that they may have life more abundantly. He is the good shepherd, who gives His life for the sheep, as the hireling (hired man, by the NKJV note) who flees from the wolf does not; He has other sheep not of this fold, and there will be one flock and one shepherd; He lays down His life of Himself, that He may take it again, and this command He received from His Father. The Jews were divided again, many saying He had a demon and was mad (insane, by the NKJV note), others asking whether a demon can open the eyes of the blind. At the Feast of Dedication in Jerusalem, in winter, as He walked in the temple in Solomon's porch, the Jews surrounded Him, asking how long He would keep them in doubt (suspense, by the NKJV note) and bidding Him say plainly whether He was the Christ. He answered that the works He did in His Father's name bore witness of Him, but they did not believe because they were not of His sheep, as He had said to them (NU omits as I said to you, by the NKJV note); His sheep hear His voice, no one can snatch them out of His Father's hand, and I and My Father are one. They took up stones again to stone Him for blasphemy, saying that He, being a Man, made Himself God; He answered from their law, I said, You are gods, and that the Scripture cannot be broken, and bade them believe the works, that they may know and believe (NU understand, by the NKJV note) that the Father is in Him and He in the Father. They sought again to seize Him, but He escaped out of their hand and withdrew once more across the Jordan to where John had baptized at first, and remained there; many came to Him, saying that John did no sign yet all he had said of this Man was true, and there many believed in Him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 11:18; NKJV notes, note on John 11:30; NKJV notes, note on John 11:31; NKJV notes, note on John 11:41; NKJV notes, note on John 11:50; John 11:1–57","v":"Lazarus of Bethany, the town of Mary and her sister Martha, was sick; this Mary is the one who, with fragrant oil, anointed the Lord and used her hair to wipe His feet. The sisters sent word to Jesus, who said the sickness was 'not unto death' but for God's glory, remained two more days where He was, and then set out for Judea again, though the Jews had lately sought to stone Him; He said Lazarus slept, and then plainly that he was dead, and Thomas, called the Twin, said they should go and die with Him. When Jesus came, Lazarus had been four days in the tomb; Bethany was about two miles (lit. 15 stadia, by the NKJV note) from Jerusalem, and many Jews had come to comfort the sisters. Martha met Jesus, who said He was the resurrection and the life, and she believed He was the Christ, the Son of God. Jesus had not entered the town but was (NU was still, by the NKJV note) at the spot where Martha had met Him, and Mary came to Him there, the Jews following and saying she was on her way to weep at the tomb (NU supposing that she was going, by the NKJV note); seeing her weep, Jesus 'groaned in the spirit', and He wept. At the tomb, a cave with a stone against it, the stone was removed from where the dead man lay (NU omits from the place where the dead man was lying, by the NKJV note); Jesus thanked the Father for hearing Him and called aloud, Lazarus, come forth, and Lazarus came out bound with graveclothes. Many of the Jews believed in Him, while some told the Pharisees; the chief priests and Pharisees gathered a council, fearing the Romans would take away their place and nation, and Caiaphas, that year's high priest, said it was expedient for us (NU you, by the NKJV note) for one man to die for the people rather than have the whole nation perish, in this prophesying Jesus's death for the nation and the gathering into one of God's scattered children; from that day they plotted His death. Jesus went to the city called Ephraim near the wilderness and remained there with His disciples; as the Jews' Passover drew near, many who went up to Jerusalem to purify themselves sought Him in the temple, and the chief priests and Pharisees had commanded that anyone who knew where He was should report it"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 12:1; NKJV notes, note on John 12:5; NKJV notes, note on John 12:7; NKJV notes, note on John 12:24; NKJV notes, note on John 12:32; NKJV notes, note on John 12:41; NKJV notes, note on John 12:47; Isaiah 6:10; 53:1; Zechariah 9:9; John 12:1–50","v":"Six days before the Passover Jesus came to Bethany, where Lazarus was, who had been dead (NU omits who had been dead, by the NKJV note), whom He had raised. At a supper there Martha served and Lazarus sat at the table with Him, and Mary anointed His feet with a pound of very costly oil of spikenard and wiped them with her hair. Judas Iscariot, Simon's son, a thief who had the money box, asked why it was not sold for three hundred denarii (about one year's wages for a worker, by the NKJV note) and given to the poor; Jesus bade him let her alone, for she had kept it for the day of His burial (NU that she may keep it, by the NKJV note). Many of the Jews came to see Lazarus and believed in Jesus, and the chief priests plotted to kill Lazarus also. The next day a great multitude come to the feast went out with palm branches to meet Him, crying Hosanna and the King of Israel, as He sat on a young donkey, as it is written; those who had been with Him when He raised Lazarus bore witness, and the Pharisees said the world had gone after Him. Certain Greeks asked Philip to see Jesus, and He said the hour had come for the Son of Man to be glorified, and that a grain of wheat that dies produces much grain (lit. fruit, by the NKJV note). His soul troubled, He said, Father, glorify Your name, and a voice from heaven answered, which some took for thunder; He said the ruler of this world would be cast out, and that if lifted up (Crucified, by the NKJV note) from the earth He would draw all peoples to Himself; bidding the people walk while they had the light, He departed and was hidden from them. Though He had done so many signs they did not believe, that Isaiah's words might be fulfilled, spoken when (NU because, by the NKJV note) he saw His glory; many even among the rulers believed but did not confess Him for fear of the Pharisees. Jesus cried out that he who believes in Him believes in Him who sent Him, that He came as a light into the world, that He does not judge whoever hears His words and does not believe (NU keep them, by the NKJV note), having come not to judge the world but to save it, and that the word He has spoken will judge in the last day whoever rejects Him and does not receive His words"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 13:2; NKJV notes, note on John 13:12; NKJV notes, note on John 13:18; NKJV notes, note on John 13:23; NKJV notes, note on John 13:25; Psalms 41:9; John 13:1–38","v":"Before the Feast of the Passover, knowing His hour had come to depart from this world to the Father, Jesus loved His own to the end. When supper was ended (NU during supper, by the NKJV note), the devil having already put it into the heart of Judas Iscariot, Simon's son, to betray Him, He rose, girded Himself with a towel and washed the disciples' feet; when Peter would not let Him, He said that unless He washed him he had no part with Him, and that they were clean, but not all. Seated again, He asked whether they knew (understand, by the NKJV note) what He had done: if their Lord and Teacher had washed their feet, they ought to wash one another's, for a servant is not greater than his master. He said the Scripture must be fulfilled, that he who eats bread with Him (NU My bread, by the NKJV note) has lifted up his heel against Him (Psalm 41:9), and, troubled in spirit, that one of them would betray Him; the disciple whom He loved, leaning (reclining, by the NKJV note) on His bosom, leaned back (NU, M add thus, by the NKJV note) at Peter's sign and asked who it was; Jesus gave the dipped piece of bread to Judas, Satan entered him, and he went out at once, and it was night. Then Jesus said the Son of Man was now glorified, that He would be with them a little while longer, and gave them a new commandment, to love one another as He had loved them; when Peter said he would lay down his life for Him, He told him the rooster would not crow till he had denied Him three times"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 14:2; NKJV notes, note on John 14:14; NKJV notes, note on John 14:15; NKJV notes, note on John 14:16; NKJV notes, note on John 14:21; NKJV notes, note on John 14:26; NKJV notes, note on John 14:28; John 14:1–31","v":"He bade them not let their heart be troubled but believe in God and in Him: in His Father's house are many mansions (lit. dwellings, by the NKJV note); if it were not so He would have told them (NU reads it otherwise, by the NKJV note); He goes to prepare a place for them and will come again to receive them to Himself. To Thomas He said He is the way, the truth, and the life, and no one comes to the Father except through Him; to Philip, that he who has seen Him has seen the Father, for He is in the Father and the Father in Him. Whoever believes in Him will do greater works, because He goes to His Father, and what they ask (NU ask Me, by the NKJV note) in His name He will do. If they love Him they are to keep (NU you will keep, by the NKJV note) His commandments, and the Father will give them another Helper (Comforter, Gr. Parakletos, by the NKJV note), the Spirit of truth, to abide with them forever; He will not leave them orphans. He will manifest (reveal, by the NKJV note) Himself to him who loves Him, and to Judas, not Iscariot, He said that whoever loves Him will keep His word, and His Father and He will make Their home with him. The Helper (Comforter, Gr. Parakletos, by the NKJV note), the Holy Spirit, whom the Father will send in His name, will teach them all things and bring to their remembrance all He said. He left them His peace; if they loved Him they would rejoice because He said (NU omits I said, by the NKJV note) He was going to the Father, for His Father is greater than He; the ruler of this world is coming and has nothing in Him; and He said, Arise, let us go from here"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 15:2; NKJV notes, note on John 15:7; NKJV notes, note on John 15:26; Psalms 35:19; 69:4; John 15:1–27","v":"Jesus said He is the true vine and His Father the vinedresser, who takes away (or lifts up, by the NKJV note) every branch in Him that bears no fruit and prunes the fruitful; the disciples, the branches, already clean by His word, were to abide in Him, for without Him they could do nothing, and abiding in Him they would (NU omits you will, by the NKJV note) ask what they desired and it would be done. As the Father loved Him He loved them; they were to keep His commandments and love one another as He loved them, for no one has greater love than to lay down his life for his friends, and He called them friends, not servants, having chosen them to bear fruit that remains. The world would hate and persecute them as it had hated Him without a cause, as written in their law; the Helper (Comforter, Gr. Parakletos, by the NKJV note), the Spirit of truth whom He would send from the Father, would testify of Him, and they also would bear witness"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 16:3; NKJV notes, note on John 16:4; NKJV notes, note on John 16:15; NKJV notes, note on John 16:18; NKJV notes, note on John 16:32; NKJV notes, note on John 16:33; John 16:1–33","v":"He told them these things that they should not stumble: they would be put out of the synagogues, and whoever killed them would think he served God, doing this (NU, M omit to you, by the NKJV note) because they had not known the Father nor Him; He told them so that when the time (NU their time, by the NKJV note) came they would remember. Now He was going to Him who sent Him, and it was to their advantage, for then He would send the Helper, who would convict the world of sin, of righteousness and of judgment, and the Spirit of truth would guide them into all truth and glorify Him, taking of what is His (NU, M takes of Mine and will declare, by the NKJV note). When He said that in a little while they would not see Him, and again a little while they would see Him, some of the disciples did not know (understand, by the NKJV note) what He meant; He told them their sorrow would be turned to joy, as a woman's when her child is born, and that the Father would give whatever they asked in His name. When they said they believed He came forth from God, He told them they would be scattered, each to his own (own things or place, by the NKJV note), and leave Him alone, yet the Father was with Him; in the world they would (NU, M omit will, by the NKJV note) have tribulation, but He had overcome the world"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 17:2; NKJV notes, note on John 17:5; NKJV notes, note on John 17:6; NKJV notes, note on John 17:11; NKJV notes, note on John 17:12; NKJV notes, note on John 17:17; NKJV notes, note on John 17:20; John 17:1–26","v":"Lifting up His eyes to heaven, Jesus prayed that, the hour having come, the Father glorify His Son, to whom He had given authority over all flesh to give (M shall give, by the NKJV note) eternal life to those the Father gave Him, eternal life being to know the only true God and Jesus Christ whom He sent; having finished His work, He asked to be glorified together with (lit. alongside, by the NKJV note) the Father with the glory He had before the world was. He had manifested (revealed, by the NKJV note) the Father's name to the men given Him out of the world, and prayed for them, not for the world: that the Holy Father keep them through His name (NU, M keep them through Your name which You have given Me, by the NKJV note), that they be one; He had kept them (NU omits in the world, and reads Your name which You gave Me. And I guarded them, by the NKJV notes), and none was lost (destroyed, by the NKJV note) but the son of perdition (destruction, by the NKJV note), that the Scripture might be fulfilled; that they be kept from the evil one and sanctified (set apart, by the NKJV note) by the truth, sent into the world as He was sent. He prayed also for those who will (NU, M omit will, by the NKJV note) believe in Him through their word, that all may be one and the world believe that the Father sent Him, and that those given Him may be with Him to behold His glory, for the Father loved Him before the foundation of the world"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 18:5; NKJV notes, note on John 18:14; NKJV notes, note on John 18:15; NKJV notes, note on John 18:20; NKJV notes, note on John 18:22; NKJV notes, note on John 18:28; NKJV notes, note on John 18:30; NKJV notes, note on John 18:33; John 18:1–40","v":"When He had spoken these words Jesus went out with His disciples over the Brook Kidron into a garden where He often met with them, and Judas came there with a detachment of troops and officers from the chief priests and Pharisees, with lanterns, torches and weapons. When those who sought Jesus of Nazareth (lit. the Nazarene, by the NKJV note) heard Him say, I am He, they drew back and fell to the ground; He bade them let His disciples go, that His saying might be fulfilled that of those given Him He had lost none. Simon Peter cut off the right ear of Malchus, the high priest's servant, and Jesus bade him sheathe his sword, for He would drink the cup His Father had given Him. They bound Jesus and led Him first to Annas, the father-in-law of Caiaphas, the high priest that year, who had advised the Jews that it was expedient (advantageous, by the NKJV note) that one man should die for the people. Peter followed with another (M the other, by the NKJV note) disciple, known to the high priest, who brought him into the courtyard, and Peter told the servant girl who kept the door that he was not one of His disciples. Asked by the high priest about His disciples and His doctrine, Jesus answered that He had taught openly in synagogues and in the temple, where the Jews always meet (NU all the Jews meet, by the NKJV note); an officer struck Him with the palm of his hand (lit. gave Jesus a slap, by the NKJV note), and Annas sent Him bound to Caiaphas. Warming himself at the fire, Peter denied Him twice more, and a rooster crowed. Early in the morning they led Jesus from Caiaphas to the Praetorium (the governor's headquarters, by the NKJV notes) but did not go in, lest they be defiled, that they might eat the Passover; they said they would not have delivered Him up had He not been an evildoer (a criminal, by the NKJV note), and that it was not lawful for them to put anyone to death, fulfilling His saying of what death He would die. Jesus told Pilate that His kingdom was not of this world and that He was a king, born to bear witness to the truth; Pilate asked, What is truth, and found no fault in Him. When he offered by their custom to release to them the King of the Jews at the Passover, they cried out for Barabbas, a robber"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 19:3; NKJV notes, note on John 19:7; NKJV notes, note on John 19:10; NKJV notes, note on John 19:16; NKJV notes, note on John 19:28; Psalms 22:18; Zechariah 12:10; John 19:1–42","v":"Pilate took Jesus and scourged Him, and the soldiers put a crown of thorns on His head and a purple robe on Him, hailed Him King of the Jews (NU And they came up to Him and said, by the NKJV note) and struck Him. Pilate brought Him out wearing them, that they might know he found no fault in Him, and said, Behold the Man; the chief priests and officers cried, Crucify Him, and the Jews said that by their (NU the, by the NKJV note) law He ought to die, for He made Himself the Son of God. The more afraid, Pilate asked Jesus where He was from; when He gave no answer Pilate spoke of his power (authority, by the NKJV note) to crucify or release Him, and Jesus said he could have none unless it were given him from above, and the one who delivered Him had the greater sin. When the Jews cried that in letting Him go he would be no friend of Caesar, Pilate sat in the judgment seat at The Pavement, in Hebrew Gabbatha, on the Preparation Day of the Passover about the sixth hour, and said, Behold your King; they cried, Crucify Him, the chief priests saying they had no king but Caesar, and he delivered Him to be crucified, and they led Him away (NU omits and led Him away, by the NKJV note). Bearing His cross He went out to the Place of a Skull, in Hebrew Golgotha, and was crucified between two others; Pilate's title on the cross, in Hebrew, Greek and Latin, read Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews, and he would not change it for the chief priests. The soldiers parted His garments in four and cast lots for the seamless tunic, that the Scripture might be fulfilled (Psalm 22:18). By the cross stood His mother, her sister, Mary the wife of Clopas and Mary Magdalene, and He gave His mother to the disciple whom He loved, who took her to his own home. Knowing (M seeing, by the NKJV note) that all was accomplished, He said, I thirst, received sour wine on hyssop, said, It is finished, and gave up His spirit. Because it was the Preparation Day and that Sabbath a high day, the Jews asked that the legs be broken; the soldiers broke the two others' legs, but finding Jesus dead, one of them pierced His side with a spear, and blood and water came out, as he who saw it has testified, that the Scripture might be fulfilled, Not one of His bones shall be broken, and another, They shall look on Him whom they pierced (Zechariah 12:10). Joseph of Arimathea, a disciple in secret for fear of the Jews, took the body with Pilate's leave, and with Nicodemus, who brought about a hundred pounds of myrrh and aloes, bound it in strips of linen with the spices, as the Jews bury, and laid it in a new tomb in a garden at the place of the crucifixion, because of the Jews' Preparation Day, for the tomb was nearby"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 20:7; NKJV notes, note on John 20:9; NKJV notes, note on John 20:16; NKJV notes, note on John 20:18; NKJV notes, note on John 20:19; NKJV notes, note on John 20:29; John 20:1–31","v":"On the first day of the week Mary Magdalene came to the tomb early, while it was still dark, and finding the stone taken away ran to Simon Peter and the other disciple, whom Jesus loved, saying the Lord had been taken out of the tomb. They ran to the tomb; the other disciple came first and, stooping, saw the linen cloths lying there, and Peter went in and saw them, and the handkerchief (face cloth, by the NKJV note) that had been around His head folded together in a place by itself; the other disciple went in also and saw and believed, for as yet they did not know (understand, by the NKJV note) the Scripture that He must rise again from the dead. Mary, weeping by the tomb, saw two angels in white where the body had lain, and then Jesus, whom she took for the gardener until He called her by name and she turned and said to Him (NU adds in Hebrew, by the NKJV note), Rabboni. He bade her not cling to Him, for He had not yet ascended to His Father, but go to His brethren, and she told the disciples that she had seen the Lord (NU I have seen the Lord, by the NKJV note). That evening, the doors shut where the disciples were assembled (NU omits assembled, by the NKJV note) for fear of the Jews, He stood in their midst and showed them His hands and His side; He sent them as the Father had sent Him, breathed on them and said, Receive the Holy Spirit, and spoke of the sins they would forgive and retain. Thomas, absent, would not believe without seeing and touching the print of the nails; after eight days Jesus came again, the doors being shut, and bade him reach his finger and his hand, and Thomas answered, My Lord and my God. Jesus told him, Thomas (NU, M omit Thomas, by the NKJV note), that he had believed because he had seen, and called blessed those who have not seen and yet have believed. Jesus did many other signs before His disciples that are not written in this book; these are written that the reader may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and believing have life in His name"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 21:3; NKJV notes, note on John 21:15; NKJV notes, note on John 21:16; NKJV notes, note on John 21:17; NKJV notes, note on John 21:22; John 21:1–25","v":"After these things Jesus showed Himself again to the disciples at the Sea of Tiberias. Simon Peter, Thomas called the Twin, Nathanael of Cana in Galilee, the sons of Zebedee and two others of His disciples were together; Peter went fishing and the others with him, and they went out and immediately (NU omits immediately, by the NKJV note) got into the boat, but caught nothing that night. In the morning Jesus stood on the shore, unknown to them, and bade them cast the net on the right side of the boat, and they could not draw it in for the multitude of fish. The disciple whom Jesus loved said to Peter, It is the Lord, and Peter put on his outer garment and plunged into the sea; the others came in the little boat dragging the net, and on land found a fire of coals with fish laid on it, and bread. Peter dragged the net to land, full of one hundred and fifty-three large fish, and it was not broken; Jesus bade them eat breakfast and gave them the bread and the fish, the third time He showed Himself to His disciples after He was raised from the dead. After breakfast He asked Simon, son of Jonah (NU John, by the NKJV note), three times whether he loved Him, the third time whether he loved (had affection for, by the NKJV note) Him; the first two times Simon answered that He knew that he loved (had affection for, by the NKJV note) Him, and grieved at the third asking he said, Lord, You know all things; You know that I love You. Jesus bade him feed His lambs, tend His sheep and feed His sheep, told him that when he was old another would gird him and carry him where he did not wish, signifying by what death he would glorify God, and said, Follow Me. Peter, turning, saw the disciple whom Jesus loved following, who had leaned on His breast at the supper, and asked what of this man; Jesus answered that it was nothing to Peter whether He willed (desired, by the NKJV note) that he remain till He came, and that Peter was to follow Him. So the saying went out among the brethren that this disciple would not die, though Jesus had not said so. This is the disciple who testifies of these things and wrote them, and his testimony is true; and Jesus did so many other things that the world itself, the writer supposes, could not contain the books that would be written of them one by one"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 1:1","v":"In the beginning was the Word"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 1:1–14","v":"It opens in the beginning, with the Word who was with God and was God, through whom all things were made, and who became flesh and dwelt among us"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 1:6–8, 15, 19–36","v":"It calls John the Baptist a man sent from God to bear witness of the Light, and gives his witness to Jesus as the Lamb of God and the Son of God"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 1:38, 41–42","v":"It gives some words as they were spoken and translates them: Rabbi, Teacher; Messiah, the Christ; Cephas, A Stone"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 1:29, 35, 43","v":"It marks the days one after another: the next day, again the next day, the following day"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 2:11, 23; 3:2","v":"It calls the works of Jesus signs, the water made wine at Cana being the beginning of them"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 7:53; John 7:53–8:11","v":"Its passage 7:53–8:11 is bracketed in the NU text as not in the original text, though it is present in over 900 manuscripts of John","b":"NKJV note"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 19:35","v":"Of the piercing it gives the true witness of one who saw it and knows he speaks the truth, so that those it addresses may believe"},{"p":"feature","r":"Psalms 22:18; Zechariah 12:10; John 19:24, 28, 36–37","v":"At the cross it tells of Scripture fulfilled in the garments divided by lot, in His thirst and in no bone of His being broken, and gives another Scripture, of the One they pierced"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 20:30; 21:25","v":"It says that Jesus did many other signs in the presence of His disciples that are not written in it, and so many other things that the world itself, the writer supposes, could not contain the books of them"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 21:20–24","v":"It closes by saying that the disciple whom Jesus loved, who had leaned on His breast at the supper, testifies of these things and wrote them, and that his testimony is true"},{"p":"made by","r":"John 21:20–24","e":"PER-03184"},{"p":"purpose","r":"John 20:30–31","v":"That its readers may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that believing they may have life in His name"}],"nm":{"PER-03184":"The disciple whom Jesus loved"}},"OBJ-00638":{"k":"object","n":"The six waterpots of stone at Cana","d":"Six waterpots of stone set at the wedding in Cana for the purification of the Jews, which the servants filled with water at Jesus's word (John 2:6–9)","f":[{"p":"count","r":"John 2:6","v":"6"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 2:6","v":"six waterpots of stone"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 2:6","v":"Each held twenty or thirty gallons"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 2:7–9","v":"At Jesus's word they were filled with water up to the brim, and what was drawn from them and taken to the master of the feast was water made wine","at":"EVT-02996"},{"p":"material","r":"John 2:6","v":"Stone"},{"p":"placed at","r":"John 2:1, 6","e":"PLC-01514"},{"p":"purpose","r":"John 2:6","v":"Set there for the manner of purification of the Jews"}],"nm":{"EVT-02996":"Jesus turns water into wine at a wedding in Cana","PLC-01514":"Cana"}},"OBJ-00639":{"k":"object","n":"The whip of cords Jesus made","d":"A whip Jesus made of cords, with which He drove the sellers and money changers out of the temple (John 2:15)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"John 2:15","v":"a whip of cords"},{"p":"made by","r":"John 2:15","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"material","r":"John 2:15","v":"Cords"},{"p":"occasion","r":"John 2:15","e":"EVT-02999"},{"p":"purpose","r":"John 2:15","v":"With it Jesus drove them all out of the temple, with the sheep and the oxen","b":"inferred","w":"When He had made a whip of cords, He drove them all out of the temple (John 2:15); the text does not say in words that He drove them with it."}],"nm":{"EVT-02999":"Jesus drives the sellers out of the temple with a whip of cords","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00640":{"k":"object","n":"The illustration of the sheepfold","d":"One who climbs into the sheepfold some other way than by the door is a thief and a robber, but the shepherd enters by the door, calls his own sheep by name and leads them out, and they follow him, knowing his voice (John 10:1–6)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"John 10:1–5","v":"Whoever climbs into the sheepfold some other way than by the door is a thief and a robber; the shepherd comes in by the door, the doorkeeper opens to him, and the sheep hear his voice. He calls his own by name and leads them out, walking ahead of them, and they follow because they know his voice; a stranger they will never follow but will run from, since they do not know his voice"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 10:6","v":"this illustration"},{"p":"made by","r":"John 10:1–6","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"John 10:1–6","e":"EVT-03040"}],"nm":{"EVT-03040":"Jesus says He is the door of the sheep and the good shepherd","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00641":{"k":"object","n":"The oil of spikenard with which Mary anointed Jesus","d":"The pound of very costly oil of spikenard with which Mary anointed the feet of Jesus at Bethany six days before the Passover (John 12:3–7)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"John 12:3","v":"a pound of very costly oil of spikenard"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 12:5; John 12:3–5","v":"Its fragrance filled the house; Judas said it might have been sold for three hundred denarii (about one year's wages for a worker, by the NKJV note)","at":"EVT-03054"},{"p":"material","r":"John 12:3","v":"oil of spikenard"},{"p":"owner","r":"John 12:3, 7","e":"PER-03150"},{"p":"purpose","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 12:7; John 12:7","v":"She had kept it for the day of His burial (NU that she may keep it, by the NKJV note)"}],"nm":{"EVT-03054":"Mary anoints the feet of Jesus at Bethany","PER-03150":"Mary, the sister of Martha"}},"OBJ-00642":{"k":"object","n":"The money box Judas Iscariot kept","d":"The money box Judas Iscariot had, from which he used to take what was put in it, for he was a thief (John 12:6)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"John 12:6","v":"What was put in it, which Judas used to take"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 12:6; 13:29","v":"the money box"},{"p":"owner","r":"John 12:6","e":"PER-03039"}],"nm":{"PER-03039":"Judas"}},"OBJ-00643":{"k":"object","n":"Jesus's prayer for His disciples and all who will believe","d":"The prayer Jesus prayed to the Father, lifting up His eyes to heaven, for Himself, for His disciples and for those who will believe in Him through their word (John 17:1–26)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 17:2; NKJV notes, note on John 17:5; John 17:1–5","v":"Jesus asked the Father, the hour having come, to glorify His Son, that the Son might glorify Him, as He had given Him authority over all flesh to give (M shall give, by the NKJV note) eternal life to as many as He had given Him; eternal life is to know the only true God and Jesus Christ whom He sent; He had glorified the Father on the earth and finished His work, and asked to be glorified together with (lit. alongside, by the NKJV note) the Father with the glory He had with Him before the world was"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 17:6; NKJV notes, note on John 17:11; NKJV notes, note on John 17:12; NKJV notes, note on John 17:17; John 17:6–19","v":"He had manifested (revealed, by the NKJV note) the Father's name to the men given Him out of the world, who had kept the Father's word and believed that the Father sent Him; He prayed for them, not for the world: that the Holy Father keep through His name those given Him (NU, M keep them through Your name which You have given Me, by the NKJV note), that they be one as They are one; while with them He had kept them (NU omits in the world, and reads Your name which You gave Me. And I guarded them, by the NKJV notes), and none was lost (destroyed, by the NKJV note) but the son of perdition (destruction, by the NKJV note), that the Scripture might be fulfilled; not that they be taken out of the world, but kept from the evil one; that the Father sanctify (set apart, by the NKJV note) them by His truth, for His word is truth; as the Father sent Him into the world He has sent them, and for their sakes He sanctifies Himself"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 17:20; John 17:20–26","v":"He prayed also for those who will (NU, M omit will, by the NKJV note) believe in Him through their word, that all may be one, as the Father is in Him and He in the Father, that the world may believe and know that the Father sent Him and loved them as He loved Him; He had given them the glory the Father gave Him, and desired that those given Him be with Him where He is to behold His glory, for the Father loved Him before the foundation of the world; the world has not known the righteous Father, but He has, and these have known that the Father sent Him; He has declared the Father's name and will declare it, that the Father's love may be in them, and He in them"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 17:1","v":"Father, the hour has come"},{"p":"made by","r":"John 17:1","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"John 17:1","e":"EVT-03068"}],"nm":{"EVT-03068":"Jesus prays for Himself, His disciples and all who will believe","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00644":{"k":"object","n":"The myrrh and aloes Nicodemus brought","d":"A mixture of myrrh and aloes, about a hundred pounds, that Nicodemus brought and that was bound with the body of Jesus in strips of linen (John 19:39–40)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"John 19:39","v":"a mixture of myrrh and aloes"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 19:40","v":"the spices"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 19:39","v":"About a hundred pounds"},{"p":"material","r":"John 19:39","v":"Myrrh and aloes"},{"p":"purpose","r":"John 19:40","v":"To be bound with the body of Jesus in strips of linen, as the Jews bury"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00645":{"k":"object","n":"The net full of fish at the Sea of Tiberias","d":"The net the disciples cast on the right side of the boat at Jesus's word, which Simon Peter dragged to land full of one hundred and fifty-three large fish, and which was not broken (John 21:6–11)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"John 21:11","v":"Large fish, one hundred and fifty-three"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 21:6, 8, 11","v":"the net"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 21:11","v":"Though there were so many fish, it was not broken","at":"EVT-03077"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 21:6, 8","v":"Cast on the right side of the boat at Jesus's word, it could not be drawn in for the multitude of fish, and the other disciples dragged it after the little boat","at":"EVT-03077"}],"nm":{"EVT-03077":"Jesus shows Himself to seven disciples at the Sea of Tiberias"}},"OBJ-00646":{"k":"object","n":"The Acts of the Apostles","d":"The account of what the apostles did after Jesus was taken up, from Jerusalem to Rome, written to Theophilus after a former account (Acts 1:1–28:31)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:2; NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:3; NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:8; NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:14; NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:20; Acts 1:1–26","v":"The writer's former account to Theophilus ran until the day Jesus was taken up. He showed Himself alive to the apostles by many infallible (unmistakable, by the NKJV note) proofs during forty days, bade them wait in Jerusalem for the Promise of the Father, the baptism with the Holy Spirit, and said they would be witnesses (NU My witnesses, by the NKJV note) to Him to the end of the earth; while they watched He was taken up, and two men in white said He would come again in like manner. Returning from Olivet to the upper room, the apostles continued with one accord (purpose or mind, by the NKJV note) in prayer (NU omits and supplication, by the NKJV note) with the women, Mary and His brothers. Peter told the disciples (NU brethren, by the NKJV note), about a hundred and twenty, that Scripture had to be fulfilled concerning Judas, who bought a field with the wages of iniquity (reward of unrighteousness, by the NKJV note) and, falling headlong, burst open so that his entrails (intestines, by the NKJV note) gushed out, and that his dwelling should be desolate (deserted, by the NKJV note) and another take his office (Gr. episkopen, position of overseer, by the NKJV note); the lot fell on Matthias, who was numbered with the eleven apostles"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:1; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:3; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:8; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:11; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:23; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:24; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:30; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:33; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:38; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:40; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:41; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:42; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:45; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:47; Acts 2:1–47","v":"On the Day of Pentecost, as they were all with one accord (NU together, by the NKJV note) in one place, a sound as of a rushing mighty wind filled the house, divided tongues as of fire (or tongues as of fire, distributed and resting on each, by the NKJV note) sat on each of them, and filled with the Holy Spirit they spoke with other tongues, so that devout men from every nation heard them each in his own language (dialect, by the NKJV note), Parthians to Arabs (Arabians, by the NKJV note). Peter said they were not drunk at the third hour (9 a.m., by the NKJV note), but this was Joel's word; Jesus, attested by God and crucified by lawless hands (NU omits have taken, by the NKJV note), God raised up, having loosed (destroyed or abolished, by the NKJV note) the pains (lit. birth pangs, by the NKJV note) of death, as David foresaw of the One to sit on his throne (NU He would seat one on his throne, by the NKJV note); exalted to (possibly by, by the NKJV note) the right hand of God, He poured out the Spirit. He bade them repent and be baptized for the remission (forgiveness, by the NKJV note) of sins and be saved from this perverse (crooked, by the NKJV note) generation; those who gladly (NU omits gladly, by the NKJV note) received his word were baptized, about three thousand, and continued in the apostles' doctrine (teaching, by the NKJV note), sold (would sell, by the NKJV note) their goods and divided (distributed, by the NKJV note) them as any had need, and the Lord added to the church (NU omits to the church, by the NKJV note) daily those who were being saved"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 3:2; NKJV notes, note on Acts 3:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 3:20; NKJV notes, note on Acts 3:21; NKJV notes, note on Acts 3:24; Acts 3:1–26","v":"At the ninth hour Peter and John, going up to the temple, healed in the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth a man lame from birth who was laid daily at the Beautiful Gate to ask (beg, by the NKJV note) alms. In Solomon's porch Peter told the people that the God of their fathers had glorified His Servant Jesus, whom they denied before Pilate, asking for a murderer and killing the Prince (or Originator, by the NKJV note) of life, whom God raised; they were to repent, that He might send Jesus Christ (NU, M Christ Jesus, by the NKJV note), preached (NU, M ordained, by the NKJV note) to them before, whom heaven must receive until the restoration God spoke of since the world (or time, by the NKJV note) began, of whom Moses spoke as a Prophet like him, and all the prophets foretold (NU, M proclaimed, by the NKJV note) these days"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 4:16; NKJV notes, note on Acts 4:25; NKJV notes, note on Acts 4:36; Acts 4:1–37","v":"The priests, the captain of the temple and the Sadducees put Peter and John in custody, and about five thousand men believed; the next day, before Annas, Caiaphas and the rulers, Peter, filled with the Holy Spirit, said the man stood whole by the name of Jesus, the stone the builders rejected, and there is salvation in no other name. Unable to deny the notable miracle (remarkable sign, by the NKJV note), evident (well known, by the NKJV note) to all in Jerusalem, the council forbade them to teach in that name, but they answered that they must speak what they had seen and heard. Let go, they prayed with their companions, in the words God spoke by the mouth of David (NU through the Holy Spirit, by the mouth of our father David, by the NKJV note), for boldness, and the place was shaken; the believers had all things in common, and Joses (NU Joseph, by the NKJV note), named Barnabas, Son of Encouragement (or Consolation, by the NKJV note), a Levite of Cyprus, sold his land and laid the money at the apostles' feet"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:21; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:23; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:24; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:25; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:33; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:35; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:36; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:41; Acts 5:1–42","v":"Ananias and his wife Sapphira kept back part of the price of their land and lied to the Holy Spirit, and each fell dead at Peter's word, and great fear came on the church. Many signs were done through the apostles, and the sick were laid in the streets for Peter's shadow to fall on them. The high priest and the Sadducees, filled with indignation (jealousy, by the NKJV note), put the apostles in prison, but an angel opened the doors; the officers found the prison shut and the guards (NU, M omit outside, by the NKJV note) at the doors, and the high priest (NU omits the high priest, by the NKJV note) and the chief priests wondered, until one told them (NU, M omit saying, by the NKJV note) the men were teaching in the temple. Before the council (Sanhedrin, by the NKJV note) and the elders (council of elders or senate, by the NKJV note) Peter said they must obey God rather than men; the council, furious (cut to the quick, by the NKJV note), heeded Gamaliel, who bade them take heed (be careful, by the NKJV note), recalling Theudas, whom four hundred joined (followed, by the NKJV note), and Judas of Galilee; beaten and let go, the apostles rejoiced to suffer shame for His name (NU the name; M the name of Jesus, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 6:1; NKJV notes, note on Acts 6:8; NKJV notes, note on Acts 6:13; Acts 6:1–15","v":"When the Hellenists (Greek-speaking Jews, by the NKJV note) complained that their widows were neglected, the twelve had the disciples choose seven, Stephen, Philip, Prochorus, Nicanor, Timon, Parmenas and Nicolas, and laid hands on them; the word spread and many priests obeyed the faith. Stephen, full of faith (NU grace, by the NKJV note) and power, did great wonders; men of the Synagogue of the Freedmen, unable to resist his wisdom, brought him before the council with false witnesses who said he spoke blasphemous (NU omits blasphemous, by the NKJV note) words against the holy place and the law, and his face was as the face of an angel"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 7:11; NKJV notes, note on Acts 7:14; NKJV notes, note on Acts 7:30; NKJV notes, note on Acts 7:37; NKJV notes, note on Acts 7:38; NKJV notes, note on Acts 7:51; NKJV notes, note on Acts 7:54; Acts 7:1–60","v":"Stephen told of God's appearing to Abraham in Mesopotamia before he dwelt in Haran, the promise and the covenant of circumcision; of Joseph sold into Egypt, the famine and great trouble (affliction, by the NKJV note), and Jacob's going down with seventy-five (or seventy, Exodus 1:5, by the NKJV note) souls; of Moses, born in the oppression, rejected by his brethren, sent by the Angel (NU omits of the Lord, by the NKJV note) in the bush to deliver them, who spoke of the Prophet like him (NU, M omit Him you shall hear, by the NKJV note) and was in the congregation (Gr. ekklesia, assembly or church, by the NKJV note) in the wilderness and received the living oracles (sayings, by the NKJV note), whom the fathers would not obey but made a calf; of the tabernacle of witness and the house Solomon built, though the Most High does not dwell in temples made with hands. He called them stiff-necked (stubborn, by the NKJV note), resisting the Holy Spirit; furious (cut to the heart, by the NKJV note), they stoned him outside the city as he saw Jesus standing at the right hand of God, and the witnesses laid their clothes at the feet of a young man named Saul"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 8:5; NKJV notes, note on Acts 8:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 8:11; NKJV notes, note on Acts 8:26; NKJV notes, note on Acts 8:37; NKJV notes, note on Acts 8:40; Acts 8:1–40","v":"Saul consented to Stephen's death; a great persecution scattered the church through Judea and Samaria except the apostles, devout men buried Stephen, and Saul made havoc of the church. Philip preached Christ in the city (or a city, by the NKJV note) of Samaria, where Simon had astonished the people (or nation, by the NKJV note) with sorcery (magic, by the NKJV note; magic arts, by the NKJV note on 8:11), and they believed and were baptized, Simon too; Peter and John laid hands on them and they received the Holy Spirit, and Simon, offering money for that power, was rebuked. An angel sent Philip to the road from Jerusalem to Gaza, which is desert (or a deserted place, by the NKJV note), where he preached Jesus from Isaiah to a eunuch of Ethiopia, Candace's treasurer, who confessed Jesus Christ the Son of God (NU, M omit v. 37, found in Western texts, including the Latin tradition, by the NKJV note) and was baptized; the Spirit caught Philip away to Azotus (the same as the Hebrew Ashdod, by the NKJV note), and he preached in all the cities to Caesarea"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:5; NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:13; NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:20; NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:29; NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:31; NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:36; Acts 9:1–43","v":"Saul, with letters from the high priest, was struck near Damascus by a light from heaven and heard Jesus, whom he persecuted (NU, M omit the rest of v. 5, by the NKJV note), bid him go into the city; blind three days, he was found by Ananias, whom the Lord sent though he had heard of Saul's harm (bad things, by the NKJV note) to the saints, for Saul was a chosen vessel to bear His name before Gentiles, kings and the children (lit. sons, by the NKJV note) of Israel; the Lord (M omits Jesus, by the NKJV note) restored his sight and he was baptized. He preached the Christ (NU Jesus, by the NKJV note) in the synagogues, escaped the Jews' plot over the wall in a basket, was brought to the apostles by Barnabas, disputed with the Hellenists (Greek-speaking Jews, by the NKJV note) and was sent to Tarsus. The churches (NU church, by the NKJV note) had peace and were edified (built up, by the NKJV note). Peter healed Aeneas at Lydda, and at Joppa raised Tabitha, which is Dorcas (lit., in Aramaic and in Greek, Gazelle, by the NKJV notes), and stayed with Simon a tanner"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:1; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:2; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:3; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:6; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:21; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:30; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:31; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:32; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:36; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:39; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:43; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:45; Acts 10:1–48","v":"At the ninth hour (3 p.m., by the NKJV note) an angel bade Cornelius, a centurion of the Italian Regiment (Cohort, by the NKJV note) who gave alms (charitable gifts, by the NKJV note), send to Joppa for Peter, who lodged with Simon a tanner by the sea (NU, M omit the rest of v. 6, by the NKJV note). At the sixth hour (noon, by the NKJV note) Peter saw in a trance a sheet let down with all kinds of beasts and heard that what God had cleansed (declared clean, by the NKJV note) he must not call common; while he wondered (was perplexed, by the NKJV note) the men came (NU, M omit who had been sent to him from Cornelius, by the NKJV note), and he went with them to Caesarea. Cornelius told of his vision four days before (NU four days ago to this hour, at the ninth hour, by the NKJV note), and his alms (charitable gifts, by the NKJV note) remembered (NU omits the rest of v. 32, by the NKJV note); Peter preached the word sent to the children (lit. sons, by the NKJV note) of Israel, Jesus whom they (NU, M they also, by the NKJV note) killed and God raised, through whose name is remission (forgiveness, by the NKJV note) of sins; the Holy Spirit fell on all who heard, to the astonishment of those of the circumcision (the Jews, by the NKJV note), and they were baptized"},{"p":"contents","r":"Acts 11:1–30","v":"At Jerusalem those of the circumcision contended with Peter for eating with uncircumcised men, but when he told them in order of his vision and of the Holy Spirit falling on the Gentiles, they glorified God, who had granted the Gentiles repentance to life. Those scattered after Stephen preached as far as Phoenicia, Cyprus and Antioch, and at Antioch men of Cyprus and Cyrene preached the Lord Jesus to the Hellenists and many believed; Barnabas, sent by the church in Jerusalem, brought Saul from Tarsus, they taught there a whole year, and the disciples were first called Christians at Antioch. Agabus foretold a great famine, which came in the days of Claudius Caesar, and the disciples sent relief to the elders in Judea by Barnabas and Saul"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 12:4; NKJV notes, note on Acts 12:5; NKJV notes, note on Acts 12:6; NKJV notes, note on Acts 12:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 12:20; NKJV notes, note on Acts 12:23; NKJV notes, note on Acts 12:25; Acts 12:1–25","v":"Herod the king killed James the brother of John with the sword and, during the Days of Unleavened Bread, put Peter in prison under four squads (Gr. tetrads, squads of four, by the NKJV note) of soldiers, while the church prayed constantly (NU constantly or earnestly, by the NKJV note). The night before he was to be brought out, with the guards keeping (guarding, by the NKJV note) the prison, an angel freed Peter, and he came to the house of Mary, the mother of John Mark, where Rhoda left him knocking at the gate; he bade them tell James and the brethren, and went to another place. At day there was no small stir (disturbance, by the NKJV note) among the soldiers; Herod had the guards put to death and went down to Caesarea, where the people of Tyre and Sidon, having made Blastus, the king's personal aide (who was in charge of the king's bedchamber, by the NKJV note), their friend, asked for peace, for their country was supplied with food (lit. nourished, by the NKJV note) by the king's; hailed as a god, Herod was struck by an angel of the Lord, eaten by worms, and died (breathed his last, by the NKJV note). Barnabas and Saul returned from (NU, M to, by the NKJV note) Jerusalem with John Mark"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:6; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:8; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:23; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:26; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:34; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:36; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:37; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:42; Acts 13:1–52","v":"At Antioch the Holy Spirit bade the church set apart Barnabas and Saul; they sailed from Seleucia to Cyprus and went through the island (NU the whole island, by the NKJV note) to Paphos, where Elymas the sorcerer withstood (opposed, by the NKJV note) them and Saul, also called Paul, struck him blind, and the proconsul believed. From Perga, where John left them, they came to Antioch in Pisidia; bidden to give a word of exhortation (encouragement, by the NKJV note), Paul told how God chose Israel (M omits Israel, by the NKJV note) and brought them out with an uplifted arm (mighty power, by the NKJV note), gave them judges and kings, and of David's seed raised up a Savior (M salvation, by the NKJV note), Jesus, the word of salvation (message, by the NKJV note) sent to the family (stock, by the NKJV note) of Abraham, whom God raised, no more to see corruption (the state of decay, by the NKJV note), giving the sure mercies (blessings, by the NKJV note) of David, while David, having served his (in his, by the NKJV note) generation, saw corruption (underwent decay, by the NKJV note) and He did not (underwent no decay, by the NKJV note); when they went out (NU And when they went out, they begged, by the NKJV note), the Gentiles begged to hear more, and when the Jews opposed, Paul and Barnabas turned to the Gentiles and were expelled"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 14:2; NKJV notes, note on Acts 14:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 14:12; Acts 14:1–28","v":"At Iconium many believed, but the unbelieving Jews poisoned (embittered, by the NKJV note) the Gentiles' minds (lit. souls, by the NKJV note), and they fled to Lystra and Derbe; at Lystra Paul healed (lit. who, by the NKJV note) a man lame from birth, and the people called Barnabas Zeus (Jupiter, by the NKJV note) and Paul Hermes (Mercury, by the NKJV note) and would have sacrificed to them; Jews from Antioch and Iconium stoned Paul, but he rose and went to Derbe. Returning through the cities they appointed elders, and sailed from Attalia to Antioch, where they told how God had opened the door of faith to the Gentiles"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:8; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:11; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:13; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:20; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:22; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:24; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:25; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:29; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:33; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:34; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:37; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:40; Acts 15:1–41","v":"Men from Judea taught at Antioch that the Gentiles must be circumcised, and Paul and Barnabas went up to Jerusalem. Peter said God acknowledged (bore witness to, by the NKJV note) the Gentiles by the Holy Spirit and that all are saved through the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ (NU, M omit Christ, by the NKJV note); when they stopped speaking (by the NKJV note), James judged by the prophets' words of the tabernacle of David rebuilt, by the Lord who does these things (NU who makes these things, by the NKJV note), known from eternity (NU known from eternity of old, by the NKJV note), that the Gentiles should only abstain from things polluted by idols, sexual immorality (or fornication, by the NKJV notes), things strangled and blood. The apostles and elders, with one accord (purpose or mind, by the NKJV note), wrote so to the Gentile brethren (NU omits saying, You must be circumcised and keep the law, by the NKJV note) and sent Judas called Barsabas (NU, M Barsabbas, by the NKJV note) and Silas with Paul and Barnabas; at Antioch the letter brought joy, and Judas was sent back to the apostles (NU those who had sent them, by the NKJV note), but Silas stayed (NU, M omit v. 34, by the NKJV note). When Barnabas was determined (resolved, by the NKJV note) to take Mark, they parted; Barnabas sailed with Mark to Cyprus, and Paul, commended (committed, by the NKJV note) by the brethren, went with Silas through Syria and Cilicia"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 16:6; NKJV notes, note on Acts 16:7; NKJV notes, note on Acts 16:12; NKJV notes, note on Acts 16:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 16:35; Acts 16:1–40","v":"At Lystra Paul took Timothy, son of a believing Jewess and a Greek father, and they delivered the decrees through the cities. Forbidden by the Holy Spirit to preach in Asia (the Roman province of Asia, by the NKJV note) and not permitted by the Spirit (NU adds of Jesus, by the NKJV note) to enter Bithynia, they came to Troas, where Paul saw a man of Macedonia pleading for help; by Samothrace and Neapolis they came to Philippi, the foremost (lit. first, by the NKJV note) city of that part of Macedonia, a colony, where Lydia was baptized. Paul, annoyed (distressed, by the NKJV note), cast a spirit of divination out of a slave girl; her masters had Paul and Silas beaten and imprisoned, but at midnight an earthquake opened the doors and the jailer believed; at day the magistrates sent officers (lictors, lit. rod bearers, by the NKJV note) to release them, and learning they were Romans pleaded with them to depart"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:5; NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:7; NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:11; NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:16; NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:19; NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:22; NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:26; Acts 17:1–34","v":"At Thessalonica Paul reasoned three Sabbaths that the Christ had to suffer and rise, and many Greeks believed; the Jews who were not persuaded (NU omits it, by the NKJV note), envious (M omits becoming envious, by the NKJV note), set the city in an uproar and dragged Jason, who had harbored (welcomed, by the NKJV note) them, before the rulers. The Bereans were more fair-minded (lit. noble, by the NKJV note) and searched the Scriptures daily. At Athens, a city given over to (full of, by the NKJV note) idols, Epicurean and (NU, M add also, by the NKJV note) Stoic philosophers called Paul a babbler (lit. seed picker, an idler who makes a living picking up scraps, by the NKJV note) and brought him to the Areopagus (lit. Hill of Ares, or Mars' Hill, by the NKJV notes), where he proclaimed the unknown God, who made from one blood (NU omits blood, by the NKJV note) every nation and will judge the world by the Man He raised from the dead; some mocked, but Dionysius and Damaris believed"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:5; NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:7; NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:12; NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:21; NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:22; NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:23; Acts 18:1–28","v":"At Corinth Paul worked with Aquila and Priscilla, tentmakers come from Italy because Claudius had commanded the Jews to leave Rome; when Silas and Timothy came, Paul, compelled by the Spirit (or in his spirit or in the Spirit, by the NKJV note), testified that Jesus is the Christ, and when the Jews blasphemed he went to the house of Justus (NU Titius Justus, by the NKJV note); Crispus believed, and Paul stayed a year and six months. The Jews brought him to the judgment seat (Gr. bema, by the NKJV note) of Gallio, who drove them away, and the Greeks (NU they all, by the NKJV note) beat Sosthenes. After a good while (lit. many days, by the NKJV note) Paul sailed for Syria, cut his hair at Cenchrea for a vow, reasoned at Ephesus, promising to keep the feast at Jerusalem (NU omits it, by the NKJV note), landed at Caesarea, went up (to Jerusalem, by the NKJV note) and greeted the church, and went down to Antioch; then he went over Galatia and Phrygia in order (successively, by the NKJV note). Apollos of Alexandria, knowing only John's baptism, taught at Ephesus, was taught the way of God more accurately by Aquila and Priscilla, and in Achaia refuted the Jews"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:13; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:16; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:24; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:27; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:31; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:35; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:37; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:38; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:40; Acts 19:1–41","v":"At Ephesus Paul baptized about twelve disciples who knew only John's baptism, and the Holy Spirit came on them; he reasoned three months in the synagogue and two years in the school of Tyrannus, and God worked unusual miracles by his hands. Itinerant Jewish exorcists said they exorcised (NU I exorcise, by the NKJV note; adjure, solemnly command, by the NKJV note) by the Jesus whom Paul preached, but the evil spirit overpowered (M and they overpowered, by the NKJV note) the seven sons of Sceva (NU both of them, by the NKJV note), and many burned their books of magic. Demetrius, who made silver shrines of Diana (Gr. Artemis, by the NKJV note), said her magnificence might be destroyed (NU she be deposed from her magnificence, by the NKJV note), and the city rushed into the theater; the officials of Asia (Asiarchs, rulers of Asia, the province, by the NKJV note) begged Paul not to go in, and the city clerk said Ephesus was temple guardian of Diana (Gr. Artemis, by the NKJV note) and of the image that fell from Zeus (heaven, by the NKJV note), these men were no blasphemers of their (NU our, by the NKJV note) goddess, a case (lit. matter, by the NKJV note) belonged to the courts, and they were in danger of being called in question (or charged with rebellion concerning today, by the NKJV note) for the uproar"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:8; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:13; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:24; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:26; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:27; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:28; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:30; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:34; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:37; Acts 20:1–38","v":"Paul went through Macedonia to Greece and stayed three months, returned through Macedonia because of a plot, and sailed from Philippi after the Days of Unleavened Bread to Troas, where in the upper room where they (NU, M we, by the NKJV note) were gathered Eutychus fell from the third story and was taken up alive. Having arranged (by the NKJV note) to go on foot to Assos, he sailed by Mitylene, Chios and Samos to Miletus, hurrying to be at Jerusalem for Pentecost, and told the elders of Ephesus that, though he did not count his life dear (NU But I do not count my life of any value or dear to myself, by the NKJV note), finishing his race (course, by the NKJV note), he was innocent (lit. clean, by the NKJV note) of the blood of all, had not shunned (avoided declaring, by the NKJV note) the whole counsel of God, and bade them shepherd the church of God (M of the Lord and God, by the NKJV note), for wolves and men speaking perverse (misleading, by the NKJV note) things would come; they wept freely (lit. much, by the NKJV note) at his words"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:4; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:8; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:22; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:25; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:26; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:29; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:31; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:39; Acts 21:1–40","v":"By Cos, Rhodes and Patara Paul's company sailed to Tyre, where the disciples (NU the disciples, by the NKJV note) told him through the Spirit not to go up to Jerusalem; at Caesarea they (NU omits who were Paul's companions, by the NKJV note) stayed with Philip the evangelist, and Agabus bound himself with Paul's belt; they packed (made preparations, by the NKJV note) and went up to Jerusalem. James and the elders said the assembly must meet (NU What then is to be done? They will certainly hear, by the NKJV note) and bade Paul be purified with four men, reminding him of what they wrote to the Gentiles (NU omits that they should observe no such thing, except, by the NKJV note; or fornication, by the NKJV note); at the completion (by the NKJV note) of the days the Jews from Asia, who had previously (M omits previously, by the NKJV note) seen Trophimus with him, seized him in the temple, and the commander of the garrison (cohort, by the NKJV note) took him; asked whether he was the Egyptian, Paul said he was a Jew of Tarsus, a citizen of no mean (insignificant, by the NKJV note) city"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 22:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 22:20; NKJV notes, note on Acts 22:23; Acts 22:1–30","v":"In Hebrew Paul told the people of his upbringing at the feet of Gamaliel, his persecuting of the Way, the light near Damascus, which those with him saw (NU omits and were afraid, by the NKJV note), Ananias's word, and the Lord's word in a trance in the temple sending him to the Gentiles, for he had stood by consenting to Stephen's death (NU omits to his death, by the NKJV note); at that word they cried out and tore off (lit. threw, by the NKJV note) their clothes, and the commander would have him examined under scourging until Paul said he was a Roman, born a citizen"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 23:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 23:11; NKJV notes, note on Acts 23:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 23:30; NKJV notes, note on Acts 23:35; Acts 23:1–35","v":"Struck at the high priest Ananias's command, Paul called him a whitewashed wall; crying that he was a Pharisee judged for the resurrection, he divided the council, and the scribes of the Pharisees found no evil in him (NU what if a spirit or an angel has spoken to him, by the NKJV note). The Lord bade him be of good cheer (take courage, by the NKJV note), for he must witness at Rome. More than forty bound themselves to kill him when he was brought down tomorrow (NU omits tomorrow, by the NKJV note), but his sister's son told the commander, who sent him by night to Felix, writing that the Jews lay in wait (NU there would be a plot, by the NKJV note), and Felix kept him in Herod's Praetorium (headquarters, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 24:2; NKJV notes, note on Acts 24:4; NKJV notes, note on Acts 24:6; NKJV notes, note on Acts 24:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 24:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 24:20; NKJV notes, note on Acts 24:26; Acts 24:1–27","v":"After five days Tertullus praised Felix for peace and prosperity (or reforms, by the NKJV note), begged his courtesy (graciousness, by the NKJV note), and accused Paul of dissension and of profaning the temple (NU ends the sentence there, omitting the rest of v. 6, all of v. 7 and the first clause of v. 8, by the NKJV note), and the Jews assented (NU, M joined the attack, by the NKJV note). Paul answered that he worshiped the God of his fathers by the Way, hoping in the resurrection of the dead (NU omits of the dead, by the NKJV note), and asked what wrong they found (NU, M what wrongdoing they found, by the NKJV note); Felix put them off, heard Paul with Drusilla, hoped for money to release him (NU omits that he might release him, by the NKJV note), and after two years left him bound"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 25:2; NKJV notes, note on Acts 25:16; NKJV notes, note on Acts 25:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 25:23; Acts 25:1–27","v":"Three days after coming to the province Festus went up to Jerusalem, where the high priest (NU chief priests, by the NKJV note) and the chief men asked him to summon Paul, meaning to ambush him; at Caesarea the Jews laid complaints they could not prove, and Paul, asked to be judged at Jerusalem, appealed to Caesar. King Agrippa and Bernice came to greet Festus, who told the king that the Romans deliver no man to destruction (NU omits to destruction, although it is implied, by the NKJV note) unheard, and that the accusers brought no charge such as he supposed (suspected, by the NKJV note), but questions about a certain Jesus whom Paul affirmed to be alive; the next day Agrippa and Bernice came with great pomp (pageantry, by the NKJV note), and Festus said he had nothing certain to write of Paul"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 26:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 26:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 26:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 26:25; Acts 26:1–32","v":"Permitted to speak, Paul told Agrippa he had lived a Pharisee and was judged for the hope of the promise to the fathers; he had done many things contrary to (against, by the NKJV note) the name of Jesus of Nazareth, until on the road to Damascus a light brighter than the sun shone around him and Jesus spoke to him in Hebrew, sending him to the Gentiles, from whom and from the Jewish people He would deliver (rescue, by the NKJV note) him, to whom He now sent him (NU, M omit now, by the NKJV note), that they might be sanctified (set apart, by the NKJV note) by faith in Him; he had preached repentance to Jew and Gentile, saying only what the prophets and Moses foretold. Festus said he was mad, and Paul answered that he was not (out of my mind, by the NKJV note); Agrippa said Paul almost persuaded him to become a Christian, and might have been set free had he not appealed to Caesar"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:14; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:16; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:22; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:29; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:40; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:41; Acts 27:1–44","v":"Delivered with other prisoners to Julius, Paul sailed by Sidon and Myra to Fair Havens on Crete, where, the Fast being over (the Day of Atonement, late September or early October, by the NKJV note), he warned of disaster; making for Phoenix they were caught by Euroclydon (a southeast wind that stirs up broad waves; NU Euraquilon, a northeaster, by the NKJV note) and let the ship drive (be driven, by the NKJV note), and under Clauda (NU Cauda, by the NKJV note), fearing the Syrtis Sands (M Syrtes, by the NKJV note), they struck sail. When hope was gone Paul bade them take heart (courage, by the NKJV note), for an angel had told him none would be lost; on the fourteenth night they dropped anchors and prayed (or wished, by the NKJV note) for day, and after Paul had them eat, they let go (cast off, by the NKJV note) the anchors and ran aground where two seas met (a reef, by the NKJV note), and all two hundred and seventy-six escaped to land"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 28:2; NKJV notes, note on Acts 28:7; NKJV notes, note on Acts 28:11; NKJV notes, note on Acts 28:19; NKJV notes, note on Acts 28:25; NKJV notes, note on Acts 28:29; Acts 28:1–31","v":"On Malta the natives (lit. barbarians, by the NKJV note) showed kindness, a viper fastened on Paul's hand without harm, and Publius, the leading citizen (magistrate, by the NKJV note), received them, and Paul healed his father and the sick of the island. After three months they sailed in an Alexandrian ship whose figurehead was the Twin Brothers (Gr. Dioskouroi, Zeus's sons Castor and Pollux, by the NKJV note) by Syracuse, Rhegium and Puteoli to Rome, where Paul dwelt with a soldier; he told the leaders of the Jews that when the Jews (the ruling authorities, by the NKJV note) spoke against his release he appealed to Caesar, and when some disbelieved he said the Holy Spirit spoke rightly through Isaiah to their fathers (NU your fathers, by the NKJV note), and they departed disputing (NU omits v. 29, by the NKJV note); he dwelt two whole years in his rented house, preaching the kingdom of God with no one forbidding him"},{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 1:1","v":"The former account I made"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:2; Acts 1:1–2","v":"It is written to Theophilus, after a former account of all that Jesus began both to do and to teach until the day He was taken up (He ascended into heaven, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"feature","r":"Acts 16:10–17; 20:5–21:18; 27:1–28:16","v":"From Troas on its writer tells part of the journeys as one of Paul's company, saying we"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00647":{"k":"object","n":"The letter of the apostles and elders to the Gentile brethren","d":"The letter the apostles, elders and brethren at Jerusalem wrote to the Gentile brethren in Antioch, Syria and Cilicia, laying on them no greater burden than four necessary things (Acts 15:23–29)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:24; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:25; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:29; Acts 15:23–29","v":"The apostles, elders and brethren greet the Gentile brethren in Antioch, Syria and Cilicia: some who went out from them troubled them, saying they must be circumcised and keep the law (NU omits it, by the NKJV note), without commandment; with one accord (purpose or mind, by the NKJV note) they send chosen men with Barnabas and Paul, Judas and Silas, for it seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to them to lay on them no greater burden than to abstain from things offered to idols, from blood, from things strangled and from sexual immorality (or fornication, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 15:23","v":"this letter"},{"p":"feature","r":"Acts 16:4","v":"Its decrees were delivered to the churches through the cities"},{"p":"made by","r":"Acts 15:22–23","e":"GRP-01049"},{"p":"made by","r":"Acts 15:22–23","e":"GRP-01199"},{"p":"occasion","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:25; Acts 15:22–29; 21:25","e":"EVT-03154"}],"nm":{"EVT-03154":"The apostles and elders send their letter with Judas and Silas","GRP-01049":"The twelve apostles","GRP-01199":"The elders of the church at Jerusalem"}},"OBJ-00648":{"k":"object","n":"The altar to the unknown God at Athens","d":"An altar at Athens with the inscription TO THE UNKNOWN GOD, found by Paul among the objects of their worship (Acts 17:23)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Acts 17:23","v":"The inscription: To the unknown God"},{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 17:23","v":"an altar"},{"p":"placed at","r":"Acts 17:23","e":"PLC-01586"}],"nm":{"PLC-01586":"Athens"}},"OBJ-00649":{"k":"object","n":"The books of magic burned at Ephesus","d":"The books of those who had practiced magic at Ephesus, brought together and burned in the sight of all (Acts 19:19)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 19:19","v":"their books"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Acts 19:19","e":"EVT-03192"}],"nm":{"EVT-03192":"Those who practiced magic burn their books at Ephesus"}},"OBJ-00650":{"k":"object","n":"The image of Diana that fell down from Zeus","d":"The image which fell down from Zeus, of which the city of the Ephesians was guardian (Acts 19:35)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:35; Acts 19:35","v":"the image which fell down from Zeus"},{"p":"owner","r":"Acts 19:35","e":"DEI-00034","b":"inferred","c":"probable","w":"The city clerk named the image with the temple of Diana, of which Ephesus was guardian (Acts 19:35)."},{"p":"placed at","r":"Acts 19:35","e":"PLC-01594"}],"nm":{"DEI-00034":"Diana","PLC-01594":"Ephesus"}},"OBJ-00651":{"k":"object","n":"The letter of Claudius Lysias to Felix","d":"The letter the commander wrote to Felix the governor when he sent Paul to Caesarea (Acts 23:25–30)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 23:30; Acts 23:26–30","v":"Claudius Lysias greets the most excellent governor Felix: this man, seized by the Jews and about to be killed, he rescued with the troops, having learned he was a Roman; brought before their council, he was accused of questions of their law but of nothing deserving death or chains; told that the Jews lay in wait for him (NU there would be a plot against the man, by the NKJV note), he sent him at once and bade his accusers state their charges before Felix"},{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 23:25","v":"a letter"},{"p":"made by","r":"Acts 23:25–26","e":"PER-03273"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Acts 23:25–33","e":"EVT-03217"}],"nm":{"EVT-03217":"Paul is sent by night to Felix at Caesarea","PER-03273":"The commander of the garrison at Jerusalem"}},"OBJ-00652":{"k":"object","n":"The ship of Adramyttium","d":"The ship of Adramyttium in which Paul put to sea for Italy, meaning to sail along the coasts of Asia, as far as Myra (Acts 27:2–6)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 27:2","v":"a ship of Adramyttium"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00653":{"k":"object","n":"The Alexandrian ship that was wrecked at Malta","d":"The Alexandrian ship sailing to Italy which the centurion found at Myra, driven by the tempest and broken up off Malta (Acts 27:6–44)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 27:6","v":"an Alexandrian ship"},{"p":"owner","r":"Acts 27:11","e":"PER-03290"}],"nm":{"PER-03290":"The owner of the Alexandrian ship"}},"OBJ-00654":{"k":"object","n":"The skiff of the Alexandrian ship","d":"The ship's skiff, secured under Clauda, by which the sailors sought to escape until the soldiers cut it away (Acts 27:16–32)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 27:16, 30, 32","v":"the skiff"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00655":{"k":"object","n":"The wind Euroclydon","d":"The tempestuous head wind that caught the ship off Crete and drove it (Acts 27:14–15)","f":[{"p":"name","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:14; Acts 27:14","v":"Euroclydon"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00656":{"k":"object","n":"The viper that fastened on Paul's hand","d":"The viper that came out of the sticks Paul laid on the fire on Malta and fastened on his hand (Acts 28:3–5)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 28:3","v":"a viper"},{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 28:4–5","v":"the creature"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00657":{"k":"object","n":"The Alexandrian ship of the Twin Brothers","d":"The Alexandrian ship which had wintered at Malta, whose figurehead was the Twin Brothers, in which Paul sailed from Malta (Acts 28:11)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 28:11","v":"an Alexandrian ship"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 28:11; Acts 28:11","v":"Its figurehead was the Twin Brothers (Gr. Dioskouroi, Zeus's sons Castor and Pollux, by the NKJV note), and it had wintered at the island"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00658":{"k":"object","n":"The Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Romans","d":"The letter Paul wrote to all in Rome beloved of God and called to be saints, of the gospel of God's righteousness through faith for Jew and Gentile, and of his wish to come to them (Romans 1:1–16:27)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 1:16; NKJV notes, note on Romans 1:18; NKJV notes, note on Romans 1:29; NKJV notes, note on Romans 1:31; Romans 1:1–32","v":"Paul, called to be an apostle and set apart to the gospel God promised through His prophets, greeted all in Rome called to be saints; he thanked God for their faith and longed to come to them, having often been hindered, for he was not ashamed of the gospel (NU omits of Christ, by the NKJV note), God's power to save all who believe, in which His righteousness is revealed from faith to faith. God's wrath is shown against men who suppress (hold down, by the NKJV note) the truth: knowing God, they did not glorify Him or give thanks, and He gave them up to uncleanness, vile passions and a debased mind, full of every wrong (NU omits sexual immorality and unforgiving, by the NKJV notes)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 2:5; NKJV notes, note on Romans 2:9; NKJV notes, note on Romans 2:29; Romans 2:1–29","v":"Whoever judges another while doing the same has no excuse and stores up (by the NKJV note) wrath for the day of God's righteous judgment, when He will repay each by his works without partiality, the Jew first and also the Greek (Gentile, by the NKJV note). Gentiles who by nature do what the law asks show it written on their hearts; a Jew who boasts in the law and breaks it dishonors God, and the true Jew is one inwardly, circumcised in heart, whose praise is from God (Jew being literally praise, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 3:2; NKJV notes, note on Romans 3:22; NKJV notes, note on Romans 3:24; NKJV notes, note on Romans 3:25; NKJV notes, note on Romans 3:28; Romans 3:1–31","v":"The Jews' advantage is that God's oracles (sayings, Scriptures, by the NKJV note) were entrusted to them, and their unbelief cannot undo His faithfulness; yet Jews and Greeks alike are under sin, as the Scriptures say, and no one is justified by the works of the law. Now God's righteousness is shown apart from the law, through faith in Jesus Christ, to all who believe (NU omits and on all, by the NKJV note), all being justified freely (without any cost, by the NKJV note) by His grace through the redemption in Christ Jesus, whom God set forth as a propitiation (mercy seat, by the NKJV note); boasting is shut out, a man is justified (declared righteous, by the NKJV note) by faith apart from the deeds of the law, and the law is upheld"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 4:3; Romans 4:1–25","v":"Abraham was not justified by works: he believed God, and it was counted (imputed, credited, reckoned, counted, by the NKJV note) to him for righteousness, as David too speaks of the blessedness of the man whose sins are forgiven. It was counted while he was uncircumcised, and circumcision was the seal of that faith, so that he is the father of all who believe; the promise came through faith and not through the law, and at about a hundred years old he did not waver but was sure God could do what He had promised, as righteousness will be counted to those who believe in Him who raised Jesus"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 5:1; NKJV notes, note on Romans 5:3; NKJV notes, note on Romans 5:6; Romans 5:1–21","v":"Justified by faith, we have (some ancient manuscripts let us have, by the NKJV note) peace with God through Jesus Christ and rejoice in hope, tribulation working perseverance (endurance, by the NKJV note), character and hope; Christ died for the ungodly at the right time (by the NKJV note), and God shows His love in that Christ died for us while we were sinners. As sin and death came into the world through one man, and death reigned from Adam to Moses, so the gift of grace and righteousness abounds through the one Man, Jesus Christ: one man's disobedience made many sinners, one Man's obedience will make many righteous, and where sin abounded grace abounded much more"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 6:11; NKJV notes, note on Romans 6:23; Romans 6:1–23","v":"Those baptized into Christ Jesus were baptized into His death and buried with Him, to walk in newness of life as He was raised; their old man was crucified with Him so that they need serve sin no longer, and they are to count themselves (consider, by the NKJV note) dead to sin and alive to God, under grace and not under law. Set free from sin, they have become slaves of righteousness and of God; sin pays its wage in death, while God's free gift (by the NKJV note) is eternal life in Christ Jesus"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 7:1; Romans 7:1–25","v":"The law rules (by the NKJV note) a man only while he lives, and as a wife is free when her husband dies, so believers have died to the law through the body of Christ, to belong to Him who was raised and to serve in the newness of the Spirit. The law is not sin but holy, and the commandment holy, just and good; yet sin seized its chance in the commandment, deceived and killed, and Paul found another law in his members at war with the law of his mind, cried out for one to rescue him from this deadly body, and gave thanks to God through Jesus Christ"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 8:1; NKJV notes, note on Romans 8:6; NKJV notes, note on Romans 8:7; NKJV notes, note on Romans 8:15; NKJV notes, note on Romans 8:21; NKJV notes, note on Romans 8:26; Romans 8:1–39","v":"There is now no condemnation for those in Christ Jesus (NU omits the rest of the verse, by the NKJV note), for the law of the Spirit of life has freed them from the law of sin and death; God sent His Son in the likeness of sinful flesh and condemned sin in the flesh. The fleshly (by the NKJV notes) mind is at enmity with God, but those led by the Spirit of God are His sons, crying Abba (Father, in Aramaic, by the NKJV note), heirs of God with Christ. The creation, made subject to futility, waits to be freed from its bondage to corruption (decay, by the NKJV note); the Spirit intercedes for the saints (NU omits for us, by the NKJV note); everything turns to good for those who love God and are called by His purpose, and nothing can part them from His love in Christ Jesus"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 9:3; NKJV notes, note on Romans 9:28; NKJV notes, note on Romans 9:29; NKJV notes, note on Romans 9:32; Romans 9:1–33","v":"Paul grieves for his countrymen (or relatives, by the NKJV note), the Israelites, to whom the covenants and promises belong and from whom Christ came; yet God's word has not failed, for not all who are of Israel are Israel: the children of the promise are counted as the seed, through Isaac, and before the twins were born it was said that the older would serve the younger. God has mercy on whom He wills, as He told Moses, and raised up Pharaoh to show His power; as the potter has power over the clay, He has called vessels of mercy from Jews and Gentiles, as Hosea and Isaiah said, a remnant to be saved (NU the Lord will finish the work and cut it short upon the earth, by the NKJV note), left by the Lord of Sabaoth (Hosts, in Hebrew, by the NKJV note). Gentiles gained the righteousness of faith, while Israel, seeking it by works (NU by works, omitting of the law, by the NKJV note), stumbled at the stone laid in Zion"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 10:1; NKJV notes, note on Romans 10:15; Romans 10:1–21","v":"Paul prays that Israel (NU them, by the NKJV note) may be saved: zealous for God but without knowledge, they sought a righteousness of their own, but Christ ends the law as a way to righteousness for all who believe. The word of faith is near: whoever confesses Jesus as Lord and believes that God raised Him from the dead will be saved, with no distinction between Jew and Greek. Yet how can they call without hearing, or hear without a preacher who is sent (NU omits part of the words on the feet of those who preach, by the NKJV note)? Faith comes by hearing the word of God, and Moses and Isaiah said that God would be found by a nation that did not seek Him, while He held out His hands all day to a disobedient people"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 11:6; NKJV notes, note on Romans 11:11; NKJV notes, note on Romans 11:26; NKJV notes, note on Romans 11:32; Romans 11:1–36","v":"God has not cast away His people: Paul himself is an Israelite of the tribe of Benjamin, and as in Elijah's day God kept seven thousand, so now there is a remnant chosen by grace (NU omits the rest of v. 6, by the NKJV note), the rest being blinded. Through Israel's fall (trespass, by the NKJV note) salvation came to the Gentiles; the wild branches grafted into the olive tree are not to boast, for God can graft the natural branches in again. Blindness in part has come on Israel until the fullness of the Gentiles comes in, and so all Israel will be saved (or delivered, by the NKJV note), for God's gifts and calling cannot be revoked, and He has shut all up in (by the NKJV note) disobedience to have mercy on all; to Him be glory forever"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 12:1; Romans 12:1–21","v":"Paul urges (by the NKJV note) the brethren to offer their bodies to God as a living sacrifice, their rational (by the NKJV note) service, and to be changed by the renewing of their mind; each is to think soberly of himself and use the gift he has been given, for they are many members of one body in Christ. Love is to be without hypocrisy, warm and humble; they are to bless those who persecute them, repay no one evil for evil, leave vengeance to the Lord, feed a hungry enemy and overcome evil with good"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 13:9; NKJV notes, note on Romans 13:13; Romans 13:1–14","v":"Every soul is to be subject to the governing authorities, for all authority is from God, and rulers are His ministers for good who bear the sword against evil; so they pay taxes and give each his due. They are to owe nothing but love, for the commandments (NU omits the one against false witness, by the NKJV note) are summed up in loving one's neighbor as oneself; knowing the time, they are to wake from sleep, cast off the works of darkness, walk decently (by the NKJV note) as in the day and put on the Lord Jesus Christ"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 14:9; NKJV notes, note on Romans 14:10; NKJV notes, note on Romans 14:19; NKJV notes, note on Romans 14:23; Romans 14:1–23","v":"They are to receive the one weak in faith without disputes: the one who eats all things and the one who eats only vegetables, the one who keeps a day and the one who does not, each lives and dies to the Lord, for Christ died and rose (NU omits and rose, by the NKJV note) to be Lord of the dead and the living, and all will stand before His judgment seat (NU God's, by the NKJV note). They are to put no stumbling block in a brother's way but pursue peace and what builds up (by the NKJV note), for the kingdom of God is righteousness, peace and joy in the Holy Spirit, and whatever is not from faith is sin (M places 16:25–27 here, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 15:1; NKJV notes, note on Romans 15:8; NKJV notes, note on Romans 15:24; NKJV notes, note on Romans 15:31; Romans 15:1–33","v":"The strong are to bear with the weaknesses (by the NKJV note) of the weak and please their neighbor, as Christ did not please Himself; the Scriptures were written for our learning, and Paul prays the God of patience and comfort to make them of one mind. Christ became a servant (minister, by the NKJV note) to the circumcision to confirm the promises to the fathers, and that the Gentiles might glorify God, as the Scriptures and Isaiah say. Paul wrote boldly as a minister of Jesus Christ to the Gentiles, having fully preached the gospel from Jerusalem round about to Illyricum where Christ was not named; often hindered from coming to Rome, he hoped to see them on his way to Spain (NU omits I shall come to you, by the NKJV note), once he had taken the contribution of Macedonia and Achaia to the poor among the saints at Jerusalem, and asked their prayers that he be delivered from the unbelievers (the disobedient, by the NKJV note) in Judea"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 16:5; NKJV notes, note on Romans 16:11; NKJV notes, note on Romans 16:16; NKJV notes, note on Romans 16:24; NKJV notes, note on Romans 16:25; Romans 16:1–27","v":"Paul commended Phoebe, a servant of the church in Cenchrea, and greeted Priscilla and Aquila, who had risked their necks for him, and the church in their house; Epaenetus, the firstfruits of Achaia (NU Asia, by the NKJV note); Mary; Andronicus and Junia, his countrymen and fellow prisoners; Amplias, Urbanus, Stachys, Apelles, the households of Aristobulus and Narcissus, Herodion his countryman (or relative, by the NKJV note), Tryphena, Tryphosa, Persis, Rufus and his mother, Asyncritus, Phlegon, Hermas, Patrobas, Hermes, Philologus, Julia, Nereus and his sister, Olympas and the saints with them, adding the greeting of the churches of Christ (NU all the churches, by the NKJV note). They were to avoid those who cause divisions, and the God of peace would soon crush Satan under their feet. Timothy, Lucius, Jason, Sosipater, Tertius who wrote the letter, Gaius his host, Erastus the city's treasurer and Quartus sent greetings (NU omits v. 24, by the NKJV note), and the letter closes in praise to God, alone wise, through Jesus Christ (M places 16:25–27 after 14:23, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"designation","r":"Romans 16:22","v":"this epistle"},{"p":"feature","r":"Romans 1:1–7","v":"It is a letter from Paul to all in Rome beloved of God and called to be saints, opening with grace and peace from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 14:23; NKJV notes, note on Romans 16:24; NKJV notes, note on Romans 16:25; Romans 14:23; 16:24–27","v":"By the NKJV notes, the NU text omits 16:24, and the M text places the closing praise (16:25–27) after 14:23"},{"p":"made by","r":"Romans 1:1; 15:15","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Romans 1:1–7; 15:15","e":"EVT-03254"},{"p":"purpose","r":"Romans 15:15–16","v":"Paul wrote more boldly on some points to remind them, by the grace given him to be a minister of Jesus Christ to the Gentiles"}],"nm":{"EVT-03254":"Paul writes to the saints at Rome","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00659":{"k":"object","n":"The First Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Corinthians","d":"The letter Paul and Sosthenes wrote to the church of God at Corinth, of its contentions, the message of the cross, the matters the Corinthians had written about, the Lord's Supper, spiritual gifts, love and the resurrection of the dead (1 Corinthians 1:1–16:24)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 1:2; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 1:11; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 1:18; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 1:23; 1 Corinthians 1:1–31","v":"Paul, called to be an apostle, and Sosthenes greeted the church of God at Corinth, sanctified (set apart, by the NKJV note) in Christ Jesus, and thanked God for their gifts; but Chloe's household had told him of contentions (quarrels, by the NKJV note), each saying he was of Paul, Apollos, Cephas or Christ. Paul had baptized only Crispus, Gaius and the household of Stephanas, for Christ sent him to preach; the message (word, by the NKJV note) of the cross is foolishness to those perishing but God's power to the saved, Christ crucified a stumbling block (by the NKJV note) to Jews and foolishness to Greeks (NU Gentiles, by the NKJV note), and God chose the foolish and weak things of the world so that none should glory but in the Lord"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 2:7; 1 Corinthians 2:1–16","v":"Paul came to them not with excellent speech but resolved to know nothing among them but Christ crucified, preaching in demonstration of the Spirit and of power; he speaks God's hidden wisdom, ordained (predetermined, by the NKJV note) before the ages, which the rulers of this age did not know, or they would not have crucified the Lord of glory, but which God has revealed by His Spirit, who searches His deep things; the natural man does not receive them, but we have the mind of Christ"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 3:3; 1 Corinthians 3:1–23","v":"Still carnal (walking according to man, by the NKJV note), they quarrel over Paul and Apollos, who are only ministers: Paul planted, Apollos watered, and God gave the increase; Paul laid the foundation, which is Jesus Christ, and each one's work on it will be tested by fire. They are God's temple, where His Spirit dwells; the wisdom of this world is foolishness with God, so none is to boast in men, for all things are theirs, and they are Christ's"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 4:5; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 4:9; 1 Corinthians 4:1–21","v":"Paul and Apollos are servants of Christ and stewards of God's mysteries, judged by the Lord, who will bring to light the counsels (motives, by the NKJV note) of the hearts; the apostles are made a spectacle (theater, by the NKJV note) to the world, hungry, beaten and homeless, working with their own hands. As their father in Christ Jesus through the gospel, Paul urges them to imitate him, has sent Timothy to remind them of his ways, and will come shortly, with a rod or in love"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 5:1; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 5:2; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 5:7; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 5:9; 1 Corinthians 5:1–13","v":"It is reported that a man among them has his father's wife, an immorality not even named (NU omits named, by the NKJV note) among the Gentiles, and they are puffed up (arrogant, by the NKJV note) instead of mourning; Paul, absent in body, has judged him, and bids them deliver him to Satan for the destruction of the flesh and purge out (clean out, by the NKJV note) the old leaven, for Christ our Passover was sacrificed. He had written before not to keep company (associate, by the NKJV note) with the sexually immoral, meaning not those of the world but anyone called a brother who is so"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 6:11; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 6:20; 1 Corinthians 6:1–20","v":"Brother is not to go to law against brother before unbelievers, for the saints will judge the world and angels; the unrighteous will not inherit the kingdom of God, and such were some of them, but they were washed, sanctified (set apart, by the NKJV note) and justified. The body is for the Lord, who will raise it, a member of Christ and the temple of the Holy Spirit; they are to flee sexual immorality, for they were bought at a price, and glorify God in their body (NU omits the rest of v. 20, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 7:17; 1 Corinthians 7:1–40","v":"Answering what they wrote to him, Paul says each man should have his own wife and each woman her own husband, giving each other their due; he gives the Lord's command that a wife not depart from her husband nor a husband divorce his wife, and his own word that a believer not leave an unbelieving spouse willing to stay. Each is to remain in the calling in which he was called, as he directs (by the NKJV note) in all the churches; for the present distress it is good to stay as one is, though marrying is no sin, and a widow may marry whom she wishes, only in the Lord"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 8:1; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 8:9; 1 Corinthians 8:1–13","v":"Concerning things offered to idols: knowledge puffs up (makes arrogant, by the NKJV note) but love builds up (by the NKJV note); an idol is nothing, and for us there is one God, the Father, and one Lord Jesus Christ. Yet not everyone has that knowledge, and their liberty must not become a stumbling block (cause of offense, by the NKJV note) to the weak brother for whom Christ died; if food makes his brother stumble, Paul will never eat meat"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 9:2; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 9:5; 1 Corinthians 9:1–27","v":"Paul is an apostle who has seen Jesus Christ, and the Corinthians are the seal (certification, by the NKJV note) of it; he has the right the other apostles, the brothers of the Lord and Cephas have, to eat and drink and to take along a believing wife (a sister, a wife, by the NKJV note), as the law of Moses says the ox is not to be muzzled and the Lord commanded that those who preach the gospel live from it. But he has used none of it, preaching without charge; free from all, he became a servant to all, all things to all men, that he might save some, and he disciplines his body like a runner for an imperishable crown"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 10:9; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 10:11; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 10:16; 1 Corinthians 10:1–33","v":"The fathers were all under the cloud, passed through the sea, were baptized into Moses, and ate and drank spiritual food and drink from the Rock that followed them, which was Christ; yet with most of them God was not pleased, and they fell in the wilderness for their lust, idolatry, immorality, testing (by the NKJV note) and complaining, examples written for our instruction (by the NKJV note). God is faithful and makes the way of escape; they are to flee idolatry, for the cup of blessing is the communion (fellowship or sharing, by the NKJV note) of Christ's blood and they cannot share the Lord's table and the table of demons; they may eat what is sold in the market, but do all to the glory of God and give no offense"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 11:22; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 11:30; 1 Corinthians 11:1–34","v":"They are to imitate Paul as he imitates Christ and keep the traditions he delivered: the head of every man is Christ, the head of woman is man and the head of Christ is God, so a man prays with head uncovered and a woman covered. When they come together there are divisions, and it is not the Lord's Supper they eat, one hungry and another drunk, shaming the poor (by the NKJV note); Paul delivers what he received from the Lord of the bread and the cup on the night He was betrayed, and whoever eats unworthily eats judgment to himself, so that many among them are weak and sick and many sleep (are dead, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 12:3; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 12:4; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 12:31; 1 Corinthians 12:1–31","v":"No one says Jesus is Lord but by the Holy Spirit, and none speaking by the Spirit calls Him accursed (Gr. anathema, by the NKJV note); there are diversities (allotments or various kinds, by the NKJV note) of gifts, ministries and activities, but one Spirit, one Lord and one God, the Spirit giving to each as He wills. By one Spirit all were baptized into one body, many members needing one another, and they are the body of Christ, in which God appointed apostles, prophets, teachers and the rest; they are to desire the best (NU greater, by the NKJV note) gifts, and yet there is a more excellent way"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 13:4; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 13:5; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 13:10; 1 Corinthians 13:1–13","v":"Tongues, prophecy, knowledge, faith and giving are nothing without love; love is patient and kind, not puffed up (arrogant, by the NKJV note), keeping no account of evil (by the NKJV note), bearing and enduring all things, and it never fails, while prophecies and tongues will cease when that which is perfect (complete, by the NKJV note) has come; faith, hope and love abide, and the greatest is love"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 14:12; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 14:33; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 14:34; 1 Corinthians 14:1–40","v":"They are to pursue love and desire to prophesy, for prophecy builds up (by the NKJV note) the church, while a tongue without interpretation builds up only the speaker; Paul speaks with tongues more than all, yet in church would rather speak five words with understanding. Tongues are a sign to unbelievers and prophecy to believers; speakers in tongues are to be two or three, in turn, with one to interpret, and the prophets two or three, for God is not the author of confusion (disorder, by the NKJV note) but of peace; their (NU omits your, by the NKJV note) women are to keep silent in the churches, and all is to be done decently and in order"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 15:5; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 15:20; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 15:51; 1 Corinthians 15:1–58","v":"Paul delivered what he received: Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures, was buried and rose the third day, and was seen by Cephas (Peter, by the NKJV note), then by the twelve, by over five hundred brethren at once, by James, by all the apostles, and last by Paul, the least of the apostles. If the dead do not rise, Christ is not risen and their faith is futile; but He is risen, the firstfruits of those who have died (by the NKJV note), and as in Adam all die so in Christ all will be made alive; at the end He will deliver the kingdom to God the Father, death the last enemy destroyed. The body is sown perishable and raised incorruptible, and in a mystery (hidden truth, by the NKJV note) all will be changed at the last trumpet, death swallowed up in victory"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 16:22; 1 Corinthians 16:1–24","v":"On the first day of the week each is to lay aside for the collection for the saints, as Paul ordered the churches of Galatia, to be carried to Jerusalem by those they approve. Paul will come through Macedonia and perhaps winter with them, staying at Ephesus until Pentecost, where a great door has opened and there are many adversaries; Timothy is to be received without fear, and Apollos will come when he can. They are to submit to such as the household of Stephanas, whose coming with Fortunatus and Achaicus refreshed Paul; the churches of Asia, Aquila and Priscilla greet them, Paul greets them with his own hand, and whoever does not love the Lord is accursed (Gr. anathema, by the NKJV note); O Lord, come"},{"p":"designation","r":"1 Corinthians 1:1","v":"Paul, called to be an apostle"},{"p":"feature","r":"1 Corinthians 16:21","v":"Paul writes its last greeting with his own hand"},{"p":"made by","r":"1 Corinthians 1:1; 16:21","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"1 Corinthians 1:1–3","e":"EVT-03256"}],"nm":{"EVT-03256":"Paul writes his first letter to the church at Corinth","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00660":{"k":"object","n":"Paul's earlier letter to the Corinthians","d":"The letter Paul had written to the Corinthians before this one, not to keep company with the sexually immoral (1 Corinthians 5:9)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 5:9; 1 Corinthians 5:9–11","v":"Not to keep company (associate, by the NKJV note) with the sexually immoral, by which Paul meant not those of the world but anyone called a brother who was so"},{"p":"designation","r":"1 Corinthians 5:9","v":"my epistle"},{"p":"made by","r":"1 Corinthians 5:9","e":"PER-03213"}],"nm":{"PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00661":{"k":"object","n":"The Corinthians' letter to Paul","d":"The letter in which the Corinthians wrote to Paul of matters he answers, marriage first (1 Corinthians 7:1)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"1 Corinthians 7:1","v":"Matters on which they asked Paul, of which he answers marriage first"},{"p":"designation","r":"1 Corinthians 7:1","v":"the things of which you wrote to me"},{"p":"made by","r":"1 Corinthians 7:1","e":"GRP-01214"}],"nm":{"GRP-01214":"The Corinthians who believed"}},"OBJ-00662":{"k":"object","n":"The Second Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Corinthians","d":"The letter Paul and Timothy wrote to the church of God at Corinth and the saints in Achaia, of God's comfort in affliction, the ministry of the new covenant, reconciliation, the gift for the saints, and Paul's defense of his apostleship (2 Corinthians 1:1–13:14)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 1:4; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 1:10; 2 Corinthians 1:1–24","v":"Paul and Timothy greeted the church of God at Corinth with all the saints in Achaia, and blessed the God of all comfort, who comforts them in all their tribulation that they may comfort others in any trouble (tribulation, by the NKJV note). In Asia they were burdened beyond strength and despaired of life, but God, who raises the dead, delivered them and does (NU shall, by the NKJV note) deliver them. Paul had meant to come to them on the way to Macedonia and again on his return, and was not fickle, for all God's promises are Yes in Christ; he did not come again to Corinth, to spare them"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 2:4; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 2:12; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 2:17; 2 Corinthians 2:1–17","v":"Paul resolved not to come to them again in sorrow, and wrote instead out of much affliction (tribulation, by the NKJV note) and with many tears, to show his love and test their obedience; the one who caused grief has been punished enough by the majority, and they are to forgive and comfort him, lest Satan take advantage. At Troas, though the Lord opened a door (opportunity, by the NKJV note), Paul had no rest for not finding Titus, and went on to Macedonia; God leads them in triumph in Christ, a fragrance of life to some and of death to others, not peddling (adulterating for gain, by the NKJV note) the word of God"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 3:6; 2 Corinthians 3:1–18","v":"The Corinthians are Christ's epistle, written by the Spirit of the living God on tablets of the heart, not of stone. God made Paul fit to serve the new covenant, which is of the Spirit (or spirit, by the NKJV note), who gives life, and not of the letter, which kills. If the ministry carved in stone came with such glory that Israel could not gaze at Moses' face, the ministry of the Spirit has far greater glory; a veil still lies on Israel's hearts when Moses is read, but in Christ it is taken away, and the Lord is the Spirit, who brings liberty"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 4:2; 2 Corinthians 4:1–18","v":"Having this ministry, Paul does not lose heart or handle the word of God deceitfully (adulterating it, by the NKJV note); the god of this age blinds unbelievers' minds, but God, who first called light out of darkness, has lit believers' hearts with the knowledge of His glory seen in Christ's face. They hold this treasure in earthen vessels, pressed on every side but never crushed or destroyed, carrying the dying of Jesus that His life may show in them; their outward man wastes away while the inward man is made new each day, and their brief trouble is gaining them lasting glory"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 5:1; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 5:5; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 5:19; 2 Corinthians 5:1–21","v":"If the earthly tent (the physical body, by the NKJV note) is taken down, believers have an eternal house from God in the heavens, and the Spirit is His guarantee (down payment, earnest, by the NKJV note) of it; they walk by faith, and all will stand before Christ's judgment seat. Christ's love drives them, for He died for all; whoever is in Christ is made new, and in Christ God was bringing the world back to Himself, not imputing (reckoning, by the NKJV note) its trespasses, and made the sinless One sin on our behalf; so Paul speaks as Christ's ambassador, urging them to be reconciled to God"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 6:4; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 6:16; 2 Corinthians 6:1–18","v":"Now is the day of salvation, and they are not to receive God's grace in vain; Paul proves himself God's minister in much endurance (by the NKJV note), through beatings, prisons, riots, toil, sleepless nights and fasts, dying yet alive, poor yet enriching many. His heart is open to them, and they are to open theirs; they are not to be yoked unequally with unbelievers, for light has no communion with darkness nor Christ any accord with Belial, and they are God's living temple (NU we, by the NKJV note), to leave the unbelievers and keep apart"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 7:7; 2 Corinthians 7:1–16","v":"Having these promises, they are to make themselves clean and holy, and open their hearts to Paul, who has wronged no one. When he came to Macedonia he had no rest, with conflicts outside and fears within, but God, the comforter of the downcast, cheered him when Titus arrived, and by the comfort (by the NKJV note) Titus had found among them. Paul's letter made them sorry, and he does not regret it, for their godly sorrow led to repentance and zeal; Titus was refreshed by them, who received him with fear and trembling"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 8:4; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 8:23; 2 Corinthians 8:1–24","v":"The churches of Macedonia, sorely tried and in deep poverty, gave beyond their means, begging to share in the ministering to the saints (NU, M omit that we would receive, by the NKJV note); Paul urged Titus to complete the same gift among the Corinthians, for Christ made Himself poor for their sakes. He gives advice, not command, that they finish what they began a year before, by an equality, as it is written that he who gathered much had nothing over and he who gathered little had no lack; Titus goes to them of his own accord with a brother famed in all the churches and another often proved diligent, messengers (apostles, sent ones, by the NKJV note) of the churches"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 9:5; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 9:7; 2 Corinthians 9:1–15","v":"Paul has boasted of them to the Macedonians, saying Achaia has been prepared since last year, and sends the brethren ahead so the gift is ready as a willing gift and not one wrung from them (covetousness, by the NKJV note), lest Macedonians come with him and find them unready. Sparing sowing brings a sparing harvest; each is to give what he has resolved, not under pressure (compulsion, by the NKJV note), for God loves a cheerful giver; He can give them every grace in abundance, and the service meets the saints' needs and overflows in thanks to God for His gift beyond words"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 10:4; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 10:8; 2 Corinthians 10:1–18","v":"Paul pleads with them by the meekness of Christ, lowly when present and bold when absent; the weapons he fights with are not of the flesh (by the NKJV note) but have God's power to tear down strongholds, and his authority from the Lord is for building up (by the NKJV note), not destruction. Some say his letters are weighty but his presence weak; he will be in deed what he is in his letters, and boasts only within the sphere God gave him, which reaches to them, hoping to preach in regions beyond, for the one approved is the one the Lord commends"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 11:3; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 11:7; 2 Corinthians 11:1–33","v":"Paul is jealous for them with godly jealousy, having betrothed them to Christ, and fears that as the serpent deceived Eve their minds may be led from the simplicity in Christ (NU adds and purity, by the NKJV note). He is not behind the most eminent apostles; he preached to them free of charge, putting himself down (by the NKJV note), while brethren from Macedonia met his needs. Such men are false apostles, as Satan disguises himself as an angel of light. Boasting as a fool, he names his labors, stripes, beatings, stoning, shipwrecks and perils, his care for all the churches, and his escape from Damascus in a basket when the governor under King Aretas held the city"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 12:7; 2 Corinthians 12:1–21","v":"Paul knows a man in Christ taken up fourteen years before to the third heaven, into Paradise, where he heard words no man may speak; to keep him from pride over the greatness of the revelations he was given a thorn in the flesh, Satan's messenger to beat (by the NKJV note) him, and when he asked three times for its removal the Lord answered that His grace was enough, for His power is perfected in weakness. The signs of an apostle were done among them; he is ready to come the third time and will not be a burden, though he fears he will find quarrels and unrepented sin"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 13:5; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 13:14; 2 Corinthians 13:1–14","v":"This will be his third coming, and every word is to be established by two or three witnesses; he will not spare those who sinned, since they seek proof of Christ speaking in him, who was crucified in weakness yet lives by God's power. They are to examine themselves whether they are in the faith, unless they fail the test (by the NKJV note); he writes while absent so that when present he need not use sharpness, and bids them farewell, wishing them Christ's grace, God's love and the communion (fellowship, by the NKJV note) of the Holy Spirit"},{"p":"designation","r":"2 Corinthians 1:1","v":"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ"},{"p":"made by","r":"2 Corinthians 1:1; 10:1","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"2 Corinthians 1:1–2","e":"EVT-03262"}],"nm":{"EVT-03262":"Paul writes his second letter to the church at Corinth","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00663":{"k":"object","n":"Paul's letter written with many tears","d":"The letter Paul wrote to the Corinthians out of much affliction and with many tears, to show his love and test their obedience (2 Corinthians 2:3–4, 9)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"2 Corinthians 2:4","v":"the letter written with many tears"},{"p":"designation","r":"2 Corinthians 7:8","v":"my letter","b":"inferred","c":"probable","w":"Paul writes that his letter made them sorry for a while and that he wrote to show his care for them (2 Corinthians 7:8–12), as he wrote the letter with many tears to show his love and test their obedience (2:3–4, 9)."},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 2:4; 2 Corinthians 2:4","v":"Paul wrote it out of much affliction (tribulation, by the NKJV note) and anguish of heart, with many tears"},{"p":"feature","r":"2 Corinthians 7:8–12","v":"It made them sorry for a while, and their godly sorrow led to repentance; Paul had regretted sending it but no longer did, for he wrote not for the sake of the one who did wrong or the one wronged, but that his care for them might appear","b":"inferred","c":"probable","w":"Paul writes of a letter that made them sorry (2 Corinthians 7:8–12), as he wrote earlier of the letter he sent with many tears (2:3–4, 9)."},{"p":"made by","r":"2 Corinthians 2:3–4, 9","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"purpose","r":"2 Corinthians 2:4, 9","v":"Not to grieve them but that they might know how much Paul loved them, and to test whether they were obedient in all things"}],"nm":{"PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00664":{"k":"object","n":"The Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Galatians","d":"The letter Paul, with all the brethren with him, wrote to the churches of Galatia, of the one gospel, which he received by the revelation of Jesus Christ, of justification by faith, not by works of the law, and of liberty in Christ and the walk in the Spirit (Galatians 1:1–6:18)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Galatians 1:7; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 1:8; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 1:18; Galatians 1:1–24","v":"Paul, an apostle sent not by men but by Jesus Christ and by God the Father, who raised Christ from the dead, with all the brethren with him, greeted the churches of Galatia. He marveled that they were turning so soon to a different gospel, though there is no other; some trouble them, wanting to pervert (distort, by the NKJV note) the gospel of Christ, and whoever preaches another gospel, even Paul or an angel from heaven, is to be accursed (Gr. anathema, by the NKJV note). The gospel Paul preached came not from man but through the revelation of Jesus Christ. In Judaism he had persecuted the church of God; but when God revealed His Son in him, he went to Arabia and back to Damascus, and after three years went up to Jerusalem to visit Peter (NU Cephas, by the NKJV note), staying fifteen days, and then into Syria and Cilicia; the churches of Judea, who did not know him by face, glorified God in him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:2; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:6; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:9; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:11; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:12; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:14; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:16; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:21; Galatians 2:1–21","v":"After fourteen years Paul went up to Jerusalem again by (because of, by the NKJV note) revelation, with Barnabas and Titus, and laid his gospel privately before those of reputation; Titus, a Greek, was not compelled to be circumcised, and the false brethren who crept in to spy on the liberty believers have in Christ were not yielded to for a single hour. God shows no partiality (lit. does not receive the face of a man, by the NKJV note); nothing was added to Paul by those who seemed to be something, but James, Cephas (Peter, by the NKJV note) and John gave the right hand of fellowship to him and Barnabas, the two to go to the Gentiles, the three to the circumcised, asking only that the two remember the poor. When Peter (NU Cephas, by the NKJV note) came to Antioch, Paul withstood (opposed, by the NKJV note) him to his face, for when men came from James he drew back from eating with Gentiles, fearing those of the circumcision (Jewish Christians, by the NKJV note), and the rest of the Jews and even Barnabas went with him. Paul asked him before them all why (NU how can you, by the NKJV note) he compelled Gentiles to live as Jews (some interpreters stop the quotation here, by the NKJV note): a man is justified (declared righteous, by the NKJV note) by faith in Christ, not by the law's works; Paul has been crucified with Christ, who lives in him, and if righteousness came through the law, Christ died in vain (for nothing, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Galatians 3:1; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 3:4; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 3:11; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 3:17; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 3:23; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 3:24; Galatians 3:1–29","v":"Paul asked the foolish Galatians who had bewitched them (NU omits that you should not obey the truth, by the NKJV note), before whose eyes Christ crucified was clearly portrayed (NU omits among you, by the NKJV note): had they received the Spirit through the law's works or through the hearing of faith, and had they suffered so many (or great, by the NKJV note) things in vain? As Abraham believed God and was counted righteous, only those of faith are his sons, and they are blessed with him; all who rest on the law's works are under its curse, and by the law no one is justified (declared righteous, by the NKJV note) before God, since the just shall live by faith. Christ redeemed us from the law's curse, becoming a curse for us, that Abraham's blessing might come on the Gentiles. A man's covenant, once confirmed, is not annulled or added to; the law, coming four hundred and thirty years after, cannot set aside the covenant God confirmed before (NU omits in Christ, by the NKJV note), and the promises were made to Abraham and his Seed, who is Christ. The law was added on account of transgressions, to stand until the Seed came, appointed through angels by a mediator. The law is not against God's promises; Scripture confined all under sin, so that those who believe might receive the promise that rests on faith in Jesus Christ. The law kept us under guard, kept (lit. confined, by the NKJV note) for the faith that would later be revealed, and was our tutor (in a household, the guardian responsible for the care and discipline of the children, by the NKJV note) to lead us to Christ. Now the Galatians are all God's sons through faith in Christ Jesus; the baptized have put on Christ, and all are one in Him, neither Jew nor Greek, slave nor free, male nor female, and those who are Christ's are Abraham's seed and heirs by promise"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Galatians 4:4; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 4:6; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 4:7; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 4:14; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 4:15; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 4:24; Galatians 4:1–31","v":"An heir, while a child, is no different from a slave, though master of all, but is placed under guardians and stewards till the time his father appointed; so we, as children, were held in bondage under the world's elements. When the fullness of time came, God sent His Son, born (or made, by the NKJV note) of a woman and under the law, so as to redeem those under the law, and so that we might receive adoption as sons; and because the Galatians are sons, God sent into their hearts the Spirit of His Son, crying out, 'Abba (lit., in Aram., Father, by the NKJV note), Father!' So each of them is now a son and not a slave, and, being a son, an heir (NU through God, by the NKJV note) of God through Christ (NU omits through Christ, by the NKJV note). Once, not knowing God, the Galatians served what by nature are not gods; Paul asked how it was that now, knowing God, or rather known by Him, they were turning again to those weak and beggarly elements, wishing to be in bondage again; they were observing days, months, seasons and years. Paul feared he had labored for them in vain. He urged the brethren to become as he was, for he had become as they were; they had not wronged him. Because of a physical infirmity he first preached the gospel to them, and they did not despise or reject his trial in the flesh but received him as an angel (or messenger, by the NKJV note) of God, as they would Christ Jesus. Paul asked what (NU where, by the NKJV note) was the blessing they had enjoyed, for if possible they would have plucked out their own eyes to give to him; and he asked whether he had become their enemy by telling them the truth. Others were courting them eagerly, but not for good, wishing to shut them out so that they would be zealous for those others; zeal in a good thing is good at all times, not only when Paul was with them. He travailed again, as in birth, for his little children until Christ should take form in them, and wished he were with them now to change his tone, for he had doubts about them. Paul asked those who wished to be under the law whether they did not hear the law: Abraham had two sons, one by a bondwoman, born according to the flesh, the other by a freewoman, through promise; these things are symbolic, for the women are the (NU, M omit the, by the NKJV note) two covenants. The covenant from Mount Sinai, giving birth to bondage, is Hagar, who is Mount Sinai in Arabia, answering to the present Jerusalem, in bondage with her children; while the Jerusalem above is free, and she is mother to us all; it is written that the barren one is to rejoice, since the desolate one has many more children than the one with a husband. The brethren, like Isaac, are children of promise; and as the son born according to the flesh persecuted the one born according to the Spirit, so it is now. But Scripture says the bondwoman and her son are to be cast out, for her son is not to inherit with the freewoman's son; so the brethren are children of the free, not of the bondwoman"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Galatians 5:1; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 5:3; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 5:12; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 5:19; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 5:21; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 5:23; Galatians 5:1–26","v":"Paul bade the Galatians stand fast (NU For freedom Christ has made us free; stand fast therefore, and, by the NKJV note) in the liberty with which Christ set us free, and not be held again under a yoke of bondage. If they became circumcised, Christ would profit them nothing, and every man who becomes circumcised is a debtor (obligated, by the NKJV note) to keep all the law; those of them who tried to be justified by law were estranged from Christ and fallen from grace. Through the Spirit we eagerly await the hope of righteousness by faith; in Christ Jesus, circumcision and uncircumcision alike count for nothing, but faith working through love does. They had run well, and Paul asked who had hindered them from obeying the truth; the persuasion that hindered them was not from Him who calls them, and a little leaven works through the whole lump. In the Lord he was confident they would hold no other view, but the one troubling them would bear his judgment, whoever he was. If Paul still preached circumcision, he asked, why was he still persecuted? For then the cross would no longer give offense. He wished that those who troubled them would even cut themselves off (mutilate themselves, by the NKJV note). The brethren were called to liberty, yet were not to use it as an opening for the flesh, but to serve one another through love, since one word fulfills the whole law, to love one's neighbor as oneself; and if they bit and devoured one another, they were to beware lest they be consumed by one another. Paul bade them walk in the Spirit, and they would not carry out the flesh's lust, since the flesh and the Spirit are set against each other, so that the brethren do not do what they wish; but if led by the Spirit, they are not under the law. The flesh's works are plain: idolatry and sorcery; adultery (NU omits adultery, by the NKJV note), fornication (sexual immorality, by the NKJV note), lewdness and uncleanness; envy, jealousies and hatred; contentions, dissensions, heresies and selfish ambitions; outbursts of wrath and murders (NU omits murders, by the NKJV note); drunkenness, revelries and the like; as he had told them before, he told them again that those who practice such works will not inherit God's kingdom. But the Spirit's fruit is love, joy and peace, kindness and goodness, longsuffering and faithfulness, gentleness (meekness, by the NKJV note) and self-control; no law stands against such things. Christ's own have crucified the flesh, its desires and its passions. If we live in the Spirit, we are also to walk in the Spirit, not becoming conceited, provoking or envying one another"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Galatians 6:1; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 6:14; Galatians 6:1–18","v":"Paul bade the brethren who are spiritual restore gently any man overtaken (caught, by the NKJV note) in a trespass, each watching himself lest he too be tempted; they were to bear one another's burdens, so fulfilling Christ's law. Whoever thinks himself something when he is nothing deceives himself; each was to examine his own work, and then his rejoicing would rest in himself alone, not in anyone else, for each will bear his own load. The one taught the word was to share in every good thing with his teacher. God is not mocked: a man will reap whatever he sows, corruption from the flesh if he sows to his flesh, everlasting life from the Spirit if he sows to the Spirit. We are not to grow tired of doing good, since we will reap in due season, provided we do not lose heart; so, as we have opportunity, we are to do good to all, above all to the household of faith. Paul wrote to the Galatians in large letters with his own hand. Those wanting a good showing in the flesh would compel them to be circumcised, only to escape persecution for Christ's cross; even the circumcised did not keep the law, yet wanted the Galatians circumcised so as to boast in the Galatians' flesh. Paul would boast only in our Lord Jesus Christ's cross, by whom (Or which, the cross, by the NKJV note) the world and he have been crucified, each to the other, since in Christ Jesus circumcision and uncircumcision alike count for nothing, but a new creation does. He wished peace and mercy on all who walk by this rule, and on the Israel of God. From then on no one was to trouble him, for he bore the marks of the Lord Jesus in his body; and he wished that our Lord Jesus Christ's grace be with the brethren's spirit"},{"p":"designation","r":"Galatians 1:1","v":"Paul, an apostle"},{"p":"feature","r":"Galatians 6:11","v":"Paul writes to the Galatians in large letters with his own hand"},{"p":"made by","r":"Galatians 1:1; 6:11","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Galatians 1:1–2","e":"EVT-03269"}],"nm":{"EVT-03269":"Paul writes to the churches of Galatia","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00665":{"k":"object","n":"The Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Ephesians","d":"The letter Paul wrote to the saints at Ephesus, faithful in Christ Jesus, of every spiritual blessing in Christ, of salvation by grace through faith, of Jew and Gentile made one body in Christ, of the mystery made known to him, of the walk worthy of their calling, and of God's armor (Ephesians 1:1–6:24)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 1:6; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 1:8; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 1:10; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 1:14; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 1:18; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 1:21; Ephesians 1:1–23","v":"Paul, by God's will an apostle of Jesus Christ, greeted the saints in Ephesus, faithful in Christ Jesus, wishing them grace and peace both from God our Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ. Paul blessed God, our Lord Jesus Christ's Father, for He has given us every spiritual blessing in Christ, in the heavenly places: before the world was founded He chose us in Him, to be holy and blameless before Him in love, and predestined us to be adopted as sons through Jesus Christ, as it pleased His will, to the praise of His glorious grace, through which He made us accepted (Lit. bestowed grace (favor) upon us, by the NKJV note) in the Beloved. In Him we are redeemed through His blood, our sins forgiven, by the riches of His grace, lavished on us in all wisdom and prudence (understanding, by the NKJV note); God has let us know the mystery of His will, by the good pleasure He purposed in Himself: in the dispensation when the times reach their fullness, to gather into one in Christ all things, both (NU, M omit both, by the NKJV note) in heaven and on earth. In Him we have also obtained an inheritance, predestined by the purpose of the One who works all things by the counsel of His will, so that we, the first to trust in Christ, might be for the praise of His glory. The saints at Ephesus too trusted in Him, when they heard the gospel of their salvation, the word of truth, and, having believed, were sealed, their seal the Holy Spirit of promise, who (NU which, by the NKJV note) is the guarantee (down payment, earnest, by the NKJV note) of our inheritance until the purchased possession is redeemed, for the praise of His glory. Having heard of their faith in the Lord Jesus and their love for all the saints, Paul never ceased giving thanks for them, remembering them in his prayers, asking that God, the Father of glory, would give them a spirit of wisdom and revelation in knowing Him, the eyes of their understanding (NU, M hearts, by the NKJV note) enlightened, to know the hope of His calling, the glorious riches of His inheritance in the saints, as well as how exceedingly great His power is toward us who believe, by the working of the mighty power He worked in Christ, raising Him from the dead and setting Him in the heavenly places at His right hand, far above all principality (rule, by the NKJV note) and might (power, by the NKJV note), dominion and power (authority, by the NKJV note), and every name named, in this age and in the age to come; God put all things under Christ's feet and made Him head over all things for the church, His body, the fullness of the One who fills all in all"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 2:2; Ephesians 2:1–22","v":"God made alive the saints at Ephesus, who had been dead in trespasses and sins, once walking in them by the course (Gr. aion, aeon, by the NKJV note) of this world and by the prince who holds the power of the air, that spirit now at work in the sons of disobedience; among them we all once lived in the lusts of the flesh, doing what the flesh and the mind desired, and by nature were children of wrath, like the rest. But God, rich in mercy, out of His great love for us made us alive with Christ even when we were dead in our trespasses (by grace the saints at Ephesus have been saved), raised us up with Him and seated us in Christ Jesus in the heavenly places, to show in the ages to come how exceedingly rich His grace is, in His kindness to us in Christ Jesus. By grace they have been saved through faith, and not of themselves, as God's gift, not of works, so that no one may boast; for we are God's workmanship, created in Christ Jesus to do good works, which God prepared beforehand for us to walk in. They were to remember that once, as Gentiles in the flesh, called Uncircumcision by those called the Circumcision (a circumcision made by hands in the flesh), they were without Christ, aliens from Israel's commonwealth and strangers to the covenants of promise, without God and without hope in the world; now, though, in Christ Jesus they who were once far away have been brought near by Christ's blood. For He Himself is our peace: He made Jew and Gentile one, broke down the dividing wall that separated them, abolishing in His flesh the enmity, the law of commandments in ordinances, to make of the two one new man in Himself, so making peace, and, in one body, to reconcile both to God through the cross, by which He put the enmity to death. He came preaching peace to the saints at Ephesus, once afar off, and to those near, since we both have access through Him to the Father by one Spirit. So they are strangers and foreigners no more, but fellow citizens with the saints, of God's household, built on the apostles and prophets as their foundation, with Jesus Christ Himself the chief corner stone; in Him the whole building, joined together, grows to be a temple holy in the Lord, and in Him they too are being built together into a place where God dwells in the Spirit"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 3:2; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 3:9; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 3:10; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 3:14; Ephesians 3:1–21","v":"Paul, the prisoner of Christ Jesus on behalf of the saints at Ephesus, whom he addressed as Gentiles, wrote of the dispensation (stewardship, by the NKJV note) of God's grace given him for them, if they had heard of it: by revelation God made known to him the mystery, as he had briefly written already, by which, in reading, they could understand his insight into the mystery of Christ, not made known to men in other ages as it has now been revealed to the holy apostles and prophets by the Spirit: that the Gentiles were to be fellow heirs, of one body, sharers in God's promise in Christ by the gospel. Of the gospel Paul became a minister, by the gift of God's grace given him by the working of His power. This grace was given to him, though he was less than the least of the saints, to preach Christ's unsearchable riches among the Gentiles and to make all see the fellowship (NU, M stewardship (dispensation), by the NKJV note) of the mystery, hidden from the beginning of the ages in God, the Creator of all things through Jesus Christ (NU omits through Jesus Christ, by the NKJV note), so that through the church the manifold (variegated or many-sided, by the NKJV note) wisdom of God might now be made known to the principalities (rulers, by the NKJV note) and powers in heavenly places, by the eternal purpose He fulfilled in Christ Jesus our Lord; in Him we have bold and confident access through faith in Him. Paul asked the saints at Ephesus not to lose heart at his tribulations for them, which are their glory. For this reason he bowed his knees to our Lord Jesus Christ's Father (NU omits of our Lord Jesus Christ, by the NKJV note), the whole family in heaven and on earth being named from Him, praying that by the riches of His glory He would strengthen them with might in the inner man through His Spirit, that Christ might dwell in their hearts through faith, and that they, rooted and grounded in love, might with all the saints grasp the length and width, the height and depth, and know Christ's love, which passes knowledge, and be filled with all God's fullness. Paul closed in praise, that glory be to Him who can do far more than all we ask or think, by the power at work in us, by Christ Jesus in the church, for all generations, forever"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:1; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:6; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:9; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:12; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:17; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:27; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:29; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:31; Ephesians 4:1–32","v":"Paul, the prisoner of the Lord (Lit. in, by the NKJV note), urged (exhort, encourage, by the NKJV note) the saints at Ephesus to walk in a way worthy of the calling to which they were called, in all lowliness and gentleness, longsuffering, bearing lovingly with one another, striving to keep the Spirit's unity in the bond of peace. Paul wrote of one Spirit and one body, as they were called in one hope; one faith and baptism, and one Lord; and one God and Father of all, over all, through all and in them (NU omits you; M us, by the NKJV note) all. To each of us grace was given by the measure of Christ's gift; and so it is said that He ascended on high, leading captivity captive, and gave men gifts. Paul asked what His ascending means, if not that He had also first (NU omits first, by the NKJV note) gone down into the earth's lower parts; the One who descended also ascended far above all heavens, to fill all things. It is He who gave some as evangelists, some as pastors and teachers, some as apostles, and some as prophets, to equip the saints to do the work of ministry, for the edification (building up, by the NKJV note) of Christ's body, until we all reach unity in the faith and in knowing the Son of God, and come to be a perfect man, attaining the measure of the stature of Christ's fullness; so that we should be children no longer, tossed to and fro, carried here and there by every wind of doctrine through men's trickery and their cunning craftiness in deceitful plotting, but rather, speaking the truth in love, might grow up in every way into Christ, who is the head. From Him the whole body, knit and joined together by what each joint supplies, by the effective working through which each part does its share, grows and builds itself up in love. Paul testified in the Lord that the saints at Ephesus were to walk no longer as the rest of the Gentiles do (NU omits the rest of, by the NKJV note); those Gentiles walk in their mind's futility, their understanding darkened, estranged from God's life through the ignorance in them and the blindness of their heart, and, past feeling, have given themselves up to lewdness, practicing every uncleanness greedily. But the saints at Ephesus had not learned Christ in that way, if indeed they had heard Him and were taught by Him, as truth is in Jesus: to put off the old man of their former conduct, which grows corrupt by deceitful lusts, to be made new in the spirit of their mind, and to put on the new man, created according to God in righteousness and holiness that are true. So, putting away lying, each was to speak the truth to his neighbor, since we are members of one another. They were to be angry without sinning; they were not to let the sun set on their wrath, nor give the devil place (an opportunity, by the NKJV note). The one who stole was to steal no more, but rather to labor, working with his hands at what is good, so as to have something to give the one in need. No corrupt word was to come out of their mouth, but only what is good for needed edification (building up, by the NKJV note), to give grace to those who hear. They were not to bring grief to the Holy Spirit of God; by Him they had been sealed for the day of redemption. All malice and bitterness, anger and wrath, clamor (loud quarreling, by the NKJV note) and evil speaking were to be put away from them; they were to be kind and tenderhearted to one another, forgiving each other as God in Christ forgave them"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:5; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:9; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:11; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:13; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:15; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:21; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:26; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:30; Ephesians 5:1–33","v":"The saints at Ephesus were to imitate God as dear children, walking in love as Christ loved us and gave Himself for us as an offering and sacrifice to God, a sweet-smelling aroma. Fornication, every kind of uncleanness and covetousness were not even to be named among them, as befits saints, nor filthiness, foolish talk or coarse jesting, which are unfitting; rather there was to be thanksgiving, for, as they knew (NU know this, by the NKJV note), no fornicator, no unclean person and no covetous man, the covetous man being an idolater, has an inheritance in Christ's and God's kingdom. No one was to deceive them with empty words, for these things bring God's wrath on the sons of disobedience; so the saints at Ephesus were not to be partakers with them. They were once darkness, but now light in the Lord; they were to walk as children of light, finding out what pleases the Lord, since the fruit of the Spirit (NU light, by the NKJV note) lies in all goodness, righteousness and truth. They were to have no fellowship with the fruitless works of darkness, but were rather to expose (reprove, by the NKJV note) them, since what such men do in secret is shameful even to mention; but all that is exposed (reproved, by the NKJV note) is made manifest by the light, since what makes manifest is light. So it is said that the sleeper was to awake and rise from the dead, and Christ would give him light. They were to see that they walked circumspectly (carefully, by the NKJV note), as wise men and not as fools, redeeming the time, since the days are evil; they were not to be unwise, but to understand what the Lord's will is. They were not to be drunk with wine, for in it is dissipation, but to be filled with the Spirit, addressing one another in hymns, psalms and spiritual songs, and singing and making melody to the Lord in their heart, always giving thanks to God the Father for all things in our Lord Jesus Christ's name, and submitting to one another out of the fear of God (NU Christ, by the NKJV note). Wives were to submit to their own husbands as to the Lord, since the husband is the wife's head as Christ is the church's head, He being the body's Savior; and as the church submits to Christ, wives were likewise to be subject in everything to their own husbands. Husbands were to love their wives as Christ loved the church, giving Himself for her to sanctify (set it apart, by the NKJV note) and cleanse her by the word, washing her with water, and present to Himself a glorious church with no spot, wrinkle or such thing, holy and without blemish. Husbands were to love their wives as their own bodies; a husband who loves his wife loves himself, since no one hates his own flesh, but feeds and cherishes it, as the Lord does the church; and we, the members of His body, are of His bones and His flesh (NU omits the rest of v. 30, by the NKJV note). Therefore a man will leave his father and mother and will be joined to his wife, and both will become one flesh: Paul called this a great mystery, but spoke of Christ and the church. Each husband was to love his wife as himself, and each wife to respect her husband"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 6:9; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 6:11; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 6:12; Ephesians 6:1–24","v":"Children were to obey their parents in the Lord, as is right, and honor father and mother, which Paul called the first commandment with promise, so that it would go well with them and they would have long life on the earth; fathers were not to provoke their children to wrath, but to raise them in the Lord's training and admonition. Bondservants were to obey their masters in the flesh in fear and trembling, with a sincere heart, as to Christ, not serving with eyeservice, to please men, but doing God's will from the heart as Christ's bondservants, serving with goodwill as to the Lord rather than to men, since the Lord will repay whatever good anyone does, slave or free; masters were, in turn, to do the same to their bondservants and give up threats, knowing that their own Master also is (NU He who is both their Master and yours is, by the NKJV note) in heaven, a Master who shows no partiality. Finally, the brethren were to be strong in the Lord, in His mighty power, and put on God's whole armor to stand against the devil's wiles (schemings, by the NKJV note), for our wrestling is not with flesh and blood, but with the rulers of this age's darkness (NU this darkness, by the NKJV note), with principalities and powers, and with the spiritual hosts of wickedness that are in heavenly places; so they were to take up God's whole armor, to hold their ground in the evil day and, when all is done, to stand, armed with salvation for a helmet, righteousness for a breastplate, truth girded about the waist, their feet shod with the preparation that is of the gospel of peace, the Spirit's sword, the word of God, and above all faith as a shield to quench every fiery dart of the wicked one. They were to pray always in the Spirit with all prayer and supplication, watchful for it and persevering in it, for all the saints and on Paul's behalf, that words might be given him to open his mouth boldly and make the gospel's mystery known, for which he was an ambassador in chains. Paul sent them Tychicus, a beloved brother and a faithful minister in the Lord, to make known to them how Paul was doing and all his affairs, and to comfort their hearts. Paul wished the brethren peace, and love joined to faith, from God the Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ, and grace on all who sincerely love our Lord Jesus Christ"},{"p":"designation","r":"Ephesians 1:1","v":"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ"},{"p":"made by","r":"Ephesians 1:1","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Ephesians 1:1–2","e":"EVT-03275"}],"nm":{"EVT-03275":"Paul writes to the saints at Ephesus","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00666":{"k":"object","n":"The Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Philippians","d":"The letter Paul and Timothy wrote to the saints in Christ Jesus at Philippi, together with the bishops and deacons, of Paul's chains and the furtherance of the gospel, of the mind of Christ, who humbled Himself and was exalted, of knowing Christ and pressing toward the goal, of rejoicing in the Lord, and of the gift the Philippians sent Paul (Philippians 1:1–4:23)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Philippians 1:1; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 1:13; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 1:16; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 1:22; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 1:28; Philippians 1:1–30","v":"Paul and Timothy, Jesus Christ's bondservants, greeted all the saints in Christ Jesus at Philippi, together with the bishops (Lit. overseers, by the NKJV note) and deacons, wishing them grace and peace both from God our Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ. Paul thanked his God whenever he remembered them, praying always for them all with joy for their fellowship in the gospel ever since the first day, confident that He who began a good work in them will bring it to completion until the day of Jesus Christ; it was right for him to think so, for he held them in his heart, since in his chains and in defending and confirming the gospel they all shared grace with him, and God was his witness how greatly he longed for them all with Jesus Christ's affection. Paul prayed that their love might abound still more in knowledge and all discernment, so that they might approve what is excellent and be sincere and without offense until the day of Christ, filled with the fruits of righteousness that come by Jesus Christ, to God's glory and praise. What had happened to Paul had turned out to further the gospel: it became plain to the whole palace guard (Or Praetorium, by the NKJV note) and everyone else that his chains are in Christ, while most brethren in the Lord, made confident by his chains, dared far more to speak the word fearlessly. Some preached Christ from envy and strife, some from goodwill; the former (NU reverses vv. 16 and 17, by the NKJV note) from selfish ambition, not sincerely, thinking to add affliction to his chains, the latter from love, knowing he is appointed to defend the gospel. What then? Only that Christ is preached in every way, in pretense or in truth, and in this Paul rejoiced and would rejoice. He knew this would turn out for his deliverance through the prayer of the saints at Philippi and the supply that is of the Spirit of Jesus Christ, as he eagerly expected and hoped that he would be ashamed in nothing, but that with all boldness Christ would now, as always, be magnified in his body, by life or by death. For him, to live is Christ, and dying is gain; if he lived on in the flesh, that would mean fruit from his labor, and which to choose he could not tell (do not know, by the NKJV note); he was hard-pressed between the two, desiring to depart and to be with Christ, which would be far better, yet remaining in the flesh was more needful for them. Confident of this, he knew he would remain and continue with them all for their progress and joy of faith, so that their rejoicing for him might abound in Jesus Christ by his coming to them again. Above all, their conduct was to be worthy of Christ's gospel, so that, whether he came and saw them or was absent, he might hear that they stand fast in one spirit, striving together with one mind for the faith of the gospel, in no way terrified by their adversaries, which is to the adversaries a proof of perdition, but to the saints at Philippi of salvation (NU of your salvation, by the NKJV note), and that from God, since to them it has been given, for Christ's sake, both to believe in Him and to suffer for Him, having the same conflict they had seen in Paul and now heard was in him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Philippians 2:1; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 2:6; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 2:7; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 2:14; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 2:15; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 2:19; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 2:30; Philippians 2:1–30","v":"If there was any affection and mercy, any fellowship of the Spirit, any comfort of love, any consolation (Or encouragement, by the NKJV note) in Christ, the saints at Philippi were to fulfill Paul's joy by being of one mind and one accord, like-minded and sharing the same love, doing nothing from selfish ambition or conceit, but each, in lowliness of mind, esteeming others better than himself and looking out for others' interests as well as his own. They were to have the mind that was in Christ Jesus. Though He was in the form of God, He did not count it robbery to be equal (Or something to be held onto to be equal, by the NKJV note) to God, but rather made Himself of no reputation (emptied Himself of His privileges, by the NKJV note), taking a bondservant's form and coming in men's likeness; and, found in appearance to be a man, He humbled Himself, obedient even to death, death on the cross. Therefore God has exalted Him highly and given Him the name above every name, so that at Jesus' name every knee should bow, in heaven, on earth and beneath it, and every tongue should confess Jesus Christ as Lord, to God the Father's glory. As they had always obeyed, not only in Paul's presence but much more now in his absence, they were to work out their own salvation with fear and trembling, since God is the one at work in them, both to will and to act, for His good pleasure. They were to do everything without complaining (grumbling, by the NKJV note) and disputing (arguing, by the NKJV note), so as to become blameless and harmless (innocent, by the NKJV note), God's children without fault amid a crooked and perverse generation, in which they shine as lights in the world as they hold fast to the word of life, so that Paul might rejoice on the day of Christ that he had not run or labored in vain. If he was being poured out like a drink offering upon the sacrifice and service of their faith, he was glad and rejoiced with them all, and so they too were to be glad and rejoice with him. Paul trusted in the Lord Jesus that he would soon send Timothy to them, to be encouraged himself on learning their state (condition, by the NKJV note), for he had no one else like-minded who would sincerely care for their state, since all seek their own, not what is Christ Jesus'; they knew Timothy's proven character, how he served with Paul in the gospel like a son with his father. Paul hoped to send him without delay, once he saw how his own affairs would go, and trusted in the Lord that he himself would also come shortly. He thought it needful to send back to them Epaphroditus, his fellow soldier, fellow worker and brother, their messenger, who ministered to his need, since Epaphroditus longed for them all and was distressed that they had heard he was sick, as he was, almost to death; yet God had mercy on him, and on Paul too, lest Paul have sorrow upon sorrow. So Paul sent him the more eagerly, that they might rejoice to see him again and Paul be less sorrowful. They were to welcome him gladly in the Lord, and to hold such men in esteem, since he came close to death for the work of Christ, not regarding (risking, by the NKJV note) his life, to make up what was lacking in their service to Paul"},{"p":"designation","r":"Philippians 1:1","v":"Paul and Timothy, bondservants of Jesus Christ"},{"p":"made by","r":"Philippians 1:1","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Philippians 1:1–2","e":"EVT-03277"}],"nm":{"EVT-03277":"Paul writes to the saints at Philippi","PER-03213":"Saul"}}});
